window.BanesDocs={"atlas": [], "chronicle": [{"date": "Spring, 2965", "html": "<h2>The mushroom-gatherer</h2>\n<p>Marigold Cotton had crossed the Brandywine for mushrooms. Her children were grown and settled about the\nShire, her husband was dead, and she had taken to going further afield than a respectable hobbit\nought. Even so, the river had always been a boundary to her. She had never crossed it before.</p>\n<p>The Old Forest smelled different from anything on the Shire side. A garden smells one way and a\ncommon another, and the woods of the Shire different again; but this was older than any\nof them, a thick musky smell of loam and leaf-rot and wet bark. She found a few mushrooms quickly,\nand then more, and many were kinds she had never seen. She kept a sense of the way back. She also\nwent in deeper than she had meant to.</p>\n<p>Then she heard singing. It was a man's voice, a deep baritone, loud without bellowing, and it filled\nthe trees. It came from a brook nearby, and the song seemed to be about fish.</p>\n<p>Marigold went towards it quietly, as only a hobbit can. The soil was soft under her bare feet. At the brook stood a big man — big even for one of the Big Folk, some six feet and more, and\nslim — skipping stones down the water. He was very good at it. The stones went in long low arcs and\nbounced four and five times. She watched until he threw a particularly fine one, and then she\ncould not help herself.</p>\n<p>He turned at once and swept her a grand bow. \"I didn't know I had an audience! Come and try your\nhand.\"</p>\n<p>She ran down and tried her hand. She kept her apron gathered up, with the morels hidden in it, but\nhe sniffed the air hugely and smiled as if he knew exactly what was there.</p>\n<p>\"The south side of the oaks is where the best mushrooms are,\" he said. \"Goldberry is making some\nfor supper tonight. Will you join us? The name's Tom. What's yours?\"</p>\n<p>\"Marigold.\"</p>\n<p>\"Marigold! Wonderful.\"</p>\n<p>He led her through the wood, singing again. This song was in a language she did not know. It was\nbeautiful, and a little sad in a way she could not place. They came to a small cottage with\nanimals wandering about it, and Tom burst in at the door.</p>\n<p>\"Goldberry! I've brought a guest for supper!\"</p>\n<p>A fair-haired woman stood over a great pot on the fire. She welcomed Marigold as though she had\nbeen expected, and there were bowls already poured.</p>\n<h2>The Elf and the bear</h2>\n<p>Makheneb was in the Old Forest too, for his own reasons.</p>\n<p>He was an Elf of Mirkwood, a Warden of that wood, young for his people at something under three\nhundred years. He had fought in the Battle of Five Armies. For the last while his luck had been\nbad; his family had fallen on hard times long ago, and lately he had lost nearly everything he\ncarried, down to the shirt on his back. He had come west on foot and lived off the land.</p>\n<p>What drew him was a feeling. Once or twice in his long life he had been near something truly evil\n— near wraiths, or things like them — and each time there had been the same\nprickle down the spine, the same awareness of a presence close by. What he felt now was not that.\nIt lacked that depth of malice. But it was alien, and it had a pull to it, and for some time it had\ndrawn him westward without telling him why.</p>\n<p>He cast about the forest for its source and found nothing he could follow. But he knew where he\nwas. He had heard his elders speak of this country as Tom's land, and they spoke of Tom as older\nthan themselves, and not an Elf, and that had always puzzled him. They did not speak of Tom with\nawe. They mentioned him in passing, the way one mentions the weather: <em>and then the thing went off\ninto Tom's land</em>, and that was the end of the story.</p>\n<p>As the light began to fail he heard a woman singing, far off, and a man's voice joined hers. He\nstood listening. Behind him something snuffled.</p>\n<p>It was a bear, something like a black bear, and much too close. Makheneb held his bow ready and\nmade no threatening move, and began to back away slowly towards the singing. The bear came on. He\ndid not trust his feet in the gloom, and the first blow nearly caught him. He began to run between\nthe trees, weaving from trunk to trunk to keep them between himself and the bear, and blows that\nwould have taken his face off shuddered into bark instead.</p>\n<p>Then the song he had been following broke off. A little later a new one began, closer: a low\nvoice, singing a slow and soothing song in a language Makheneb did not know. The bear pulled up.\nIt sat back on its haunches with its forepaws in front of it and stared at him sullenly.</p>\n<p>The song had a refrain that came round and round. Makheneb caught it and began to sing it too. The\nbear did not seem to mind.</p>\n<p>Out of the dusk came a tall man in bright yellow boots. He laughed, scratched the bear behind the\nears, and rubbed its head. \"Be off with you,\" he said, and the bear stumbled away into the trees.\nThen he looked Makheneb up and down.</p>\n<p>\"An Elf! Welcome, welcome to Tom's land! I'm Tom.\"</p>\n<p>\"I have heard much and little of you,\" said Makheneb.</p>\n<p>\"Well then, come and have supper.\"</p>\n<p>\"I have not had a good meal in quite some time.\"</p>\n<h2>The runaway pony</h2>\n<p>Lily Goatleaf had left Bree in a hurry. She waited tables at the Prancing Pony, and over the years\nshe had listened closely to the Rangers when they trusted someone enough to talk. She had\nlearned from them that Bree-land was not the safe place Bree-folk took it for. It was a small\nisland with enemies all round it that no one else could see. She had never told anyone. They would\nnot have believed her.</p>\n<p>Lately the folk coming through Bree had grown stranger and more unsavoury, and they were gathering\nthere. Then a Ranger called Findemir passed through the inn. She overheard him, speaking low, and\nlearned that he was setting out west; and he was gone again before she could speak with him. She\nfelt a dire portent in it, and decided she could not wait any longer. She borrowed a half-starved pony called\nBuddy on the vague promise that she needed to leave town for a while, and went after the Ranger to\nbring back help.</p>\n<p>Perry Oakroot saw her go and came with her: not to bring her back, but to help. They were distant cousins, of a kind that depends on which way you\ncount. Perry was a Man of Bree and one of the gate-wardens of Bree-land, and he had spent many cold\nlonely nights on watch at the gates with only the stars and the owls for company. He knew a good\nmany people in town, and a good many travellers by sight. He had come to know some of them as good\nfolk, not at all like Bill Ferny and his cronies. He had not been free from fear or care for some\ntime.</p>\n<p>They were a day out of Bree on the East Road, walking and leading Buddy with their gear on his\nback. Lily hoped Findemir had at least stopped in Hobbiton, and that someone at an inn there\ncould tell them where he had gone. Dusk was coming on and they were talking about where to camp\nwhen something frightened Buddy. He bolted off the road and down into the forest, and everything\nthey owned went with him.</p>\n<p>Lily sprinted after him at once. Perry stood in the road for a moment. He was thinking of Butterbur,\nand what Butterbur would say. Then he ran after her. The ground fell away steeply from the road. Lily was quick\nand sure-footed, and she overtook the pony a little way into the trees and began to calm him.\nPerry came down the slope with more speed than he had meant to. At the first stand of trees a root\ncaught his foot and he fell flat on his face.</p>\n<p>He got up at once, took his bearings, and went on at a measured jog, watching the ground for roots\nthe whole way.</p>\n<p>While Lily soothed Buddy, another horse came walking towards them out of the trees. It had no\ntether and it was plainly well cared for, and it came up to them without any fear at all. It\nseemed to be hoping for a treat. Lily edged towards it carefully, as one approaches a new friend,\nand it let her.</p>\n<p>Perry stumbled out of the wood covered in twigs, his face smeared with dirt. \"What happened, Lily?\"</p>\n<p>Lily was already rummaging in the pack for an apple. She gave Buddy one and offered another to the\nstrange horse, but it turned and cantered off.</p>\n<p>\"We should be careful,\" said Perry. \"There are strange folk in these parts.\"</p>\n<p>\"Strange folk can't have horses?\"</p>\n<p>\"We should follow it. It may even be the Ranger's. But carefully.\"</p>\n<p>They followed. They passed through a garden, though it was not like any garden in Bree. Nothing was\nfenced or set in rows. The vegetables tumbled over one another, and the beans zigzagged all the way\nup to a cottage door. As they came round the corner of the house they saw a big man in bright\nyellow boots and a blue jacket stroking the horse's mane and feeding it something.</p>\n<p>Lily called out to him. The man turned to the horse. \"Strawberry, you've brought new friends! And\nwho's this?\" He held out his hand, and there was an apple in it that had not been there before,\nand Buddy took it.</p>\n<p>\"This is Buddy,\" said Lily. \"I've only had him a day, and I would do anything for him.\"</p>\n<p>\"Anything for Buddy,\" said Perry. \"What is your name, sir?\"</p>\n<p>\"Tom. Tom, and yours?\"</p>\n<p>\"Perry.\"</p>\n<p>\"Lily.\"</p>\n<p>\"Perry and Lily! Wonderful. Well, you must stay for supper. We have a mushroom stew, and the peaches\nhave come in, and there are wild strawberries not far off — hence the horse. I go and gather them\nnow and then.\" He promised that Buddy would have plenty to eat. Lily told him they had been trying\nto fatten Buddy up the whole way. It had been their pet project.</p>\n<h2>Supper</h2>\n<p>Tom brought them in and introduced his wife, Goldberry, and she sat them down beside Marigold.\nPerry had seen hobbits at the Prancing Pony, which kept rooms for them, and dealt with a few at the\ngate, but he could count on one hand the hobbits he had ever spoken to. Lily introduced herself.\nMarigold was glad to see young people, and before long she had brought out her mushrooms and was\nteaching Lily their names. Lily listened. She liked learning things. But the Ranger was never far\nfrom her mind.</p>\n<p>Tom and Goldberry were perfect hosts, and nothing seemed to trouble them. Their land was bountiful,\nbut they did not seem to work it hard or to fret over it. Animals wandered in and out of the house\nas they pleased. Nothing was penned and nothing was tied. Strawberry ambled where she liked. There\nwas a pumpkin patch in one corner that made no sense where it was.</p>\n<p>After supper Tom asked, \"You don't mind if we sing a little, do you?\"</p>\n<p>Lily said she would love it. They built up the fire and served tea and small cakes, and Tom and\nGoldberry took turns singing. Some of the songs were in languages the guests knew and some were\nnot. The words they understood were mostly tender things about the land: a walk on a warm spring\nday in the rain, and how the rain felt; the moment when the sun catches a stream just so. But now\nand then a verse spoke of the land in ways that did not match anything they knew of it, or named\npeople and places none of them had ever heard of.</p>\n<p>In the middle of a duet Tom stopped.</p>\n<p>\"Sorry, my love. The bears are getting a little anxious. I'll be right back.\" And he walked out of\nthe door.</p>\n<p>Perry asked whether there was danger, and whether Tom might want help.</p>\n<p>\"We don't usually have trouble with the bears,\" said Goldberry. \"But sometimes travellers meet\nthem, and then there can be trouble. Tom likes to make sure nobody gets too ornery. I wouldn't fancy\nyour chances if you came to fisticuffs with a bear.\" Then she said, \"I expect we'll have another\nguest. I'll warm up some supper. Would one of you set a place, and fetch some wine?\"</p>\n<p>They set a place. Before long Tom came back with Makheneb behind him.</p>\n<p>\"I knew to expect somebody,\" Tom said. \"I must admit I wasn't quite expecting you lot. We'll talk\nonce you've finished. Eat! Sit! Enjoy yourselves!\"</p>\n<p>Makheneb ate like one who had not eaten for some time. Now and then he glanced round the room. Tom\nsettled by the fire with a pipe whose bowl was nearly the size of a small dish, and offered a pipe\nto anyone who wanted one. His mood had grown quieter. Goldberry got out her knitting, put it away\nagain, and went about tidying.</p>\n<p>\"There are not many of your people outside the Shire,\" Makheneb said to Marigold.</p>\n<p>\"You'd be surprised how many hobbits love to see the world,\" said Marigold. \"Nothing surprises me\nout here.\" She had never seen an Elf before. On the inside she was wondering what to do, and whether\nshe had already offended him.</p>\n<p>He had not seen many hobbits either. There were few in Mirkwood; one, in living memory. But he had\nbeen at the Battle of Five Armies, and he knew the tale of the hobbit who had smuggled the Dwarves\nout of the Elvenking's halls.</p>\n<p>\"What brings two Men of Bree out here?\" he asked.</p>\n<p>\"Is it so plain that we're from Bree?\"</p>\n<p>\"I can smell it.\"</p>\n<p>\"Chasing a pony,\" said Perry. \"And what brings an Elf of Mirkwood this far west?\"</p>\n<p>\"A feeling,\" said Makheneb. \"Not good, and not bad. Strange.\"</p>\n<p>\"The Shadow grows in the East,\" said Marigold.</p>\n<p>\"And he's come west,\" said Perry.</p>\n<p>\"Perhaps the Shadow has,\" said Makheneb.</p>\n<p>Perry said they had felt something too. Unsavoury folk were gathering in Bree, and Bree needed\nhelp. Lily turned to Tom and Goldberry and described the Ranger they were chasing. She said he was\ntall, by which she meant anyone taller than a Bree-lander.</p>\n<p>Goldberry knew him at once. \"Oh — Findemir! He came through only the other day.\" She described him\nbetter than Lily had: a bushy, curly auburn beard, no moustache, and hair that curled too, though\nnot tightly.</p>\n<p>\"That's why you're here,\" said Tom. \"That makes perfect sense. That is why you're here.\"</p>\n<p>Then he said, \"You aren't wrong to feel there are ill winds about. It seems things have been loosed\nthat need to be put back to sleep. Findemir was going north, beyond Fornost, to Barad Tarminalë. He is looking for\nhelp. The Rangers are dealing with strange creatures coming through the woods, and he believes some\nold power has loosed them. They have started coming into the Old Forest. I've had to kick one or two\nof them in the hind parts.\"</p>\n<p>\"What do they look like?\"</p>\n<p>\"The one I met had the form of a wolf. It was a good deal bigger than most wolves, and it was\nalone, which is odd in a wolf. Its fur was white and greyish. And though we are having a lovely\nspring here, it carried the hoar-frost of winter with it.\"</p>\n<p>\"So the one we're looking for is looking for help with strange creatures,\" said Marigold. \"And we\nare that help?\"</p>\n<p>\"Within the Old Forest my power is complete,\" said Tom. \"Beyond it, I will give whatever aid I can.\nYou have my blessing and my support in all you do, and I would dearly like you to come back and tell\nus what Findemir has found. You have a hobbit in Marigold, an Elf in Makheneb, and two good Men of\nBree. I believe that with you we will get to the bottom of this, and set the wild things right\nagain.\"</p>\n<p>Makheneb had another realisation, once the bear had worn off and his belly was full. This house was\nthe heart of it. The strangeness he had followed west came from here. He had\ntravelled west to find it, and now that he had, he was not glad. He had already been robbed down to\nhis shirt, and the journey had hardly begun. At least, he thought, it had not all been hunger.</p>\n<p>So the four of them became a company, with Tom Bombadil and Goldberry as their patrons, and Tom's\nhouse as a refuge to return to.</p>\n<h2>Freeze-berries</h2>\n<p>The guests slept on bedrolls and cots that night. Marigold and Lily were up before dawn, both of\nthem early risers, and they woke to the smell of something like strawberry muffins. Tom was in the\nkitchen with flour to the elbows and flour on every surface, pulling a tray out of the oven.</p>\n<p>\"Eat them while they're hot!\"</p>\n<p>There were strawberries in them, and another berry none of them knew, with a tartness like\nrhubarb. \"Freeze-berries,\" Tom said. \"They only grow in the Old Forest. They come up just after the\nlast snow is gone, and they're only about for a few weeks.\"</p>\n<p>Goldberry came in an hour later from seeing to the animals, and by then Perry and Makheneb were up.</p>\n<p>Over breakfast somebody asked Lily, quietly, \"Is he a Man?\"</p>\n<p>\"I don't think so,\" said Lily. \"But if you asked me anything at all this morning, I'd say I don't\nthink so.\"</p>\n<p>They turned to Makheneb, who as an Elf was presumed to know. \"He is not of my kind. Even the\neldest of my kin know little of him. I half thought he was a tale. But here he is. He seems a good\none to have owe us a favour.\"</p>\n<p>\"The man, the myth, the legend,\" said Tom, coming in at the door. He had plainly heard every word.</p>\n<p>They spread Perry's maps on the table, weighing the corners down with muffins. The maps had come\ndown to him from his grandfather Herbert. Perry asked Tom which way he would go: east along the Road\nand then north to Fornost, which might be quicker, or straight north across country if speed\nmattered. Tom said roads were kinder to ponies. Then he ran a jammy finger over the map and stopped. \"Oh, that won't do. That\nhasn't been there for a couple of generations.\" He rummaged in his pockets for a stub of pencil and\nbegan to mark the map in three places. He did not say what the marks meant.</p>\n<p>There was a jam smudge on them now as well.</p>\n<p>Goldberry led Strawberry over to Makheneb. \"Your companions all have mounts. Your road will go more\nsmoothly if you have one too. We only ask that you bring Strawberry back to us within a year, when\nyou're done. Care for her as I would my own.\" Lily had Buddy. Perry had brought his old horse, Leaper, who\nhad earned the name long ago and no longer deserved it. Marigold had a pony too, Margarita. All of\nthem were a little jealous of Makheneb.</p>\n<p>Goldberry gave them a basket of the mushrooms left over from supper, some of them kinds even\nMarigold had not seen, and a few jars of preserves. They set out for Barad Tarminalë, ten days'\njourney away, in the spring of the year 2965.</p>\n<h2>The road north</h2>\n<p>They kept to the road. Not far along it, riding in the sun on a fine spring day, they saw the bear.</p>\n<p>It had seen them too, and it was ambling towards the road on a line that would meet them. Makheneb\nbegan to sing the song Tom had sung to it. He got one verse out before he told the others to join\nin, and they did, though none of them knew what the words meant:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Bârun, Bârun, zîrân lô,</em><br />\n<em>Uzûn bâri nardim tô.</em><br />\n<em>Katha lô îdô kalab,</em><br />\n<em>Nardê batîr, nardê khâlab.</em><br />\n<em>Zimra-nîdô, azra-lad,</em><br />\n<em>Aphadad, lô nardad!</em><br />\n<em>Nimmê, Bârun, târik bal,</em><br />\n<em>Anadê thâni, nardê zal.</em> The bear came up to the road and fell in\nbeside Strawberry, and walked along with them for two hours. Then it seemed to grow bored and\nwandered off another way.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>When the song died down and they had wet their throats, the questions came. \"Are you friends?\"</p>\n<p>\"It is an Elf thing,\" said Makheneb.</p>\n<p>\"Now you know what to do if you meet a bear,\" said Makheneb. \"I would advise exactly that.\"</p>\n<p>One of them tried the song alone a little later. It did not go well.</p>\n<p>\"Maybe it only works when we sing it together,\" said another. \"I think we could make a drinking\nsong of it.\"</p>\n<p>They passed west of Bree at the crossroads and went north up the old road without going into the\ntown. Perry was relieved. He had not asked for much leave from the gate, and he did not care to explain\nthe whole adventure to everyone in town. Thanks to Tom and Goldberry they had all the\nfood they needed.</p>\n<p>Three days or so out, the road began to change. The roads east and west and south of Bree were kept\nup well enough, but the North had not been a place of trade for a long time, and on this road they\nmet no one at all. Lily scouted ahead, riding forward and falling back to rejoin the others. On one\nof her rides she came to a place where the road had been swept away. For a few hundred feet it was\nsimply gone: shoved up, broken, and pushed under the earth.</p>\n<p>Her first feeling was awe at whatever had had the strength to do that. Her second was dread,\nbecause it did not look like the work of wind or flood. It looked as if someone had done it. They\npicked their way round it with care, and it wearied them.</p>\n<p>After that the going was better. They found a path through the foothills that no one seemed to\nuse any longer. It had been well trodden once, and no trees had grown up in it. They followed it and\nmade surprisingly good time.</p>\n<h2>The woman in the foothills</h2>\n<p>In the foothills Makheneb went off from the path, drawn by some smell or some sense of wrongness,\nand found the dead.</p>\n<p>There were four of them, mostly bone, and long dead. Three were Orcs or goblins of some kind. The\nfourth had been an explorer, well equipped for the Wild and nothing like a travelling merchant. Her\nbody was much of a size with his own, and her helm and shield were still sound. He had gone\nwithout both since his luck turned, and he took them.</p>\n<p>Makheneb did not want to leave them lying as they were. There were trees enough there, though it\nwas not quite a forest. He gathered what personal things he could find, and then he laid the dead\nto rest in the way of his people. The earth and the growing things closed about them faster than\nany earth should, and the green grew over them. He said a few halting words for them. He\nwished them rest and asked that their fathers might look on them. He felt sure afterwards that no\nwight or wraith would rise from that place.</p>\n<p>While he worked, he saw clearly how she had died. She had been caught off her guard by three or\nfour Orc-warriors. She had killed two of them and maimed a third so badly that he could not move,\nand the others had left him there. Then they had killed her.</p>\n<p>Beyond the foothills Perry's maps ran out. None of them had been this far north, and none of them\nknew the country. Lily was scouting again, and she led them wrong for half a day. The broken road\nstill troubled her. She had been swept up in the adventure — in Tom, and Strawberry, and the new\nfriends — and the broken road had brought her back to why she had set out. It disheartened her, too:\nshe had only just left Bree, and already she was seeing more of the thing she had left to find help\nagainst.</p>\n<p>A day and a half later they saw the shape of hills that Tom had described. Coming in from the west\nby the wrong road turned out to be lucky. The fort was a ring of stone, nearly cut into the hills,\nand it lay mostly on their northern and western faces. From the south, the way they should have\ncome, they might never have seen it.</p>\n<h2>Barad Tarminalë</h2>\n<p>Lily knew more than the others about the Dúnedain. The Rangers had often talked more freely with her\nthan with most folk in Bree, because she was gently curious and polite, never prying, and she had\nthe innkeeper's habit of hospitality. They had told her of their own customs of hospitality. They\nkept way-stations across the wild lands. There was nearly always a steward at such a place,\ntaking turns with others, to keep a fire lit and the house open. You could turn up unannounced,\neven after a month in the wild, and find the plainest of comforts, and help if you were badly\nhurt. She guessed this was such a place. She could not be sure the welcome would extend to people\nwho were not Rangers, but she thought the odds were good.</p>\n<p>\"We should go up openly,\" she said. \"As what we are: tired folk looking for someone. Nothing to\nmake them wary. They'll give us the plain welcome, but they are Rangers, and they're suspicious by\nnature.\"</p>\n<p>They agreed to go up to the gate together and speak from a little way off, so no one inside would\nthink they were rushing it. There was an hour and a half of daylight left. The sun sat just above\nthe hills.</p>\n<p>The gates were great stakes of timber that opened inwards. There was no one on them. Lily went a\nlittle ahead of the others. \"Hello?\" No answer came. She called louder. \"I'm looking for Findemir!\"</p>\n<p>\"One minute!\" a woman's voice called from within.</p>\n<p>The gates creaked. A woman had hold of a big iron ring on one side and was walking it slowly\nbackward. It was a heavy gate, but not a hard one once it was moving. \"Come in, come in,\" she said.\nThen she saw the rest of them and looked startled. \"Well! What a party. Come in and warm\nyourselves.\"</p>\n<p>\"What's your name?\" asked Perry.</p>\n<p>\"I'm Moriel.\"</p>\n<p>\"Moriel. I'm Perry, and I'm pleased to make your acquaintance.\"</p>\n<p>She was a sturdy woman, dressed as a Ranger, in good boots, with one of the heavy cloaks they\nwear against the rain. It was a little much for the season, though the nights were still cold. She\nlooked as if she meant to be on the road before long.</p>\n<p>\"You've only just missed Findemir,\" she said. \"But I can help you a little. Come and eat something,\nand let me get you settled.\" She shut the gate behind them and showed them round.</p>\n<p>Inside, the place was all put away. There were cabins with bare beds, and cupboards that were\nmostly empty. Moriel kept a small store in one of them, and she began taking out vegetables for a\nstew. She had eaten already, she said, but they could cook something together. She talked freely,\nand she was curious about everything: how they knew Findemir, and what had brought them all the way\nup here.</p>\n<p>They told her plainly that they were looking for Findemir. Bree needed help, he had seemed a capable\nman when he came through, and he had said he might need help himself in the north. They did not\nmention Tom.</p>\n<p>She was the only one there. She was waiting for someone and would go west in a day or two, when they\narrived. She had kept the winter at this post for some months, and only three travellers had come\nall season. It was her first winter here. At the places she had wintered before there had been\nthirty or forty people, all hunkered down for the cold, and it had been more of a festival. Here\nthere had been a month and a half with no one at all. She had invented a good many games in that\ntime and got very good at beating herself, but she could not promise she would be much of an\nopponent for anyone else. Lily offered to teach her a card game she knew, for two or three or four\nplayers, and Moriel was glad to learn it.</p>\n<p>Findemir had been there only a night, more or less. There was a task of caretaking that had to be\ndone every year, Moriel said, and this year it was his turn. It lay a day and a half's journey\nnorth-east. She had never gone herself, but she knew others had, and she told them what she knew.</p>\n<p>Perry and Lily had heard the word <em>Númenórean</em>. Now and then a Ranger would turn up in Bree with some\nold thing: a piece of pottery, a tool, a dagger. The designs were strange, and the things were\nsometimes said to have strange properties, and the Rangers always said the same thing about them:\nthat no one knew any longer how such things were made. They called them Númenórean. Lily and Perry\nhad never been sure what that meant — only that there had once been a great people in the North, a\nvery long time ago.</p>\n<p>Findemir's errand was to a place like that. There was a ruin of some kind, and it had to be tended,\nlike grounds that must be kept. That was where he had gone.</p>", "played": "2025-05-06", "slug": "01-from-bree-to-toms-house", "title": "From Bree, to Tom’s House, to Barad Tarminalë"}, {"date": "Spring, 2965", "html": "<h2>Már</h2>\n<p>Már had been sent north without being told why.</p>\n<p>She was a Dwarf of Durin's folk, a hundred and seventeen years old, and among her clan she was the\none they sent for when something dead would not stay dead. In the mountains there are always things\nthat creep into the places where people live, and she had a knack for finding them and putting an\nend to them. That was her work, and she was known for it. So when a Ranger of the North came to her\nclan on some errand of his own, it was Már they told to go with him. Nobody explained what the\nerrand was. The Ranger, Findemir, plainly knew where he was going and what he meant to do there, and\nhe talked to her as though she knew it too. She did not. She went anyway.</p>\n<p>Findemir would not ride. He insisted on going on foot, and never said why, and so they left their\nmounts at the Rangers' post and walked north together into the hills.</p>\n<p>The land there was low and rolling, with no forest to speak of, only stands of trees here and there.\nIt had been settled once and had long since gone wild. Further south the spring was in full flood,\nwith weeds and flowers bursting into bloom along every road; but these hills seemed not to have\nheard of it. The buds were late. Snow still lay in the lee of the slopes and in the valleys where\nthe shade kept it.</p>\n<p>The wolves came on them a few hours out. There were four, and they were not like any wolves Már had\nseen. They were dire wolves, white-furred and huge, and they were cold. The air about them frosted\nas they moved, though it was spring and the day was mild. Each one was like a walking blizzard, and\nthe biggest was bigger than the rest by a good deal.</p>\n<p>Findemir and Már set their backs to one another and stood on their guard. Two wolves went for each\nof them. Már watched for the openings and made the wolves' strikes go wide, and she beat them off,\nbut the fight went against them. It is hard to judge the reach of a wolf when there are two of them\nand they take turns. The great wolf worked its way in on Findemir. Something drew his eye for an\ninstant, and in that instant it was at his throat.</p>\n<p>He went down. Már saw the blood and saw the wolves close in on him. She could have run. Instead she\nwent in over his body to drive them off him, and one of them snapped at her as she came and another\ncaught her by the leg.</p>\n<h2>Four out of the south</h2>\n<p>The Company had left Barad Tarminalë that morning on Moriel's directions, following Findemir. They\nheard the snarling from over a rise, and came over the top of it to find a Dwarf alone among four\nwolves with a man down at her feet.</p>\n<p>Makheneb had an arrow on the string before the others had taken it in. He picked the wolf that had\njust bitten the Dwarf, the next most dangerous after the great one, and loosed. It was a long shot,\nand the arrow from his great two-handed bow went in with a thud they heard from the rise. The wolf\nsquealed. The arrow stood sideways out of its ribs.</p>\n<p>Perry had a bow, though he had not drawn one in earnest in a long while; his arrow went well wide.\nMarigold had not shot anything in twenty years, and hers fell almost at her feet. Then they were\ndown the slope and in among the wolves.</p>\n<p>Perry came in hard and swung his long-hafted axe at one of them. He did not cleave it. He clipped it,\nand the weight of the blade knocked it sideways and left it winded and staggering.</p>\n<p>Lily had grown up on a farm, and all her training with a spear had been mock fights with her cousins\nin the yard. She told herself this was the same thing. It was the biggest and most savage animal she\nhad ever seen, and she was in a panic, and she put her spear through it with both hands and killed it\noutright. When she pulled the spear free the head of it was white with frost. It was so cold that if\nshe had touched her tongue to it, it would have stuck.</p>\n<p>Marigold went for the great wolf with her axe. Her nerve failed her at the last. At home she had had\na dog called Bobo, one of those big Shire dogs that stand as tall as a hobbit and are as long as\nthree or four of them, and he had been a great soft cuddly creature. This thing had a man's blood\nall over its face, but it was shaped like Bobo. She checked for a fraction of a second too long, and\nthe wolf sprang over her and round behind her. She turned with it and caught it with her axe as it\ncame down.</p>\n<p>The wolf Perry had clipped leapt on him. Its claws sank in under his arm, between the leather of his\ncorslet and his body, and there they jammed. It thrashed to wrench its paw free and savaged him while\nit did. Perry was wounded, and worse than that, he was weary. He had loaded himself with far too much\ngear, and he had not known it until now, when the weight of it dragged at every movement.</p>\n<p>Makheneb saw Perry in danger. He spun round and loosed. The arrow went clean through the wolf's head\nand out the other side. Perry went on shaking it for a moment before he understood that what he was\nshaking was dead weight, and then it was only a matter of untangling himself.</p>\n<p>Marigold called out to all of them that these were only dogs; they need not worry. Lily went in\nscreaming beside her. \"Think of it as a haystack!\" She was pretending she was back on the farm.\nWhatever they had told themselves, it put heart into the others. Már, who was in a fury by now,\ncame in with her axe raised high above her head. The blow was slow and plain to see, and the wolf\nshe aimed it at did not move in time. It was the one Makheneb had shot first. The axe came down and\nit lay still.</p>\n<p>Perry went to Findemir and tried to do something for him. He knew nothing about wounds. While he knelt\nthere, the great wolf came for him and clamped its jaws on his arm.</p>\n<p>It had him fast and could not turn to meet anyone else. Makheneb took the shot. To hit a wolf in the\nhead is hard enough; to hit it in the head without harming the arm in its mouth is harder. Perry felt\nthe jaws slacken, and then begin to tighten again, and he tore his arm out; and the jaws snapped shut\non nothing and stayed shut. The arrow had gone into its head.</p>\n<h2>After the wolves</h2>\n<p>The first thing was Findemir. Lily had a healing balm in her pack. She knew nothing about healing\neither, but Makheneb did, and with the balm and his help she cleaned and bound the wounds. They were\nbad. But Findemir was breathing, and he would not die. His eyes flickered. He could not sit up or\nspeak, and his breath came shallow.</p>\n<p>Perry's wound had stopped bleeding. He had packed it with mud. He was not sure whether that was good\nor bad.</p>\n<p>It was not yet noon. Moriel had said the place Findemir was bound for lay a day and a half from\nBarad Tarminalë, and they were only a few hours out. They could be back at the fort in half a day.\nThey agreed quickly: safety first, and introductions after.</p>\n<p>Findemir could not ride, and so Már built a sledge. It lay low to the ground behind a horse, and she\nbent saplings over it so that they sprang and cushioned it as it rocked, and though the ground was\nrough the litter hardly moved. It was very fine work. Perry rode Leaper back.</p>\n<p>They reached the gate of Barad Tarminalë and shouted. \"Good news! We found him! He's alive!\"</p>\n<h2>The evening at the fort</h2>\n<p>Moriel took Findemir in at once and saw to his wounds.</p>\n<p>Már had questions of her own. The others knew Findemir only by hearsay; they had come looking for\nhim. She had been with him, and yet she knew almost as little. He had talked to her about his errand,\nand she had understood hardly any of it. He had needed to find something. There was some kind of\nmission, and she was supposed to be going along with him. Nobody had ever told her why. \"They sent\nme with him and told me nothing,\" she said. \"I do everything for them, and they tell me nothing.\"</p>\n<p>Lily was over the worst of her shock, and she did what she knew how to do. She put her innkeeper's\nmanner back on and made supper, and opened the preserves Goldberry had given them, and passed round\nher flask of liquor. It had been a long day. She told Moriel and Már about Bree and the ill feeling\nthere, and how they had come looking for Findemir because they needed help. She did not mention Tom.\nNone of them did. They did not understand Tom themselves, and they did not know how they would\nexplain him to anyone else.</p>\n<p>Lily set her spear in a pot of boiling water to take the frost out of it. It worked; the frost went.\nBut the frost left something behind. Etched into the metal, like the pattern on frosted glass, was an\nafter-image of it, and it would not come off.</p>\n<p>Perry sat with Moriel and asked her everything she could tell him about treating wounds. He had\nrealised that he was wholly incompetent at it. She told him the mud had not been the right move,\nthough it was not always the wrong one; mostly you should wash it. Perry took a great deal of this in.\nHe had some ideas of his own, such as giving a man who was losing blood some of his own. Moriel said\nthat was a lesson for a good deal later. In a few generations, said Lily, the Oakroots might get\nthere.</p>\n<p>Makheneb had gathered up his arrows after the fight. They were covered in the wolves' blood, and he\nsat and studied them. Long ago, when he was young, he had gone on an expedition into the southern\nforest of Mirkwood while the Necromancer still held Dol Guldur. That was the worst place he had ever\nbeen, and the worst thing he had ever felt. This was worse. The longer he looked at it, the more it\nwas like handling something that burned without heat. He snapped the arrow in his hands and threw it\nin the fire. He said nothing to the others. He did not want to frighten them away.</p>\n<p>Lily and Perry talked by the fire. \"How are you doing?\" she asked him.</p>\n<p>\"You ran in screaming,\" said Perry. \"That was impressive. Though I found the haystack confusing.\"</p>\n<p>\"Thank you,\" said Lily. \"It turns out it was not a haystack.\"</p>\n<p>Perry asked what they made of the Dwarf they had picked up. She had simply fallen in with them, and\nthey with her. She had not had much choice. Perry had been half-conscious on his horse for most of\nthe way back, and had hardly seen her.</p>\n<p>It was a quiet evening, and in the course of it the five of them began, a little, to choose one\nanother. Perry felt he owed Makheneb for the shot that had taken the wolf off him, and he wanted to\nbe in the good graces of a traveller who had seen so much more of the world. Már found herself\nwatching out for Marigold. She was used to being the smallest in any company, and here she was not\nthe smallest. Lily was still worried for Perry, and a little guilty that she had not been able to\nkeep her own cousin from harm. And Marigold took to Lily, who was curious about everything and\nalways caring for somebody, and was up at five in the morning like herself.</p>\n<h2>Moriel's charge</h2>\n<p>Late in the evening Moriel took Marigold aside. She had Findemir's satchel, and she laid out what was\nin it on the table: a book bound in leather, a map, and some other things.</p>\n<p>\"Findemir will need a great deal of care,\" she said. \"I expect I'll be here tending him for a while.\nIt seems he was working on something that can't wait. I know you were attacked, and I don't say this\nlightly. But I need you to finish what he started.\"</p>\n<p>She hesitated. \"This isn't something that's shared outside the Dúnedain. But I trust you, in a way I\ncan't quite explain.\"</p>\n<p>She unrolled the map and went over it with Marigold. They had been heading up into the downs, north\nof here. On the map was something Marigold had never seen marked on any map: ruins, underneath what\nlooked like a natural cavern, inside the cavern itself.</p>\n<p>People speak of the First Age and the Second and the Third, Moriel said; the Third is the present\ncount of years, the last few thousand. These ruins went back to the First Age, and the records about\nthem were very incomplete. Marigold had never heard the word <em>Númenórean</em>. Moriel explained that the\nNúmenóreans were the people who had lived in these lands many thousands of years ago. Some of their\nruins lay underground now. There were whole cities hidden away, with the earth risen over them, and\ncities under the sea.</p>\n<p>This place was a very important one, and it needed tending on a schedule. The journal set out the\ninstructions: how to get in, and what to do once inside. There were two levels. One could be called\nthe outside of the front door, and the other the inside of the house, short of the inner vestibule.\nOn each level there was what Moriel called an artifact, though it was not a single thing. It was part\nof the building, a working piece of it, and it had to be kept whole and clean and working. If it was\nbroken, or had pieces missing, that had to be put right.</p>\n<p>It sounded to Marigold like being asked to go through an old empty warehouse and make sure the lamps\nworked on every floor. The journal was clear enough. Its language was odd: it read as if it were a\ncouple of hundred years old, though the instructions were plainly recent. The map was clear too, and\nit led to the same place they had been making for.</p>\n<p>\"Here,\" Moriel said, and put the whole of it into Marigold's hands. \"Once you're there, it should\ntake you a day at the most.\"</p>\n<p>\"We will carry on this mission,\" said Marigold. \"You won't regret trusting us.\"</p>\n<p>Moriel hugged her.</p>\n<h2>The blizzard</h2>\n<p>Marigold woke the others at five in the morning. \"We've got work to do,\" she said, and spread the map\nout, and told them. It was a big responsibility. It was also, she thought, pretty easy. They could\ncertainly handle it.</p>\n<p>It was the direction they had been going anyway, a day and a half away. Lily said that Tom and\nGoldberry had told them to help Findemir, and this was helping Findemir. They would need to learn a\nsong for wolves, Perry said, next time they saw Tom. Már felt that she owed it to Findemir; she had\nbeen given this task in all but name when she was sent with him.</p>\n<p>Some of it did not quite make sense to her. If this was only a matter of going into a warehouse and\nseeing that the lamps worked, why had her clan sent someone whose work was the dead? The thought did\nnot come into her head.</p>\n<p>They camped the first night. On the second day it grew noticeably colder as they climbed. It was not\na mountain they were climbing, only higher hills, a few of them standing shoulder to shoulder; but by\nthe time they came near the place where the cavern should be, they were riding into a full blizzard.</p>\n<p>They could see very little. It felt unnatural, or at least unseasonable. It might have been only a\nfreak storm. None of them had been this far north before, and none of them knew what was usual here.\nThey were very cold. Everyone had put on every layer they owned, with blankets over the top. Már\nrode behind Makheneb on Strawberry, the sprightliest of their mounts.</p>\n<p>Makheneb asked Marigold whether the journal gave any sense of how urgent this was. It did. The work\nhad to be done under a certain moon, within a month at most, or perhaps half a month. They did not\nknow where they stood in that window. An hour or a day would make no difference, but they could not\ngo away for a couple of weeks and come back.</p>\n<p>Perry was worried that if they pressed on now they would go in the wrong direction. His maps did not\nreach this far north. They agreed to look for shelter, and if there was none, to push on.</p>\n<p>Lily found it. Something told her to go higher, though going higher meant more wind. At the top of\none of the hills there were ruins: a large circle of cobbles, broken up, with a low wall round parts\nof it and a ring of pillars, most of them long since fallen. The wall was about three feet high, and\nbetween the wall and the drifts it made a good break from the wind, with room for a fire. No one had\nbeen there. The snow might have hidden the signs, but there were no other signs at all.</p>\n<p>They sat out the storm there for a few hours. It was not quite nightfall when the blizzard died, and\nthe last of the sun came out, though the valley below was already partly in shadow.</p>\n<h2>The cave</h2>\n<p>Perry kept watch on the way they had come, in case the wolves were tracking them; this was wolf\ncountry. Now that the air had cleared he could see a very long way south, and he could see the\nchange from winter into spring across miles of land.</p>\n<p>Makheneb looked the other way, inward, down into a bowl among the hills. Three of the great wolves\nwere walking in single file out of a patch of shadow. He looked again and saw that the shadow was\nthe mouth of a cavern. The wolves were leaving it. He was several hundred feet above them and a long\nway off, and the wind was blowing from them to him. He followed them with his eyes as they wound up\nbetween two of the far foothills and into a glade, half a mile off and still going. Then they were\ngone.</p>\n<p>He climbed down and told the others. \"Three of the big ones. They were leaving.\"</p>\n<p>The place matched the documents. It was the only cave they had seen.</p>\n<p>\"I wouldn't trust them to stay gone,\" said Makheneb.</p>\n<p>The journal said the work had last been done about forty years ago. It said nothing about wolves. But\na great deal can happen in forty years.</p>\n<p>They decided to scout it while the light held. The entrance was wide, perhaps forty feet across, with\na low overhang of frozen snow across the front of it, so that from outside it looked like a low, wide\nslot. Inside, the rock went up in a great arch, thirty feet high or more. Már and Makheneb stayed\noutside to keep watch, and the other three crept in. Perry had so many pots and pans hung about him\nthat he rang like a tinker's cart, and somebody had to reach over and still them.</p>\n<p>At first it was a cave like any other, with earth and pebbles underfoot, and bones, and scat. By the\nlight of Perry's lantern they could see one side where the wolves plainly lay up and slept, though\nnone were there now. Then the cave began to wind, curving slowly round to the right and sloping\ngently down. About the length of a great hall in, the floor under their feet turned to cobbles. The\ncave was natural, but this was not.</p>\n<p>They could not see where it ended. They went back for the others.</p>\n<h2>A night of traps</h2>\n<p>There was a long argument. If they went down now, they might finish before the wolves came back, but\nthey would be hurrying, and they would have to come out the same way, perhaps into the wolves, and\nperhaps hurt. If they waited, they could learn the wolves' habits, but that would waste time. And\nhad the wolves only been passing through? The den inside said otherwise. It was not one day's worth\nof bones.</p>\n<p>In the end they settled on traps. They would give it one night at most. If the wolves came back, the\ntraps would tell them. If not, they would go down in the morning.</p>\n<p>Lily and Marigold went out for materials and came back again and again with armloads of dead branches\nand kindling, and pine boughs, which burn well. Már set to work on a pit in the mouth of the cave,\nset sharpened stakes in the bottom of it, and laid pine branches over the top. They piled kindling\nbeside it so that a torch would set it all ablaze. Makheneb asked whether they could make fire-arrows\nso that no one would need to run in with a torch, and it was easily done: a rag, a little of Lily's\nliquor, and the lantern.</p>\n<p>While he waited, Makheneb felt out the thing below them. Evil has flavours, as spices do. To someone\nwho cannot bear spice, all of it is simply hot; to someone who has grown used to it, one heat is quite\nunlike another. This place had a deep foreboding in it, but it was different from Dol Guldur. It\nintrigued him. He did not feel better prepared for it. He felt drawn. He had found the trail, and\nthis was the place. However frightening that was, this was where he wanted to be, and this was the\nthing he wanted to hunt.</p>\n<p>They waited on the hillside downwind of the cave, near enough to strike quickly. Lily went up to check\non the horses. They would rather have been somewhere warmer, but they were keeping close together for\nthe heat, and they were content.</p>\n<p>The night passed. The wolves did not come back.</p>\n<h2>Down</h2>\n<p>The morning was bright and cold, the kind of cold that has a crystal quality in the sunshine. There\nwas no hot breakfast.</p>\n<p>They left the horses in the ruins on the hilltop, which was the safest place for them, tied with the\nkind of knot a horse can pull free if it has to. If they went in and did not come out, the horses\nwould not be left to starve. If anything happened to Strawberry, Makheneb said, he was never going\nback; he would take the road west to the Grey Havens instead. Lily kissed every one of the animals on\nthe forehead.</p>\n<p>Then they went down. The way sloped gently and kept on sloping. They kept to the pace of the shortest\nlegs among them and went as quietly as they could. It was like going down a very wide, very slow\nspiral stair with no stairs. It took about an hour.</p>\n<p>At the bottom the passage opened into a chamber so large that their light did not reach its walls.\nLily went back up the passage a couple of hundred feet, to where she could still see the others'\nlights, and there she put out her own lantern and kept watch in the dark, listening back up the way\nthey had come.</p>\n<p>In the middle of the floor was a wheel of stone. It was set into the floor and stood about four feet\nhigh, round, and some eight feet across, and a line of symbols ran round it. Marigold took out the\njournal and compared the two. She found where the phrase began and followed it round.</p>\n<p>Two pieces were missing, like two slices out of a pie, and the gaps broke the phrase. One of them lay\non the ground beside the wheel. Marigold lifted it and fitted it back into its place. The other was\nnot anywhere near.</p>\n<p>Looking closely at the edges, they saw the marks of a chisel. Someone had set out to break the\nwheel on purpose, and not long ago. It had been done within the last year.</p>\n<p>One of them had seen something glint against the far wall as they came in, and turned a lantern\nthat way. There were doors: a pair of them, huge, faced with a skin of metal worked in filigree. The\ndesign was abstract and flowing and graceful, and the style of it was utterly strange to all of them.\nThe doors did not look as though they had been opened in a long time.</p>\n<p>\"That is not Dwarf-work,\" said Már. \"Not any kind.\"</p>\n<p>Somebody asked whether they could open it. Somebody else said, \"Slow down.\" They did not know what\nwas on the other side.</p>\n<p>They went round the walls of the chamber instead, all together, with the lanterns. Near the wall, not\nfar from the doors, they found a skeleton, and it was holding the missing piece.</p>\n<h2>The belt</h2>\n<p>It had been dead a long while; there was nothing left of it but bones. Nothing had eaten it. It had\nnot been mauled. It looked human, and its clothes had not rotted at all. There was a weapon beside it,\nand some other things.</p>\n<p>Perry looked at it without touching it, and remembered.</p>\n<p>A year and a half ago, perhaps two, there had been a very shady-looking man outside Bree, asking a\ngreat many questions. Perry had pulled him aside at the gate and was thinking of taking it further,\nand then Perry's superior had come over and said, <em>I'll take care of this.</em> When Perry followed it up\nafterwards, the answers he got did not make sense. They implied that nothing had really happened, and\nthat the man had gone on his way without hindrance. Perry had never had a good opinion of the man he\nanswered to at the gate, though he was not one to hold a poor opinion of anybody, and he had kept it\ndown. He supposed his superior must have his reasons.</p>\n<p>That man had worn a braided belt of blue and white. Perry had never seen another like it. And here it\nwas, on the skeleton.</p>\n<p>\"This is him,\" said Perry. \"Do you remember, a year and a half back? This man asking all sorts of\nquestions outside town?\"</p>\n<p>Lily had heard about it from some of the farmsteads. It had become something of an issue.</p>\n<p>As Perry described him, the man took shape before them in the air: a shadowy form, in the likeness of\nthe man he remembered.</p>\n<p>Every lantern went out.</p>\n<h2>The ghost</h2>\n<p>Up the passage, Lily had let her eyes grow used to the dark. She had been watching the others' lights,\nand all at once the lights went out, every one of them. She called out: \"What's going on over there?\"</p>\n<p>What came back was shouting and shrieking.</p>\n<p>In the chamber it was black. There was a thing in the dark with them, and they could not see it. Már\nknew the dead as well as anyone living, and this was not like the dead she knew. She was a long way\nfrom home ground. But she knew one thing about it. It had put out the lanterns because it hated light\nand fire. That was a thing they might use.</p>\n<p>\"Follow my voice,\" someone called. \"Come to me. We'll get together so we can feel we're together, and\nput our backs to each other.\"</p>\n<p>They groped their way together. Makheneb struck his lantern alight again. The light blinded him for a\nmoment, and when his eyes opened the ghost was right in front of him. Its hand closed over the flame,\nand as it drew back the flame went out again. But he had seen it, if only for an instant, and he had\nan arrow ready. The arrow passed through it with an unnatural, shrill sound, and then it was dark\nagain.</p>\n<p>Már set herself to guard the lantern that was left. And when the light came again and Már saw the thing in it, she had had enough. She swung her axe with all\nher strength behind it and cleaved through the ghost. They heard the crunch of the skeleton behind it\nas the blade went on through. There was a howl that seemed to suck the air out of the room, and then\nsilence.</p>\n<p>They lit the lanterns. There was nothing there but the crushed and cloven bones. Everyone stood very\nstill for a long moment.</p>\n<p>Then they took the missing piece from the skeleton's hands, carried it back to the wheel, and fitted\nit in. There were two layers to the stone. The lower one was unharmed, and the piece rested on it\nsecurely, and the carving ran whole round the wheel again.</p>\n<p>As it settled into place, they heard a soft sound. The great doors had opened, a little way, inward.</p>", "played": "2025-05-20", "slug": "02-findemir-and-the-ruins", "title": "Findemir & the Ruins"}, {"date": "Spring, 2965", "html": "<h2>What the ghost had been</h2>\n<p>The great doors stood open a crack, and nobody went through them at once.</p>\n<p>\"I've seen a few ghosts in my day,\" said Már. \"That one was the most sinister I have ever met.\"</p>\n<p>\"You see why Findemir was bringing you along,\" somebody said. \"You and ghosts, split with an axe.\"</p>\n<p>Perry knew the ghost. He had known the man it had been. \"We tried to bar him from Bree,\" he said, \"and he\nstill got in. He went round by a back way.\"</p>\n<p>What had the man been doing here? He had tried to take a piece of the wheel away, and with a piece\nmissing the doors would never open. Was that what he had wanted, to keep them shut? Or had somebody\nsent him, as somebody had sent them, only to do the opposite? He had not seemed the leading kind to\nPerry. He had seemed like the kind of man someone sends to do their dirty work. And if someone had\nsent him, there might be others.</p>\n<p>They had tramped all over the floor by now, but they looked for footprints anyway, working round the\nroom from the door, past the wheel, and on to the place where the body lay. They could tell their own\nprints from any others: this was Makheneb's, and this was Lily's. Once those were set aside, the dead\nman's movements could be followed through the dust. Following them, they found a ledge in a dark\ncorner of the wall, a little above head height and nowhere near the doors. On it was a Ranger's\npack. Inside was dried food of the kind that keeps in a cool place underground: an emergency store.\nIt was not food for a hard march. It was enough to keep one person from starving for two or three\nweeks, until they could get somewhere better.</p>\n<p>\"A gift from Findemir,\" said somebody. If they were shut in behind those doors, there was food for\none of them for three weeks, or for all five of them for about five days.</p>\n<p>Near the doors they found a second set of prints, not the dead man's. They went round and round the\nwheel of stone, and then they went in through the doors, into the inner chamber. They did not come\nout again. The doors had been opened after all, and not so long ago.</p>\n<p>Már looked at the skeleton. Undead were her trade, and she knew how odd deaths looked. Round its neck\nwas a necklace of Dwarvish make, with a badger worked on it in profile. She did not believe for a\nmoment that the man had come by it honestly. She took it, and wearing it she felt a little more\nformidable than before.</p>\n<p>Then she read the bones as Perry had read the floor. The man had fallen several feet away from the\nwall, and then he had pushed himself backward along the ground until the wall stopped him. He had\nbeen trying to get away from something. She thought he had died of terror.</p>\n<p>\"Did he just need his friend to open the door,\" Lily wondered, \"and then his friend said goodbye?\"</p>\n<h2>The stair</h2>\n<p>The doors were higher than the chamber's own ceiling and half again as wide as a room. They had\nopened inward just a crack, a couple of feet across. There were handles; to swing a door wide would\nhave taken two of them hauling together, and it might have been loud. They slipped through the gap\ninstead.</p>\n<p>Beyond, a wide staircase went down, as wide as the doors. Here were the ruins proper. But nothing had\ncome in here in earnest, and it had been sealed for most of its long life, so it was not ruined at\nall, only old and unattended. The stonework was beautiful. The floor was level and the stairs were\ntrue stairs. Lily badly wanted to touch the stone.</p>\n<p>Should they prop the door? They had nothing to do it with, short of the skeleton, and no one wanted to\ntouch that. In the end they simply went on.</p>\n<p>Going quietly was impossible. They had no light but the lanterns, and the lanterns would draw\nanything that was waiting for them; and stairs are loud. They went down together, the stairs wide\nenough for all of them, with one of them three steps behind to watch the way they had come. The stair\ncurved gently to the left, not a corkscrew but a slow turn, and went down three or four storeys.</p>\n<h2>The chamber</h2>\n<p>At the bottom there were lamps along the walls, ready to be lit. The floor gave off a faint chill.\nIt looked almost crystalline, a pale blue, like frosted glass. It crunched a little underfoot, like\nice, though it did not feel like true ice. The light of their lanterns died away before it reached\nthe ceiling. On the walls were beautiful geometric patterns in tile, of the kind usually set into a\nfloor; a few had fallen, but most were whole.</p>\n<p>\"They'll see us regardless,\" somebody said. \"We might as well be able to see them.\" Perry began\nlighting the lamps as he passed.</p>\n<p>As the lamps caught one after another, the room came out of the dark. It was very large, some two\nhundred and fifty feet across, and nearly eight-sided. The ceiling arched up to a small, shallow dome.\nThere were three side rooms. In the middle was a wheel of stone like the one outside: a wide, flat\nround of stone, but four times as broad. And standing on it was a man.</p>\n<p>He was holding the hilt of a sword. The rest of the blade lay in pieces about his feet. He was bent\nover the stone as though he had been trying to prise it open, or break it, and he was absolutely\nstill.</p>\n<p>He had skin. He was not a skeleton, and he was not quite a statue. He looked like a perfect likeness\nof himself, in all his colours, only drained of them and turned dull and drab. There was no\nbreath and no movement in him. Marigold touched him. His skin was cold, like cold stone, and yet\nthere was still some give to it. He had not been turned to stone. He had simply stopped.</p>\n<p>The sight of him chilled them all.</p>\n<p>The sword had been a long sword. \"That's a noble's weapon,\" somebody said. \"It's not what the other\none would carry.\" Perhaps this was the man who had hired him.</p>\n<p>Perry looked at the hilt. It bore a bird of prey, an eagle perhaps. He had seen something like it once\nbefore, though not the same. A Ranger who came through Bree had carried a sword with a hilt of that\nkind, and had said it was an heirloom of his forefathers, and that the first pieces of it went back\nto the Second Age, thousands of years ago. This was not that sword, but it came from the same family\nof things. It was as old as this place.</p>\n<p>The man had been trying to drive the blade into the stone like a wedge. There was hardly a seam to\ndrive it into, only a tiny crack.</p>\n<p>The Bree-folk had seen enough people come through their town to judge him. He was a Man, and they\nguessed he came from further south than Tharbad. He was no Ranger.</p>\n<h2>The side rooms</h2>\n<p>Makheneb went round the three side rooms while the others stood by the wheel.</p>\n<p>The first was a small living space, with room for two or three to sleep, a chest, some blankets left\nbehind, a table, and a whetstone. Anyone who needed to stay here for a while could.</p>\n<p>The second was for reading and writing. It had a rough wooden chair and a table, slanted shelves for\nscrolls, and a shelf and a half of books bound in leather. The scroll-shelves were two-thirds full.\nThere was a jar of ink. It was a spartan room; the books and scrolls were by far the most valuable\nthings in it.</p>\n<p>The third was a store. Spare furniture for the other rooms was kept there, and shelves of things\nwrapped in cloths and leather. Makheneb unwrapped some of them. They were relics, extremely old, some\nof precious metal and some of exquisite craft. At the back were a few weapons and some armour, of\nstrange style and interesting workmanship.</p>\n<p>Before he said a word to anyone, he went over it for any taint. He was more learned in the Shadow than\nthe others. None of it felt like the two dead men. The things in this room looked as though they had\nalways been here, and were meant to be. There was also a bow there, a very fine one. He took it.</p>\n<p>Then he came back and told the others that there was treasure in there, some kind of armoury, and he\nwas not sure it was theirs to take. Perhaps it was the sort of place where you take a bowl and leave a\nbowl.</p>\n<h2>The wheel, and what was under the floor</h2>\n<p>Marigold compared the carving on the great wheel with the journal. It matched perfectly. She did not\nknow the language, but she had heard of protective charms, and of wards, and of words that seal\nsomething shut. She thought this might be one. If so, what mattered was that the words were whole.\nThey were whole. They were here to check on things, and this checked out.</p>\n<p>Lily was all for leaving the frozen man. Moriel had told them to check the symbols, not to rescue\npeople who had been caught trying to break them. But nobody liked the thought of leaving somebody who\nmight still be alive.</p>\n<p>While they talked, Marigold saw something. A faint vapour, almost too slight to see, was rising from\nthe crack in the stone where the man had been prying. She felt a slight chill from it before it\nthinned away. \"We should get him off that,\" she said.</p>\n<p>Then Perry and Lily looked down.</p>\n<p>The floor under their feet was beautiful, and they had thought it opaque. Something shifted, and they\nsaw that it was not as opaque as they had thought. The whole floor was a window. Under it was a beast.\nIts back had been pressed up against the underside of the glass, and now it rolled over.</p>\n<p>They saw it in profile. It was a couple of hundred feet long. It had fur like a bear's, white, and\nhuge horns and tusks. It was shaped more like a man than a dragon; if they had known of such a thing,\nthey would have thought of an ape of monstrous size. It was asleep. It had only rolled over in its\nsleep. None of them had heard, even in a fairy-story, of anything like it.</p>\n<p>The dread of it went through all of them. Then it settled, and it did not move again.</p>\n<h2>The ledger</h2>\n<p>Nobody wanted to stay long after that. But the journal said that the one who came to check should add\nan entry to the log, and Marigold meant to do it properly.</p>\n<p>They tiptoed into the reading room. The book lying readiest to hand was the log. Every few decades\nthere was a new entry, and most of them were perfunctory: the date, who had come, that they had checked\nthe things they were meant to check and stayed three days, and that all was well. Sometimes they added\nsomething else they had done or noticed in the country round about. Year after year after year, it\nwent back. Even the recent entries were written in a kind of shorthand that Marigold guessed belonged\nto the Rangers. Going further back, the writing grew harder and harder to read, until a couple of\ncenturies back she could not read it at all.</p>\n<p>There was nothing in it about a monster. There was nothing about a frozen man.</p>\n<p>It was, Marigold thought, like the note on the wall of a privy that says who cleaned it last and\nwhether the soap had been filled. And now here was she: <em>Marigold was here</em>, not looking at the\nmonster the whole time.</p>\n<p>She wrote the date and a full account of what they had found. In the margins she described the sleeping\nbeast. Then, with a good deal of skill, she drew it. The head and the face came out especially well,\nand she caught the shape of the rest. It took her the best part of an hour.</p>\n<p>Lily read what she could meanwhile. The scrolls were in a different, older language, in a much more\nflowing hand. Even in the books she could not read, the years were numbered in a way she could follow,\nand she could see that some of the earliest were five hundred or seven hundred years old. The entries\nvaried, too. In one book an entry ran to eight or ten pages, and the next ran to a page and a half, and\nthe next to half a page. That long one was from about a hundred and fifty years ago. Someone, then, had\nmet with something. Lily could not read what.</p>\n<p>After three-quarters of an hour Lily was frightened and fascinated at once, and after an hour she was\nmaking frantic gestures at the others to wrap it up. Was the thing asleep for the winter, or only\ntaking a nap? What had done this to the man? She did not like that he looked petrified and not dead,\nwith that underneath him, and she wanted to be a long way from the floor.</p>\n<h2>The armoury</h2>\n<p>Perry went to the storeroom to see whether there was anything in it that might help them if the beast\ngot loose. There were some daggers. There was a gap where a sword had been, and Perry guessed it was\nthe sword the frozen man had broken. There was another gap where a bow had been — the one Makheneb had\ntaken. There were a great many arrows and arrowheads, a halberd, and some breastplates, ornamental ones\nby the look of them, with crests none of them knew.</p>\n<p>A breastplate sounded good to Perry. Steel had to be better than leather. He put one on in place of his\ncorslet. It was made for a man a good foot taller than he was, but not so much larger that he could not\nwear it.</p>\n<p>The arrowheads looked new and fit for use, not decoration. Makheneb did not trust the old shafts, but\nthe heads were good, and he could fletch new arrows easily enough. Arrowheads were not really treasure,\nhe reasoned. He took some, and dropped a few more where the others could pick them up.</p>\n<p>Már went to the store as well, to see whether there was any sign of the dead in it. There was none;\nbut there were the shelves. There were candelabra, and goblets, and pottery that had kept its shape\nthough its colours had faded, all of it exquisite, and some of the craftsmanship was new even to a\nDwarf. She meant to take only a little, and she took more. She took two goblets, a ring worked with a\nbird, and a band of gold for her hair set with very tasteful gems. It would look good on her.</p>\n<p>Makheneb gathered up the shards of the broken sword and wrapped them in a cloth, and worked the hilt out\nof the frozen man's hands. The hands were firm enough that the hilt came away on its own. It might be\nreforged somewhere.</p>\n<h2>The frozen man</h2>\n<p>In the end they would not leave him. It would be wrong to leave a man who might be alive to die down\nthere. And if he knew something, it might be worth a great deal.</p>\n<p>They tied him to a cot from the sleeping room, for they did not trust him not to wake up possessed, and\na couple of them carried him. His limbs could be moved, they found, but they did not dare try. Perry put\nout every lamp he had lit. They carried the man up the stairs and out into the outer chamber, and pushed\nthe great doors shut behind them. There was no click, but the doors felt secure.</p>\n<p>Far away up the passage, the way they had come, something whined, high and thin. Then something howled.</p>\n<p>Marigold felt a cold place in the room. Past the stone wheel, near the skeleton, there was a lump on the\nfloor that had not been there when they went in. It looked like a lump of ice, and it was changing. As\nshe watched, a paw came out of it, and then another, not quite finished. It was making itself out of\nthe ice.</p>\n<p>\"We have to go,\" she said. \"We have to go.\"</p>\n<p>\"Light me an arrow,\" said Makheneb.</p>\n<p>They went on up with the frozen man between them, and Makheneb walked backwards with his bow, waiting.\nThe thing's face began to form. It was the face of one of the wolves they had fought. He shot it through\nthe head with a burning arrow just as it found its shape. The fire went on burning inside it, and it\nburned from the inside out. It had not even formed well enough to howl. It gave a pale whimper, and fell\nin on itself, and melted away.</p>\n<p>At the top of the long slope the mouth of the cave showed against the evening light. There was a wolf\nthere, one only, looking in and down. In the pit, something was still moaning.</p>\n<p>They charged it all together, and Már led them. She was carrying the frozen man's feet, and she let\nthem go and bellowed and went forward, staring at the wolf with the badger at her throat. She was the\nbigger dog. The wolf froze where it stood and met her eyes. Then it yelped, and turned, and ran.</p>\n<p>They went round the pit. The wolf that had fallen in was dead now on the stakes. As they came out of the\ncave they could see, far off on the other side, three wolves running away over the horizon.</p>\n<h2>By night</h2>\n<p>They went up to the ruins for the horses. They had four mounts for the five of them and the frozen\nman, and they doubled up; the frozen man rode on Leaper in front of Perry. They would not reach Barad\nTarminalë before dark. It would be three or four hours after nightfall. They decided to push on, since\nthere would be a good rest at the end of it.</p>\n<p>On the way they stopped for an hour to see whether the man could be woken. Somebody felt for a pulse.\nThere was none, and no breath. Nothing that had happened, not even all the jolting, had stirred him.\nLily brought out her healing balm.</p>\n<p>Makheneb waited for nightfall. Then he laid the man down on a bed of leaves and tried the ways of his\nown people, the Elvish ways, and set himself to drive out whatever darkness had come into him. The\nhealing did nothing. But as he worked, a realisation came over him. He had dealt with things born of\nthe evil of Dol Guldur, and things near to it. This came from an evil as great as that, or greater, and\nthere was nothing in him that could undo it. The man was not dead. But Makheneb knew of no way to bring\nhim back.</p>\n<p>\"This is some bad magic,\" he told the others.</p>\n<p>They searched the man's pockets. There were no marks on him. Inside one pocket they found a slip of paper\nrolled up and tied to the lining, so that it could not be lost. When they unrolled it there was a\npassphrase written on it, like the call or the answer that gets you in at a locked door.</p>\n<h2>Ghostcleavers</h2>\n<p>They came to Barad Tarminalë in the dark, and rested there.</p>\n<p>They called for Tom, as Tom had told them they might in need, but Tom did not answer.</p>\n<p>In the days of rest that followed, they made a song of victory for Már. They called it <em>Ghostcleavers</em>.</p>\n<p>About five days later, Marigold and Lily woke early, as they always did, and found Tom Bombadil in the\nkitchen cooking breakfast.</p>\n<p>Tom looked at the frozen man. He said plainly that he could not undo what had been done to him.</p>\n<p>Then Marigold showed him her drawing of the white beast under the floor, and Tom changed. He knew it.\nThere were several of these beings, he told them, bound or sleeping in secret places. He took Perry's\nmap, and on it he marked where others lay: in the North Moors, near the Shire; south of Tharbad; and in\nthe Blue Mountains.</p>\n<h2>The road west</h2>\n<p>When the Company set out west, they fell in with another company on the road, a curious and peaceful\none. There was Celenneth, a Ranger of the Dúnedain, and Linnea, a woman of Bree, who was her partner;\ntheir children, Brynja, the older girl, and Hallas, who was still small; and with them Eryndil, a\nscholarly Elf of Lindon.</p>\n<p>The two companies travelled together for a time under the early summer sky, and told each other their\nstories, and what they meant to do.</p>", "played": "2025-06-03", "slug": "03-the-inner-chamber", "title": "The Inner Chamber"}, {"date": "Early Summer, 2965", "html": "<h2>Fellow travellers</h2>\n<p>For some days the Company travelled west with Celenneth and her household: Linnea, her partner;\ntheir two children; and the Elf Eryndil, who rode a horse laden with heavy bags. They skirted the\nsouthern end of the great lake of Evendim, and the ruins of Annúminas lay ahead of them.</p>\n<p>Celenneth's party was bound for the Grey Havens. Lily had never heard of the Grey Havens. She had no\nidea how far west it was, only that it was west. To her the name sounded like a refuge, a place of\nshelter, the sort of place where people went when they had nothing. That puzzled her, because\nEryndil was plainly a person of some standing, with fine clothes and a very fine horse, and did not\nlook poor or hungry in the least. But you could not judge, Lily thought. You never knew what was\ngoing on with people. One evening, when camp was made, Celenneth and Eryndil went off into the woods\nfor something and Linnea was busy with the children, and Lily took her chance. She stuffed their\npacks with apples and whatever else she had that might spoil, and hoped it would all come in\nuseful. She did not ask anyone about it. She saw a problem, and she solved it.</p>\n<p>Marigold asked Celenneth whether she had ever been in the North Moors. There were stories among\nhobbits of tree-men there. Celenneth had travelled widely, but mostly by the roads. Once she had\ncrossed the hills of Evendim on her way to the mountains, and that had been much further north than\nthe Moors. She did not know this country. The only thing she had heard of it was a strange tree\nthat was said to stand there, called the Tree of Sorrow. She knew a little tale about it. The tree\nhad been made by many trees growing together, fusing themselves into one to shelter from some\nthreat. It was told like a fable, short and plain, and she could not vouch for any of it.</p>\n<p>Már did not know the country either. Her people were of the Grey Mountains, far away in the\nnorth-east, beyond Mirkwood; she had come the way they had all come, from the east.</p>\n<h2>The scholar's library</h2>\n<p>Perry had a question for Eryndil. He had assumed that all Elves were immortal and had been about\nsince the dawn of time, if not before, so he asked her whether she had heard anything of what Tom\nhad called <em>Melkor's playthings</em>. She was a scholar, and she did not answer off the top of her head.\nShe waited until they had made camp.</p>\n<p>Then she unloaded her horse. The heavy bags were full of books, one after another, and some were\ngreat ancient tomes. She laid a piece of leather on the ground and stacked them on it with care, and\nbegan to look through them for the thing she half remembered. Perry is a man who likes to handle\nthings. He tried to help, awkwardly, and flipped a little too hard through some of the oldest pages,\nlooking for pictures.</p>\n<p>What she found was a verse from an old epic ballad, the kind of poem that gathers the great deeds of\nthe past into song. It spoke of beings none of the Company had heard of, called the Valar. They were\ndescribed almost as gods, though it was no religion the Company knew, and each had a domain of its\nown. Aulë was the smith, and was said to have made the Dwarves; there were Ulmo, and Yavanna, and\nothers. The verse warned that Morgoth had made a weapon for each of the Valar, fitted to each one's\nweakness, and that those weapons had been lost.</p>\n<p>The Company told her what they had seen under the frosted floor. There was a creature like that in\nthe verse. It was named there as the weapon against Tulkas, the Vala of strength and of battle.</p>\n<p>The language of the poem was overwrought and often vague. It had seven sections. Some of them were\ncryptic. One spoke of <em>a collection of the corrupted souls of the Immortal</em>. One named something\ncalled <em>Maiar</em>. And one was called <em>Lúthien's Disguise</em>.</p>\n<p>Eryndil was very careful about all of it. The ballad moved the way such poems do, she said. It\nzoomed in on a single deed, done in a single year by a single person, and then it zoomed out into\nbroad mythic language, and in those passages you could not tell within a few hundred years when it\nwas speaking of. At best you could say the beginning, the middle or the end of an age. Elsewhere it\nnamed mountain ranges and cities, but often by names they no longer bore. She told them what she\nthought the verse meant, and then told them not to quote her on it.</p>\n<p>The floodgates had opened for Perry some time ago. He had always known there were big things out in\nthe world beyond Bree, and now he was learning of so many at once that none of it could shock him\nany longer. There were evil things, and dead gods, and Immortals going about. Of course there were.\nHe trusted all Elves implicitly. Anything, just then, seemed possible to him.</p>\n<p>\"Did you ever meet Melkor?\" he asked.</p>\n<p>\"Thankfully, no,\" said Eryndil. \"I came of age in the days of Arnor.\"</p>\n<p>\"We just passed Fornost,\" said Perry. \"What was Fornost like?\"</p>\n<p>Fornost, and Annúminas ahead of them, had both been remarkable, she said. This had been the kingdom\nof Men for many, many years. There had been a time of great battles in the east of Eriador, when\nevil came down out of Carn Dûm, and the battles ran along Fornost and all the way south towards\nTharbad. The south-west had been spared, but much of the land about them had been fought over, and\nsome of it overrun, and the kingdom had fallen. Gondor had lasted far longer, in the south.</p>\n<p>And west of here, she said, was the sea: the greatest of all seas. There were whole lands in it,\nislands as large as all this country, and more than once such lands had been sunk beneath the waves.</p>\n<p>Perry had not known there was a sea. He pictured an island in the middle of a lake, not a very big\none, and could not see how anyone could sink it.</p>\n<p>Across the ocean, Eryndil told him, lay another land entirely. Sadly, the children of Men were not\npermitted to go there.</p>\n<p>\"I've only just found out about something,\" said Perry, \"and I can't go there? I want to see the\nsea. Why can't we go?\"</p>\n<p>Eryndil looked at him. She said it was reserved for those who do not die of old age.</p>\n<p>Perry saw at once that she was lying to him. She had been so precise about everything else, and now,\nall of a sudden, she was vague and opaque, like someone softening a thing she knew and did not want\nto say. He pressed her. Could he not simply take a boat?</p>\n<p>\"Your ancestors did not come in peace,\" she said. They had tried to take what was not given, long\nago, and so the way west was barred to Men.</p>\n<p>\"My grandfather? These are his maps.\"</p>\n<p>\"Perhaps your grandfather's grandfather's grandfather's grandfather.\"</p>\n<p>So passage was not permitted to Men. Perry took it hard. He had only just learned there was such a\nthing as the Undying Lands.</p>\n<p>Eryndil looked at him with some interest. \"I'll be honest,\" she said. \"I don't know that anyone has\never tried.\"</p>\n<p>\"There'll be one,\" said Perry.</p>\n<h2>Tom's names</h2>\n<p>Lily had a question too. When the Company had told their story, had Eryndil ever heard of Tom and\nLady Goldberry?</p>\n<p>Eryndil had. \"Iarwain Ben-adar,\" she said. That was Tom's name among her people, and it meant <em>the\noldest, and fatherless</em>. \"Tom was in these lands when my people first came to Eriador. All these\nlands were Tom's lands.\" What were counted his lands now, she could not say. \"You would have to ask\nTom. I have heard nothing but good of him in the songs. He is only strange.\"</p>\n<p>Eryndil had children of her own. The youngest was about three hundred and fifty. One had already\nsailed west across the ocean. The other was dead, and had died at the Battle of Five Armies.</p>\n<p>Már had gone out to that battle too, though not in the front line; she was too needed at home, where\nthings had to be kept running. She had gone out to meet the host when she was wanted, and they wanted\nher now and then, for the dead.</p>\n<h2>The bow</h2>\n<p>Before the two companies parted, Makheneb showed the others the great bow he had taken from the\nruins. Something had been wrong with it all along. In the ruins every sense he had was flooded, the\nway it is hard to pick out one noise in a loud room. Now that they were far away and his senses had\nsettled, the wrongness remained, and he knew it was the bow itself.</p>\n<p>Eryndil and Celenneth looked it over with him. It was Númenórean, clearly. It might even be the Great\nBow of Angnir. There was a shadow upon it. But it had also been blessed with a straight flight, and\nan arrow loosed from it would not be turned aside.</p>\n<p>The others were told about the ballad, and the Valar, and the corrupted souls of the Immortal.\nMakheneb, at least, had heard of the Valar before, in the tales of the making of the world. Morgoth\nhad been one of them once: the note of discord in the great song, who turned against creation and\ntried to make things of his own. He had also heard it said that Orcs were Elves, once, corrupted.</p>\n<h2>Parting</h2>\n<p>Perry's travelling went well, and they were making good time. A little past the lake, in the\nmorning, the two companies parted. Celenneth's household went on west to meet the coast and follow\nthe river down to the Grey Havens.</p>\n<p>There were islands in the lake that might be explored. But they had just heard what happened to\nislands, and they were not sure islands were safe any more. Instead they turned south towards the\nShire, meaning to pass through the North Moors on the way and look for the Tree of Sorrow.</p>\n<p>Marigold had been born a North-Took, before she married a Cotton. She had distant relations up here\nwhom she had met once or twice, at family gatherings, though the bulk of the family lived nearer\nHobbiton and east of it.</p>\n<p>In the wild country Makheneb took his rope and grapnel and climbed one of the tallest trees to look\naround. The tree was not far off, perhaps three or four hours to the west. It was a very strange\ntree. It looked knotted, as though several kinds of trees were twined together, and it stood half\nagain as tall as anything around it.</p>\n<p>The forest here was feral. It was foothill country, much wilder than the woods near Bree, like the\nChetwood, that have travellers passing through them all the time. As they came nearer, the trees\nbegan to thin, and through gaps in the leaves they caught glimpses of the great tree high above them,\nand steered by it.</p>\n<h2>The standing stones</h2>\n<p>It was well past midday when they came out on a small hill crowned with six standing stones, each\neight or ten feet tall. They tied the horses there. Makheneb had seen such places before; this was a\ngood spot for watching the stars once night fell, and for any work to do with them. That was\nprobably what it had been built for, and perhaps for other rites besides.</p>\n<p>The stones were rough-hewn, but they had been quarried and stood up on purpose. One of them was new.\nThere were grooves in the hillside where a sledge had dragged it into place, overgrown now, but not\nold. This was a place that someone still tended. It was no forgotten ruin.</p>\n<p>Beyond the hill stood the tree. It was a mass of great roots rising out of the ground, and above the\nroots a trunk that looked like forty trees congealed together. Már saw that there were gaps among the\nroots, and some looked more deliberate than others. There might be a path in. Lily, squinting up\ninto the branches, thought she saw something like a treehouse, some two hundred feet up, though\nthere was no sign of anyone about.</p>\n<p>Perry's first thought was Elves.</p>\n<p>\"I don't think most of us live in trees,\" said Makheneb. \"Most of us in Mirkwood live in caves,\nstrange as that may be.\"</p>\n<p>Lily admitted then, a little late, that she had assumed all Elves were as poor as Makheneb, and had\nsecretly stuffed Eryndil's packs with food. Perry, for his part, was still cross about his\ngrandfather's grandfather's grandfather's grandfather.</p>\n<p>Whoever lived here might not want visitors, and whether they went up to the treehouse or into the\nroots, they would be intruding. So they decided to announce themselves. They would do it with a\nsong. The one they sang best was the one they had learned from Tom, the song for bears, and they\nsang it with a will. Perry had a cajón and beat time on it. What he lacked in voice he made up in\nenthusiasm, and Marigold's voice carried them. At worst, they said, whoever heard it would only think\nthere was a bear in the woods.</p>\n<h2>The long-folk</h2>\n<p>When the song ended there was a sharp whistle far above them, four or five storeys up. Five or six\nchildren were peering down from a limb of the tree, the smallest five or so and the eldest eleven or\ntwelve, and with them a grown woman with a bow. The bow was trained on the Company, but it was not\ndrawn.</p>\n<p>The children had plainly enjoyed the song. They did not greet the Company; they only watched. The\neldest hollered something down in a language none of them knew. It sounded eager, and a little\ncurious. Then one of them tossed something down.</p>\n<p>Már moved to catch it, but at the last instant she flinched. She thought it was something thrown at\nher, and she stepped back out of its way. It struck the rock and shattered. As it broke she saw that\nit had been a child's instrument, something like a pipe, thrown down to her as a gift. A cry of\ndistress came from above.</p>\n<p>Már panicked. All she had to offer was the treasure she had brought out of the ruins. She held up one\nof the silver goblets. The children were being steered away by then, and there was a great deal of\ntalk among them. The woman had still not drawn her bow. \"Maybe they should teach their children not\nto throw things,\" Már muttered.</p>\n<p>The people above were nearer to Men than to Elves or Dwarves, but they were stretched: long in the\nlimb, long in the neck, lanky in every part.</p>\n<p>The Company laid their weapons down in the circle of the standing stones and walked forward with\ntheir hands open. Lily went first. She smiled, without showing her teeth, and made signs, and at last\nsaid aloud, \"I don't speak that language. This is what I speak. Do you understand?\"</p>\n<p>The woman lowered her bow and gave a very sharp whistle through her fingers. From the path among the\nroots came three young men, perhaps six feet and a half tall, lanky like the rest, and armed. They came\nup slowly, watching, and stopped at the edge of the circle. One of them turned to Lily and rattled off\na stream of thick speech full of <em>kh</em> and <em>th</em> sounds. It sounded rather like Dwarvish, though it had\nnothing to do with Dwarvish at all. He looked at her as though expecting an answer.</p>\n<p>Another of the young men went towards Makheneb's horse. The great bow stuck out of its case on\nStrawberry's back, and it was huge. He made a grasping motion at it and then a throwing one, and spoke\nurgently. Makheneb made it plain that it would be a bad idea to touch it. The young man seemed to agree\nwith him about something. Then he reached for the bow anyway and pulled it from its case.</p>\n<p>Makheneb took it back out of his hands with an Elf's strength, as one takes something from a child.\nThe young man did not look angry. He looked intent and troubled. The second of the three came forward\nand raised a dagger, and pointed, and his meaning was clear: <em>give it to him.</em></p>\n<p>Makheneb nocked an arrow and waited for them to make the first move. They were young, late in their\nteens or not much older. He began to back away. \"All right,\" he said. \"It's probably time to go.\" The\nCompany still meant to leave in peace.</p>\n<p>The goblet Már had offered lay where she had left it, beside the broken instrument. One of the young\nmen went over and picked it up and said something to the others, and made the same throwing gesture,\nand put the goblet in a bag at his side.</p>\n<p>Marigold thought it would look better if only one of them gathered up the weapons, rather than all of\nthem snatching up arms at once, so she took a bag and began to put the weapons into it. The young man\nwith the goblet nodded as though she was doing the right thing. When the bag was full he helped her\nlift it, and the two of them carried it between them. Then he began to walk towards the roots, and to\ntug her along with him.</p>\n<p>Lily watched how they handled the weapons. They cared about them, but not as weapons; nobody was trying\nto wield anything. It was the way you go through the house of someone old who has collected all kinds\nof junk, and it is all going out on the rubbish heap. \"I don't think they're robbing us,\" she said. \"I\nthink they think they're doing us a favour, getting rid of these.\"</p>\n<p>Perry could only think that they were being disarmed, and about to be sent back out into the wild with\nnothing. They had met a bear on the road, and evil wolves in the hills. Without their weapons they would\nnot last.</p>\n<p>Somebody looked up into the branches and counted at least three more archers up there, and another\nfigure near the way into the roots.</p>\n<p>Marigold dug in her heels, and there was a small tug-of-war over the bag. He was much stronger than she\nwas, and she stumbled forward. Lily put on a great show of tears and heartbreak and desperation. The\nyoung man holding the bag looked at her and said something to the other two, and they said something\nsharp back, and there was a short hard exchange among them. Then he dropped the bag and spat on it.\nThe other two spat at the Company's feet and pointed away, and their meaning was plain: <em>get out, now.</em></p>\n<p>Lily nodded to them and thanked them, as though that were how people said goodbye here. Then the Company\ntook the bag and their horses and went, quickly, into the woods.</p>\n<h2>What had happened</h2>\n<p>When they were far enough away to feel it was behind them, they tried to make sense of it.</p>\n<p>They had wanted the Company to go in, somebody thought. They had wanted to be rid of the weapons.\nPerhaps they thought the Company was a corrupting influence. They had gone straight for the bow; and the\nbow, as far as anyone but Makheneb knew, was only a bow. None of them had ever heard of such people.\nMarigold had had no idea that anyone like them lived so close to the Shire, and their language meant\nnothing to her.</p>\n<p>Should they sneak back, unarmed? Get a sample of their writing, if they had any, and show it to Tom? They\nknew where these people were now. And the long-folk had never troubled the Shire.</p>\n<p>Somebody tried to feel the place for any taint of Shadow. There was something there that was deeply\nwrong. Perhaps the people were corrupted in some way, and their long bodies were a sign of it. Perhaps it\nwas nothing to do with them. The tree was strange and bizarre, but not necessarily evil. It was something\nelse, and not a quality of the people or of the tree. It was not necessarily evil. But it was wrong.</p>\n<p>They marked the place on their maps. The Tree of Sorrow could wait. It was getting dark, and nobody\nwanted to be there after dark. The Shire was only three or four hours away.</p>\n<h2>Trappers</h2>\n<p>They headed south from the tree. Már was watching behind them in case the long-folk were following, and\nbecause she was looking backward she did not see the snare until she had walked into it. It was set\nalready, a bent sapling and a noose, and she touched it and it went off. She caught it just in time and\nthrew herself clear. It gave her a great fright. It could have been the end of her. And where there was\none there might be more.</p>\n<p>Voices came from up ahead. \"I think we've got him, Fosco!\"</p>\n<p>Two hobbits came charging up and looked eagerly at the trap, and found it empty. They looked about in a\npanic. One carried a stave upright and the other a small bow, and they saw Már.</p>\n<p>\"You've been stealing from our traps!\"</p>\n<p>\"No, I have not been stealing from your traps,\" said Már. \"This is the first one I've seen, and I only\njust missed it. We'd like to avoid the rest of them, if you'd help us.\"</p>\n<p>They were utterly unconvinced. \"Do you know how much small game I've lost to your thieving?\"</p>\n<p>And Marigold knew them. The older was Fosco North-Took, a second cousin of hers once removed, and the\nyounger was his boy Rollo, who had been half his present height the last time she saw him.</p>\n<p>\"Marigold Cotton?\"</p>\n<p>\"Oh, it's so nice to see you!\"</p>\n<p>\"What are you doing, aiding and abetting this criminal?\"</p>\n<p>\"Come on, let's go and eat!\" said Marigold. \"It's so nice to see you!\" And she herded them all along\nbefore anyone could think. The North-Tooks were deeply confused. A Dwarf had been robbing their traps,\nand now here were Men and an Elf and Marigold coming out of the woods, and while they were still trying\nto take it all in she had them walking home. \"Well,\" said Fosco, \"I suppose we can make you some tea.\"</p>\n<h2>Long Cleeve</h2>\n<p>Long Cleeve is a little village on the edge of the Northfarthing, a handful of houses on either side of\nthe Water where it runs down out of the Moors. It is the home of the North-Tooks, a lesser branch of that\ngreat clan, who claim to be descended from Bandobras Took, the Bullroarer himself. Their cousins further\nsouth dispute it, but the dispute is kept friendly by shared family reunions, with drinking contests and\ngames of conkers and a golf tournament.</p>\n<p>It was an hour or so to the house. A shallow stone wall ran round the house itself, though the land went\non well beyond it. There were neat gardens of flowers and vegetables, and wilder ones further out, and a\nstack of chopped wood by a grove of trees, and chickens scratching in one corner.</p>\n<p>On the way, the rest of the Company had been introduced, very briefly and not very well. Fosco repeated\ntheir names back uncertainly. \"Marigold, and, er, Perry, and Lilian.\" He scowled at the Dwarf and the\nElf. \"And Mari. And Mackie.\"</p>\n<p>\"Peony!\" he called out. \"You'll never guess who I found at our traps!\"</p>\n<p>They had to duck to get in at the door. It was built to hobbit proportions, but there was room enough\nonce they sat down, and the Bree-folk were used to it; there are plenty of hobbits in Bree. After the\nday they had had, it was a comfort to be in a house.</p>\n<p>Peony's hair was more white than fair now, all bunched up on her head. She was delighted to see\nMarigold, whom she had last seen at a family reunion some fifteen years ago, and there was panic in her\neyes too. \"I'll be right back,\" she said, and bustled off to the pantry, and took Rollo with her.</p>\n<p>Fosco looked round at them. \"So what are you all, if you're not thieves?\"</p>\n<p>\"We're friends,\" said Lily. \"We do what Marigold tells us to.\"</p>\n<p>\"Well, if you're listening to Marigold, you can't be all bad.\"</p>\n<p>\"These are the friends who've been keeping me safe on my great adventures,\" said Marigold.</p>\n<p>Fosco supposed that her adventures had taken her north into the Moors, and that was enough to prick his\ninterest. Then she told him just how far into the Moors they had been, further than he ever went\ntrapping, and that impressed him. Then she told him how far east she had gone, into the Old Forest, and\nhis eyes grew as big as saucers. \"For mushrooms,\" she said.</p>\n<p>Már was annoyed with him and held her tongue, and nodded along. Rollo came back and gave her a plate\nfirst: thick slices of bread with butter and jam, and half a pear with the seeds cut out. \"I'll be back\nwith yours in just a moment,\" he told the others.</p>\n<p>Then Fosco turned to Már and asked what she had done. She had done nothing much in that battle with the\nwolves, she said, but in the ruins there had been an undead thing, and she had been able to deal with it.\nShe told it plainly, with a little embellishment and a little suspense, but not boastfully: the dark\ncave, the lanterns and torches they could not keep alight, the dead thing moving somewhere out in the\ndark, and then her axe, and no more dead thing.</p>\n<p>Fosco sat with his mouth open. He did not believe a word of it, but she told it so matter-of-factly that\nhis mind was racing. \"So you mean,\" he said at last, \"when they buried him, he wasn't actually gone?\"</p>\n<p>\"Imagine you had a dead animal in your trap,\" said Már, \"and the ghost of it sitting right next to it.\nOne is dead. The other is the undead part of it. That's what it was like. There was the man, clearly\ndead, and his undead counterpart there too.\"</p>\n<p>\"Little old Marigold,\" said Fosco, \"gone so far, and in such company.\"</p>\n<h2>Peony</h2>\n<p>When the rest of them had been served, Peony sat down with them. She thought they were utterly full of\nit, but she was far too polite to say so. She had married into the North-Tooks; Marigold had married out\nof them. Peony thought, a little, that anyone who came up to the Northfarthing felt they had to brag and\nbluster, and it was really not necessary.</p>\n<p>She turned to Perry and Lily. \"Are you from Bree? What brings you into the Shire?\"</p>\n<p>\"We're accompanying Marigold on her travels,\" said Perry. \"We're here to guard her while she goes about\nfinding mushroom patches. That's our main purpose. You could call it a quest.\"</p>\n<p>\"That's so sweet, that she's made friends with you.\"</p>\n<p>Lily could no more watch someone play hostess without helping than she could fly. She went to find Peony\nin the pantry, where she was cutting a loaf and handing things to Rollo to carry out. Peony pointed her to\nthe far end. \"Grab a bottle of brandy, would you, and let's serve that up. And anything else that looks\ngood. You're so welcome.\"</p>\n<p>While they worked, Lily chatted, and said that they had met Marigold and their paths had lined up for a\nwhile. And Peony confided in her. \"Fosco doesn't like to ask for help. But something keeps stealing the\ngame out of his traps. He loves trapping, and we haven't had a proper catch in six months. It's been\nterrible. We've had to trade for everything.\" The garden did well enough, and they kept the rabbits out\nof it; they were not destitute. \"But if you could help, that would be wonderful. Only don't ask him.\nJust say what you're going to do.\"</p>\n<p>Perry came out carrying trays with far too much food on them, and set a great heap of nuts and dried fruit\nin front of Makheneb. \"Here, Mackie. Some extra birdseed.\" Then he turned to Fosco. \"Speaking of that,\nyou said something's been stealing from your traps.\"</p>\n<p>Rabbits, said Fosco. Small deer, even. He was a tracker as well as a trapper, and he thought it was a\nperson, not a hobbit. Perhaps a Dwarf, he said, and looked at Már. Sometimes it broke the traps, but most\nof the time it left them fit to use again. It was nimble, then, and knew something about trapping.</p>\n<p>Perry asked whether his neighbours had had any trouble. \"Hamfast across the river says his traps are\nfine,\" said Fosco. \"But I think he's only saying it to upset me.\"</p>\n<p>\"How would we know your traps from Hamfast's?\"</p>\n<p>\"His traps are shoddy. He likes snares that catch the leg. I like the ropes.\"</p>\n<p>\"We're going back up that way anyway,\" said Perry. \"We'll let you know if we see anything.\"</p>\n<p>They stayed the night. Peony took Fosco aside for a word, and it was settled. The North-Tooks had decided\nthat Perry and Lily were a couple, which they were not, and gave them Rollo's room. Lily left the bed to\nPerry. There was a special mattress in a guest room for Marigold. Már was given a place in the stable,\nwhere there were a couple of ponies and some pigs, and a ladder up to a loft full of hay; she took the\nloft. Makheneb was offered the couch. Elves do sleep, whatever people think.</p>\n<h2>Morning</h2>\n<p>Perry was up very early. He felt he owed the house something, so he went round doing the chores, tidying\nthe things they had left everywhere, and churning butter for hours. Rollo came out and found the day's\nwork already done.</p>\n<p>\"Did you tidy everything?\"</p>\n<p>Perry shrugged. \"It's no trouble.\"</p>\n<p>\"That's normally my job. This is great.\" Rollo made breakfast: a heap of sausages and wonderful potatoes.\nThey raised their own pigs.</p>\n<p>The Company met in the stable to plan. Somewhere in the North Moors was the place Tom had marked on the\nmap. Tom's mark was not exact. It was like saying that a thing lay where two rivers meet. The Tree of\nSorrow was in the general area of it, though that did not make the tree the place.</p>\n<p>What would they even do when they found it? At the first ruin they had had Findemir's journal, a diagram\nof how things ought to look. Here they had nothing. Even if they found the place, how would they know what\nwas broken? Perhaps the ring of standing stones was the first step. Perhaps it was nothing. They had begun,\namong themselves, to call these places <em>banes</em>.</p>\n<p>And could the long-folk be the ones stealing Fosco's game? Perhaps. But why would they start only six\nmonths ago? They had surely been in that tree for a long time.</p>\n<p>First, the traps. They would find one of Fosco's rope traps and bait it. Fosco sent Rollo to show them\nwhere the traps were, and Rollo would stay with them for part of the day.</p>\n<p>Már caught the rabbit. It is well known that Dwarves are natural sprinters, and she watched it and saw\nwhere it was going and headed it off, and snatched it up. She had never killed a rabbit, and she did not\nmean to; she wanted it to look as though the rope had caught it. So she bound it by the legs and hung it\nin one of Fosco's rope traps, alive and struggling, where it could not get out. A live rabbit thrashing in\nthe leaves makes better bait than a dead one.</p>\n<p>Marigold and Makheneb found hiding places near the trap. Lily and Már stayed further back with Rollo, and\nPerry further still, with the horses; he knew his feet, and he did not trust them for this. Marigold was so\nwell hidden that Makheneb did not see her three feet away from him, and wondered where she had gone.</p>\n<h2>The orc</h2>\n<p>They did not have long to wait. Something came out of the undergrowth.</p>\n<p>Makheneb had seen Orcs before. He had fought them in battle. This was an Orc soldier. It wore one of their\nconical helms with a bar down over the nose, and it carried a small spear on which it leaned as though\nthe spear bore some of its weight. It looked old, and frail, and it favoured one leg. It came up to the\ntrap and took hold of the rope with the rabbit on it.</p>\n<p>In all Makheneb's experience, Orcs were the things he had fought in wars, or the servants of the\nNecromancer in Dol Guldur. He knew of no other kind.</p>\n<p>He was ready, and he loosed. The arrow struck true, and the Orc dropped where it stood with hardly a sound.\nThe rabbit went on bobbing up and down in its rope, quite unharmed. It happened so quickly and so quietly\nthat the others, chatting in low voices further off, knew nothing of it.</p>\n<p>Marigold had seen it all. She and Makheneb waited and watched for a few minutes more, and listened. There\nwas nothing, no sound and no movement. It had been alone. Marigold poked it with a stick. It rolled over a\nlittle, and they saw what it was wearing: a torn and battered cloak of the deep forest green of Mirkwood.\nIt had taken it from an Elf of the Woodland Realm. It might even have been one of the Orcs Makheneb had\nfought against.</p>\n<p>Makheneb gave the bird-whistle they all knew, and the others came up.</p>\n<p>Rollo's eyes went wide. \"What is that?\"</p>\n<p>\"A creature of the Shadow,\" said Makheneb. \"It has come a long way from the mountains to take your rabbits.\nIt looks frail enough. It must have been desperate. I've rarely seen them alone. Only when they've got\nlost.\"</p>\n<p>Rollo stood looking at everything, taking it all in. Where had the arrow gone? Into the spine. It was the\nfirst dead body of anything but an animal he had ever seen, and it took some understanding.</p>\n<p>While everyone else stood in shock, Lily quietly let the rabbit go.</p>\n<p>Makheneb took care to remove the Mirkwood cloak from the body. The rest he left untouched.</p>\n<p>For three of them, too, it was their first Orc. They had heard the stories, more of them than Rollo had,\nbut that did not make it less of a shock. The Shire was supposed to be the safest place in all\nMiddle-earth. Perry stood still for a long time. His work was to guard the gates of Bree, and this thing\nmust have passed that way. If one Orc could come so far west, past Bree and into the fields of the Shire,\nhow many more might? He felt that it was his fault, and that it was on him to do something about it.</p>\n<p>\"Who should know?\" he asked quietly.</p>\n<p>\"Not everyone,\" said Marigold. \"Not yet. But the Sheriffs, at least.\" Perry promised to carry word to the\nBounders and the Sheriffs.</p>\n<p>It was getting late. Rollo looked to Marigold, and she sent him home. He went back through the dusk alone,\ntrying to make sense of what he had seen.</p>\n<h2>Kingsworthy</h2>\n<p>The others followed the Orc's trail. Lily found it. She began where the rabbit had hung and watched the\nbrush, the bent grass, the scuffed moss. The Orc had been old and slow, and it had dragged one foot, and\nonce she saw the pattern of it the trail was as clear as day. It led east.</p>\n<p>In half an hour they came to the Norbourn, hardly more than a stream at this point. Beyond it stood an old\nstone tower: Kingsworthy, once a hunting lodge of the kings of the North. It was far older than any hobbit\nsettlement. Anything that had been made of wood, its doors and its shutters, had long since rotted away\nor been carried off. The trail led straight into it.</p>\n<p>It was dusk. There was no light in the tower and no sound. They watched it for an hour while it grew\nquite dark, and saw nothing move. None of them knew whether Orcs were stronger in the dark. Makheneb had\nhunted them by night in Mirkwood, and had never noticed.</p>\n<p>They agreed that one of them would go in with a lantern where the rest could see the light, and the others\nwould wait outside.</p>\n<p>The stench nearly knocked Lily backward. It was the smell of a barn that has not been mucked out in years:\nfilth on filth, and rot soaked into the stone. She put a cloth over her face and climbed the stairs. There\nwere bits of rotting offal from small animals, and bones, and dung. On the level above, something had been\nliving. In one corner were gathered little bits and pieces, sorted and kept: scraps of metal, a broken\nsword, and a wooden shield of Dwarvish make leaning against the wall. None of it was worth anything. It had\nonly been kept. This had been the creature's nest, and it had lived here alone, for about the six months\nFosco had spoken of.</p>\n<p>Lily shone her light from a window and gave the bird-call, and came back down. \"It was alone,\" she said.\nMakheneb was sure of it. The Orc had been an outcast, or a deserter, or perhaps an old soldier dying far\nfrom whatever war it had come out of, living off the easy pickings at the edge of the settled lands.</p>\n<p>The mood outside was heavy. For Lily and Perry and Marigold this was the first Orc they had ever seen, and\nit was all wrong. The Shire, even the wild edges of the Moors, ought to be safe, and it was not.</p>\n<h2>Back to Long Cleeve</h2>\n<p>It was late when they came back to Long Cleeve, and they did not wake the house. They made themselves beds\nin the stable, Perry below and the rest up in the loft among the hay. No one said much. Sleep came slowly.</p>\n<p>In the morning Peony and Rollo found them already at work, breakfast under way and the chores done. Peony\nasked what had happened. Marigold told her gently that the thief had been found and dealt with. It had been\ndangerous, yes. But it had been handled.</p>\n<p>Perry apologised for what Rollo had had to see. It had been a monstrous soldier, he said, a very old one,\nliving out there alone. Perhaps it had been trying to retire. It was only the one, and it was dealt with,\nand it would not happen again. If anything else turned up, they should let the Company know.</p>\n<p>Fosco had warmed to them now that the trouble was over, though he could not quite bring himself to say so.\nRollo said it for him: he was really very sorry about earlier.</p>\n<p>Then the Company talked about what came next. They had come to look into one of the places on Tom's map,\nthe rumoured site of a bane. It might be the Tree of Sorrow, and it might not, but it was near. After that,\nthey might well find they were out of their depth, and need to seek counsel from someone who knew far more\nthan they did. Tom was a great fellow, but he was no good at explaining things. The Grey Havens were not so\nfar off. There were the Dwarves in the Blue Mountains, too. Or they could go back to Bree and look for a\nRanger. Rivendell was too far.</p>\n<p>For now they would stay close. They would leave the horses at Long Cleeve and go on foot, travelling light.\nThe Tree of Sorrow was half a day's journey north. They would go back to it once more, and try to understand\nwhat waited beneath its roots.</p>", "played": "2025-06-17", "slug": "04-the-north-moors", "title": "The North Moors"}, {"date": "Early Summer, 2965", "html": "<h2>Gifts</h2>\n<p>They stayed an extra day at Long Cleeve, and they spent it making ready.</p>\n<p>The long-folk had been afraid of their weapons, and whatever the Company did, it could not go\nback to the tree armed. So they would split. Már and Makheneb would stay out of sight with the\nweapons, and the other three would go in empty-handed and try again to talk. Makheneb was not\nthe right person to carry a gift into that tree, he said, but he would help make one.</p>\n<p>Marigold made the gift. The long-folk child had thrown down a pipe to them, and Már had let it\nbreak; so Marigold carved a new one, a little recorder, and she did not try to make a masterpiece\nof it. She made a pair of whistles as well, one for the three who went in and one for the two who\nstayed out. If anything went wrong, whoever was in trouble would blow. They agreed on a place to\nfall back to if they were separated, and that everyone would be back by nightfall. If they were\nkept later than that, it might only be that they had been asked to dinner.</p>\n<p>Marigold still had her drawing of the white beast under the floor. They talked about showing it to\nthe long-folk. Not yet, they decided. It would keep in her pocket until the long-folk did not hate\nthem.</p>\n<p>Perry left his long axe and his bow with the others, and his buckler and helm with his horse. He\nkept on the steel breastplate from the ruins. Lily left her spear and her short sword. They set out\non foot and found how much they had come to rely on horses; walking was slower, and if they meant\nto be in and out by nightfall there was not much time to spare.</p>\n<p>Makheneb found them a place to wait, close enough to the tree to hear a whistle.</p>\n<h2>The recorder</h2>\n<p>Marigold went up to the tree playing the recorder. She tried to play the bear song on it. She had\nmade it only the day before, and like many children's toys it did not have all the notes, so what\ncame out was an approximation, with some of the fingering wrong. It did not sound bad. Perry and\nLily walked beside her in the open, as openly as they could, clapping along.</p>\n<p>About a third of the way up the trunk there was a burl that served as a ledge. People began to come\nout onto it, and out of the branches and the trunk, adults and children, eight or nine that they\ncould see on three or four limbs, all looking down with great curiosity. There were no weapons that\nthey could see. The song seemed to be going well.</p>\n<p>After about five minutes a tall young woman came out from among the roots, where the young men had\ncome from before. Two or three of the young men followed her at fifteen paces, ready to act if\nneeded but otherwise only escorting her. One of them was the young man who had played tug-of-war\nwith Marigold over the bag. The woman spoke to them fluently, questioningly, and waited for an\nanswer.</p>\n<p>Marigold did not know her language. She held out the recorder in both hands with her head bowed,\nand then pointed to the children.</p>\n<p>The young woman's brown hair was in tight ringlets bunched up on her head, and her skin, like all\nthe others', was dark and thick and leathery, like the skin of someone very old who has been out in\nthe sun all their life. She was plainly hardly more than a girl. She took the recorder, put it to\nher lips, and looked at Marigold. When Marigold nodded, she tried a few notes. There was clapping\nfrom above. She said something more, and beckoned, and led them in, playing a note or two as she\nwent. The young men sat down at the entrance as the three passed, and then rose and followed.</p>\n<h2>Inside the tree</h2>\n<p>The inside of the tree was a township. The tree might have been many trees, or else part of its root\nsystem had grown up above the ground, but either way the long-folk had built their whole town in it\nand around it. A stair of roots curled up and around inside it, and the roots were shaped for use.\nPerhaps the roots had moved to suit the people, or perhaps the people had bent them slowly as they\ngrew. It took the three of them up and out through a hole to the outside of the tree at the back,\nalready fifty feet above the ground, and on up.</p>\n<p>It was dizzying. Young men and women, and grown folk too, went scampering along the branches like\nsquirrels, and leapt from one branch to another across gaps where a miss would drop them a couple\nof hundred feet. Nobody was running from the visitors. This was only everyday life. An old one, in\ntheir sixties perhaps, was climbing slowly up a ladder of vines.</p>\n<p>The young woman brought them into a chamber like the hollow a bird or a squirrel makes in a tree,\nonly twenty feet across and thirty high. There were mats on the floor. It was a room for receiving\nguests, or perhaps a throne room. Half a dozen people were gathered there, and in the middle an old\nand wizened woman sat cross-legged on the ground. The young woman handed her the recorder. The old\nwoman beckoned the three of them forward, and began to ask them questions.</p>\n<p>They had no words in common. Everything that followed was done by gesture and mime, by music, and\nby slow and careful signs.</p>\n<p>Lily began. She made a little curtsy, of the kind anyone who has worked at an inn knows how to\nmake, and pointed to each of them in turn: \"Lily. Perry. Marigold.\" The young woman followed the\nnaming. She pointed to herself. \"Fornbok.\" And back to Lily: \"Lily.\" Then Lily told them, with a\ngreat deal of gesture and a great deal of expression on her face, that their home was very\nbeautiful. Whatever else was not understood, that was.</p>\n<h2>Safe</h2>\n<p>Marigold wanted to tell them why they had come. They were travelling, and searching, and what they\nwere searching for was a great thing, to make sure it was safe. She settled on the heart of it:\n<em>safe.</em> That was what they were trying to do. They were looking to make things safe, to make them\ngood.</p>\n<p>Fornbok reached for a switch that lay near her, a long thin whippy stick with a few leaves still on\nit, and tapped Perry lightly on the chest where the breastplate was.</p>\n<p>\"Safe,\" she said.</p>\n<p>It was the only word she spoke in their tongue.</p>\n<p>Perry understood. If he wanted them to feel safe, he would have to show it. He took one for the\nteam. He unbuckled the steel breastplate and set it aside.</p>\n<p>Fornbok nodded, and said it again, more gently. The room had changed towards them.</p>\n<p>Lily took up the next part: <em>search.</em> She acted out searching as though she were playing it for a\nroom full of children, all hands and exaggeration, searching and searching, and then <em>safe</em>, and\nthen running into danger. Fornbok watched her thoughtfully. Then she gave a whistle so sharp that\ntheir ears rang, and a child came and stood at her side. It was perhaps six or seven years old, and\nit already stood past Marigold's shoulder. It waited for instruction.</p>\n<p>Then Perry played the beast. He crouched and loomed and was enormous and terrible. Marigold and Lily\nput him to sleep and covered him over, and they all had a quiet little celebration over his sleeping\nbody. It was the best acting Perry had ever done. He bowed at the end. Then he said, earnestly and\nslowly, \"Safe. We are not here to hurt anybody. We're only looking for information, to make sure\nthat you're all safe, and everybody else is too.\"</p>\n<p>Two of the young men who had followed them up began talking excitedly to each other. Then they did a\nmime of their own. One crouched low. The other came up behind him, and the first leapt and clutched\nhim about the shoulders and stabbed his fingers into the other's shoulder like teeth. The one who had\nbeen bitten took his little gourd and poured it out on the ground, and lay down on the ground himself.</p>\n<p>They knew something the Company did not.</p>\n<p>The old woman nodded and said a few words, and pointed to the child, who came and took Marigold's\nhand and began to lead her out. They were going to be shown round.</p>\n<p>Marigold showed them the map first, and pointed from it to the drawing, trying to ask where. It meant\nnothing to them. Lily thought they might never have seen a map before.</p>\n<h2>The pit</h2>\n<p>The child led them down the way they had come and further down still, through a labyrinth of passages\namong the roots, down past the entrance where they had come in and on under the tree. It was very dark\ndown there. Here and there something grew on the roots that gave off a faint light, and the passages\nglowed dimly. It was two or three in the afternoon, and there were a few hours left.</p>\n<p>The two young men who had done the mime came with them. One of them was carrying Perry's breastplate.\nWhere the passage split, the child pulled them to the right, and where it split again, to the right\nagain. Then the young man with the breastplate stepped forward and threw it.</p>\n<p>There was a pit there, and it was full of weapons and armour and metal things, old and broken, a great\nheap of them. The breastplate landed on top.</p>\n<p>Perry lunged to catch it, missed, and went into the pit after it. He landed among a great deal of metal,\nnone of it comfortable. He was not badly hurt and he did not get skewered. He put the breastplate on so\nas to have both hands free to climb out. As he came up, one of the young men put a foot to his shoulder\nand pushed him gently back.</p>\n<p>\"Sorry,\" said Perry. \"I know that wasn't graceful.\" He tried again, and was pushed back again, and at\nlast he understood. He stood in the pit for a while. \"So what are we here for? When can I leave?\"\nThere was no food down there, but he had his Ranger rations. He could last a few days.</p>\n<p>In the end he took the breastplate off and left it. He had only had it three days, though he had given\nup his old corslet for it, and it weighed as much as half of everything else he carried together. He\ntried to throw it back up out of the pit, and could not; it was too heavy.</p>\n<p>There was so much metal down there, and so much of it old and ill-used, that there might have been\nsomething valuable in it if anyone had the time to dig. Anything of leather had rotted. Marigold looked\nat it all and at the hand of the child who had led her, which was tough in the way their skin was tough,\nthough not calloused. Everything in the pit was metal, weapons or armour or smith's work of some kind.\nThe long-folk's own weapons were simple things, sticks and staves made from what grew here, with nothing\nof the forge about them. This was a whole class of things that was forbidden to them. It was not being\nkept here. It was thrown away. The long-folk had been confiscating such things for generations and\ndumping them here without ceremony.</p>\n<p>Perry climbed out without the breastplate, and they went on.</p>\n<h2>The well</h2>\n<p>The passage ended in a small chamber with a well in it. Great jars of clay stood about, like the old wine\njars of the south. The well was only a ring of roots about half a foot high, with the water just below\nthe rim, so that you could dip from it. The young men drew a ladle from one of the jars, which had a lid\nthat came off easily, took a sip themselves, and offered it round. They did not press.</p>\n<p>Perry had just climbed in and out of a pit and was thirsty. He looked into the well first, in case there\nwas anything under the water. He had seen a great beast under a floor, and he was suspicious of the ground\nnow. There was nothing. He drank, and the others drank after him, Marigold only after smelling it.</p>\n<p>It was very bitter, with a sharp earthy taste, something like pine sap. Once it was down, their hearts\nbegan to race, as though they had drunk something to keep them awake for a week.</p>\n<h2>The grotto</h2>\n<p>Back at the place where the passages first forked, the young men sent the child away. Then they pointed\ndown the other passage, where a small cold draught came up to meet them, and said something. They would\nnot go down it, and they had not wanted to talk about it where the child could hear. The tour was over.\nThey were ready to go back up as soon as the Company was.</p>\n<p>The Company went down the other passage. One of the young men caught Perry by the arm, not firmly\nenough to hold him, only to ask him not to.</p>\n<p>\"Safe,\" said Perry, and patted his hand, and went on.</p>\n<p>He walked into the dark as if he owned it, and it heartened the others. It was like going through a\nfrightening part of a strange town with a local who strolls along saying <em>this way; it's fine</em>.</p>\n<p>It was not fine. The place pressed on Lily and on Perry. It was unnatural to live in a tree to begin\nwith, and to live under the ground, and down here there was both. Marigold felt none of it. There are\nplenty of hobbit-holes that are nothing but tunnels into a hill. This one wanted airing and lighting and\nfurnishing, but it did not feel wicked to her.</p>\n<p>The glow of the roots was fading. Perry lit his lantern. The path sloped down, and the roots arched\noverhead and reached forward along it like fingers. Then there was a sudden lip, a drop of a few feet,\nand several small steps down, and at the bottom of them the water came right to the last step. It was a\npool, forty or fifty feet across, and the roots of the tree had made a grotto about it.</p>\n<p>Lily looked about from where she stood. Across the pool and a little to the right, the roots bulged out\nand made a pocket, and in it she could just see a crevice, and a nest or a space of some kind.</p>\n<p>Marigold went round the edge of the pool to see. It was strange, but not terrifying, and there were no\ngiant white beasts under the water. She came to the place Lily had seen, where the roots bent outward.\nIn it there was a woman.</p>\n<p>Marigold saw her face in profile. She was standing, leaning a little backward, with her feet planted and\nher eyes closed. She might have been dead. She was bald. She was not as tall as the long-folk and did not\nhave their look; the nearest thing Marigold could think of was an Elf, but she did not truly look like\nanything Marigold had ever seen. Things grew out of the roots, or perhaps they were the roots, and they\ncradled her body. There was a wrapping about her, and Marigold could not see her arms and legs.</p>\n<p>She came back round the pool, carefully, and told the others. She thought the woman was asleep. None of\nthem had seen anything else down there.</p>\n<h2>Már and Makheneb</h2>\n<p>Outside, Már and Makheneb had not been idle.</p>\n<p>As soon as the others had gone, Már bundled all the weapons together at the foot of a tree and covered\nthem with leaves. The two of them could have carried everything between them, but not easily, and not\nif they had to move quickly. Then they hid. Makheneb found a place where he could see the pile. Már\nstayed on the ground. That was the long-folk's country up in the trees; they were squirrel people.</p>\n<p>The long-folk came, four of them, stealing through the forest to take the two unawares, and not doing it\nwell. Makheneb and Már both saw them coming, looked at each other, and kept still. One of them passed so\nclose to Makheneb that he was two feet away, looking straight past him. Then one of them noticed the\nheap of leaves and began to brush them off, and found the weapons.</p>\n<p>They were armed with staves, sharpened sticks. They were not threatening anyone. One picked up Perry's\nbuckler and looked at it and tucked it under his arm, and reached for more.</p>\n<p>\"That's our stuff,\" said Már. And she went.</p>\n<p>She is a Dwarf, and Dwarves can sprint. She came out of her hiding place running hard and bellowing,\n<em>No, no, no!</em>, and pulled up short about an arm's length from them. They could not understand a word\nof it, but they understood very well that something was happening that she cared about a great deal.\nAt the same moment an arrow thudded into the ground a foot in front of the one holding the buckler.\nNobody saw where it had come from. He dropped the buckler.</p>\n<p>For a moment no one moved.</p>\n<p>\"Mine,\" said Már, and pointed to the weapons, and to herself. She did not reach for her axe. <em>This is\nours. It isn't yours. We don't want to fight.</em> It was the kind of lesson you teach to small children.</p>\n<p>They put back what they had picked up. They stepped away, and then they stood, two on one side of the\npile and two on the other, facing her. They did not threaten her. But they were standing guard over it,\nand one of them, looking about, spotted Makheneb, pointed straight at him, and called out, and pointed\nat the ground.</p>\n<p>Makheneb stayed where he was, a hundred or two hundred feet behind Már, with his bow ready. Már stepped\ntowards the pile. The two nearest stepped between her and it. They did not touch it, but they would not\nlet her touch it either. One of them began to take a step forward, and Makheneb put an arrow into the\nground exactly where his foot was about to land. The foot went back.</p>\n<p>So they waited, in a standoff.</p>\n<h2>Goodbyes</h2>\n<p>Up in the tree the three said their goodbyes to Fornbok and the old woman and all the rest, and everyone\nlooked at them with a mixture of awe and concern. With a great deal of patience, and gestures they had\nlearned that day, the long-folk tried to tell them something, and they understood more of it than they\nwould have thought possible. It had been a couple of generations at least, the long-folk said, since <em>she</em>\nhad last woken. And when she woke they had put her back to sleep, at great cost to themselves.</p>\n<p>Lily told them, as best she could, that she was sorry that had happened, and glad the woman was asleep.\nThen they left.</p>\n<p>They came out confidently, waving and happy, and found Már standing over the pile of weapons with four\nlong-folk on guard and Makheneb out of sight behind her with an arrow on the string.</p>\n<p>Már did not call out to them; she did not want the long-folk to think she was signalling an attack. She\nmade the gesture for <em>weapons down</em>, and tried to persuade the guards. It did not work.</p>\n<p>Perry, who had come bursting into the clearing in the middle of telling the story of their afternoon,\nabout a lady named Fornbok and all the bald people and how great they all were, stopped dead. \"I can't\nlose any more of my stuff,\" he said.</p>\n<p>So Lily sang. She sang the bear song, loudly, and as she sang she used the gestures and the signs she had\nseen the tree-folk use over and over while they talked to her, and Marigold joined in behind her. The guards\ndid not understand all of it, but they understood that these strangers had come from the tree, and that\nthey were speaking in ways the tree-folk spoke. They backed off. They went and stood between the Company\nand the Tree of Sorrow, and gave them room.</p>\n<p>The Company picked up their gear and went the other way.</p>\n<h2>The vampire, perhaps</h2>\n<p>When they were well away, they tried to make sense of it all.</p>\n<p>Had they found the bane? A lady, asleep in a nest inside a tree. They had not seen any fangs. But the young\nmen's mime had been of something that leapt on your back and bit. Nobody knew whether the long-folk had a\nword for <em>vampire</em>.</p>\n<p>Was she frozen, like the man in the ruins? No, said Marigold. Only asleep. There had been no glass wall in\nfront of her, nothing like the floor that held the white beast. At the first place they had been checking\nlocks. Here there was no lock. There were only the long-folk, who kept her asleep, at great cost, and who\nsaid she woke every couple of generations. That did not seem to be now. They would make a note of it. Tom\nshould know.</p>\n<p>Was she contained? They did not really know whether the white beast was, either. The first place had had\nmore than one defence: the doors, the wheels, the floor. Here there were the tree-folk. What more could\nthey do? Not wake her, certainly.</p>\n<p>They noticed one more thing. In the pit, the things that had kept their shape were the metal ones. Anything\nof wood or leather had rotted.</p>\n<p>Most of all they needed someone who knew more. Lily still thought the Grey Havens were some kind of home for\nElves who had fallen on hard times, and so, she said, they should go there, and perhaps somebody there could\ntalk to the tree-folk. They would at least look like Elves.</p>\n<p>It was a long walk back. The moon was high when they came to Long Cleeve, a few hours later than they had\nmeant to, though for the last hours they were on hobbit roads in country they knew, and not in the middle\nof a forest with who knew what in it. They slept in the stable again.</p>\n<h2>Frogmorton</h2>\n<p>In the morning they decided to go to the Grey Havens, and from there, perhaps, up to the Blue Mountains.\nNobody was sure they could simply turn up at the Grey Havens. It was not only for Elves, Makheneb thought,\nbut he did not know the customs for those who were not Elves; he was not even entirely sure of them for\nhimself. He had met fewer than five Elves of Lindon in his life. Those who went to the Grey Havens did not\ncome back to tell about it.</p>\n<p>Before they went west, they wanted to see Tom. They had called on him once from Barad Tarminalë and he had\nbeen slow to come. This time they would go towards him. They called, and they set off across the Shire\ntowards the Old Forest.</p>\n<p>It was a notable company to be passing along the roads of the Shire, and hobbits sitting on their porches\nor by the fruit stalls were forever flagging them down to hear their stories. The Company was polite, but\nit had no wish to spend half a day at every stop explaining itself, and by now it had taken on a certain\nfrostiness, which was the only way to get past.</p>\n<p>In Frogmorton a voice called out, \"Mum!\" It was Marigold's daughter, Flora.</p>\n<p>They embraced, and Flora was delighted to see her and wanted to hear all about her adventures. Then she put\nher hand on her mother's. \"I have the best news,\" she said. \"You're going to be a grandmother.\" It would be\nfive or six months yet, she thought.</p>\n<p>\"Then you'll have to come with me,\" said Marigold. \"I can't bear to be away from my grandchild.\"</p>\n<p>Lily was thrilled, and joined in, and promised shoulder rubs all the way. Marigold told Flora everything:\nthe wolves and the bald lady and the mushrooms and the bear song and the tree, all tumbling out at once. Flora\nlistened with a kind of stunned look, trying to take it all in. Her husband Robin was listening too. Marigold\nhad always been well disposed to Robin. By two-thirds of the way through the story he was shuddering, and\nby the end, when it was clear she meant to take Flora with her into the Old Forest, he had heard enough.</p>\n<p>With Robin's encouragement, Flora told her mother that they would gladly be there for her when she came back\nfrom the Old Forest, and that she must come back to Frogmorton again soon, and stay with them for dinner, and\ncome back and stay when the baby was near. Stay <em>with them</em>, they meant. But they would not be travelling.</p>\n<p>Marigold was heartbroken. The others said, in her support, that everything she had told them was true, and\nRobin shuddered again. Then the Company sang them <em>Ghostcleavers</em>. Perhaps the words would make them think\nbetter of the whole business. They sang well. Flora and Robin said that it was certainly not for them, but\nthey could appreciate the feats of Már the Dwarf, which was more a Dwarf's sort of thing anyway, and they were\nglad Marigold had someone as strong and capable as Már with her. And Lily and Perry seemed to have their heads\non the right way round. She would probably be all right.</p>\n<h2>Tom on the bridge</h2>\n<p>When they came to the Brandywine Bridge, Tom was sitting on the edge of it, kicking his feet and smoking his\npipe.</p>\n<p>\"I thought you'd all be coming this way!\"</p>\n<p>\"Tom, we've got news,\" said Marigold. \"One really important thing and one more mildly important. The really\nimportant thing: my daughter's pregnant. She's going to have a baby!\"</p>\n<p>\"That's delightful!\"</p>\n<p>The mildly important thing was the Tree of Sorrow, north of Hobbiton. They told him about the long-folk.\nThe matriarch's name, Perry said, as near as he could make it out, was Lingdadh.</p>\n<p>\"They've been here since before the Elves, I think,\" said Tom. \"They're still here? They used to be all over\nthe place. I don't know where most of them went. They seem to have misplaced themselves.\"</p>\n<p>There had been two near confrontations with them, they told him, but no blood drawn, and in the end they had\nbeen invited in. And in the roots, where they had suspected a bane might be, they had found a sleeping woman\nwho looked something like an Elf. They had not seen any fangs. She was bald. Was she perhaps an old\nacquaintance of his?</p>\n<p>Tom looked at them with concern. \"Well,\" he said. \"Yes, this does seem concerning. It's not familiar to me.\nBut it sounds as if the tree-folk have it in hand. The Elves may know more of it, especially the Elves of the\nWest.\"</p>\n<p>They were going to the Grey Havens, they told him. They had heard there was some sort of help given to Elves\nthere, and perhaps there would be old Elves they could talk to.</p>\n<p>\"That's wonderful,\" said Tom. \"Would you take them something for me?\" He patted his shirt, and wondered where\nhe had put it. He was wearing several layers, and at last he reached into an inner pocket and brought out a\nhoneycomb, all goopy, and handed it over. \"Give this to Círdan, with my best wishes.\"</p>\n<p>\"Who is Círdan?\"</p>\n<p>\"He builds boats, over in the Grey Havens.\" Tom spelled it out for them. \"I owed him this a while back. If you\nwish to have a nibble, you're welcome, so long as some of it gets to him.\" Perry had a nibble. Then he put the\nhoneycomb in a pocket.</p>\n<p>They were thinking of the Dwarves of the Blue Mountains too. Did Tom have anything he wanted taken there?</p>\n<p>\"They haven't asked anything of me in many a century,\" said Tom. \"They like to be self-sufficient.\" But when\nthey asked for advice, he said, quite nonchalantly, \"Fenja, who is Lif, is trustworthy, and knows many deep\nthings.\"</p>\n<p>Már was aghast. Tom had just given away a Dwarf's secret name, the name no one but a Dwarf's nearest know, as\ncasually as the weather. \"We shouldn't be talking about her other name here,\" she said. \"That's her name. It's\nfor her to know.\"</p>\n<p>Tom did not understand the problem, but he understood that Már was upset, and he apologised. \"Fenja I consider\ntrustworthy,\" he said. \"I didn't mean to offend.\" Már did not know Fenja. But she knew her secret name now,\nand she was not pleased about it.</p>\n<p>Marigold asked whether Goldberry might have any more of whatever she had given them before; it would be\nwonderful for the baby. Goldberry was otherwise engaged, Tom said, but he would get some jam to them before they\nleft the Shire. And Marigold made him promise to come and see the baby when it came. \"A newborn hobbit,\" said\nTom. \"It's been a while.\"</p>\n<p>He offered to help them slip past all the prying relatives between here and the west of the Shire. They thanked\nhim and said no. It was a comfort, after the loneliness of the wild, to be among kin.</p>\n<p>Tom looked closely at Perry. \"The twins are doing well in Bree,\" he said.</p>\n<p>Perry was a good uncle. He was very close to his brother's twins, and spent a good deal of his wages on presents\nfor them and outings, and tried to make them excited about keeping watch on the wall with him, which was not\nexciting at all. It had been hard to be away from his family for so long, and harder still to be among other\npeople's families.</p>\n<p>\"That's good,\" said Perry. \"Thank you. Thank you.\"</p>\n<p>Tom saw that Strawberry was doing well, and the loan of her was renewed.</p>\n<h2>Michel Delving</h2>\n<p>They went west across the Shire to Michel Delving, the nearest thing the hobbits have to a capital, where the\nMayor keeps a fine house and where there is a museum called the Mathom-house. Marigold knew it by reputation. It\nwas famous for its cookbooks and its family trees, its collections of hobbit-lore, and its old weapons and maps.</p>\n<p>Már needed a pony. She did not want to go on sharing a horse. She had some of her treasure from the ruins left\nto trade, a ring, the hairband, and one goblet, though she meant to keep the badger necklace. At the inn, where\nthey took rooms, she asked the innkeeper whether he could point her to anyone who might sell. She wanted\nsomething strong, with stamina, that could carry her and her gear a long way for a long time. She kept it\nlow-key about what she could pay.</p>\n<p>Lily had worked at an inn all her life, and she could see at once that it was not going the way Már wanted. The\ninnkeeper was humouring her, doing the least he could to tick the box. Lily stepped in beside Már and put in a\nword or two, and got a little further. Most people had their ponies and liked them, the innkeeper said, and\nthough some were for sale, the sellers did not much like the sound of ponies leaving town for who knew where.\nBut he gave them a couple of names.</p>\n<p>The pony they found was not the glorious beast Már had pictured, but it was not old or sickly either. It was a\nyoung chestnut with a lighter mane, lively but not wild, and its name was Charlie. Már kept the name.</p>\n<p>At the Mathom-house they looked for maps. The best of them covered a great sweep of country south of the Shire,\nfrom the South Downs down along the Greyflood almost to Lond Daer where it met the sea, with Swanfleet and\nTharbad marked. The towns on it were few; it was not about towns. It was about flowers. There were several\nversions of it, and they realised it was a series made over time. Someone had tracked six rare kinds of flower\nacross a hundred years, how common they were, and how their ground had changed.</p>\n<p>The curator apologised to them. Some young hooligans had lately made off with several items from the\nMathom-house, and they were looking for the culprits. People were usually much better behaved.</p>\n<p>The Company thanked her. They would be going down that way at some point, they said, and the map had been very\nhelpful.</p>", "played": "2025-07-01", "slug": "05-beneath-the-tree-of-sorrow", "title": "Beneath the Tree of Sorrow"}, {"date": "Early Summer, 2965", "html": "<h2>The Mathom-house</h2>\n<p>They lingered a day in Michel Delving before going west. Már and Lily went off about the town on\nbusiness of their own, and the other three spent the day as they pleased.</p>\n<p>Hobbit hospitality, Makheneb found, meant food, and then more food. It was the kind of food that\ndoes not make you want to walk ten miles afterwards, and does make you want a nap. The hobbits were\nuncomfortable with an Elf, and being uncomfortable made them overly polite, and being overly polite\nmade them serve him. With the Men of Bree they were more at ease, and a little more distrustful.\nThey knew Bree. Still, the Company was with Marigold, and that helped.</p>\n<p>The Mathom-house is something like a jumble sale, if all the things were never sold, and were kept\nall year round in a building of their own. Nothing in it was for sale. It was all heirlooms and\nthings of note, and <em>of note</em> was very generously understood. This was important because it had\nbeen someone's grandfather's; that was important because it was the only left-handed toaster. It\nwas not all rubbish. There were hand-made watches, and old paintings, and a great deal of furniture.\nThere was very little that was truly valuable, but a great deal that mattered to someone. There was\na replica of the original golf club. The original had been borrowed, sadly, and that was why the\nrules about borrowing were so strict now.</p>\n<p>Perry spent hours copying the flower map. It was kept by Malva Slowfoot, who called herself the\ncurator, though there was no sign that anything had ever been turned away. The Mathom-house was her\nlittle fiefdom. She was very glad that he was copying the map and not asking to borrow it, because a\ngreat many people came asking to borrow things, and she did not like it. <em>This is not a library. It\nis a Mathom-house.</em> It also helped that Firework, the guard dog, a tiny white terrier whose bark was\nvery much worse than its bite, had taken a shine to Perry. It lay curled up asleep on his feet while\nhe worked. Perry loves dogs.</p>\n<p>He had been at it a couple of hours, and was well into it, when he noticed a hobbit child standing\nright at his elbow.</p>\n<p>\"Oh, dear. What's your name?\"</p>\n<p>\"Primula. Are you an artist?\"</p>\n<p>\"Sometimes.\"</p>\n<p>\"Are you done with that? I need to borrow it.\"</p>\n<p>\"You need to borrow it? What for?\"</p>\n<p>\"I can't tell you.\"</p>\n<p>\"Is it secret mushroom stuff?\"</p>\n<p>\"I can't tell you.\"</p>\n<p>\"Well,\" said Perry, \"I think you could borrow this map. The copy I'm making. That'll work just as well.\"</p>\n<p>She scrunched up her face. \"I don't think Mr Bilbo will like that. But we can try.\" She said it was\nBilbo Baggins she was fetching it for. \"Don't tell Malva I'm here. It's a secret.\"</p>\n<p>\"You're very sneaky to have got past Malva,\" said Perry. \"I'll reward that. I won't tell.\"</p>\n<p>Perry is no artist. His copy was a great deal less detailed than the original. He kept the things he\ncared about, the roads and the lie of the land, so that you could tell how to get from one place to\nanother. He left out the mushrooms. There was a mushroom in the legend and not one on the map. Since\nthat was very likely the whole reason Primula and Mr Bilbo wanted it, they were going to be greatly\ndisappointed.</p>\n<p>Once Malva came by and called Firework to her, and Perry realised a moment too late that the child did\nnot want to be seen. He turned, and Primula stepped out from behind an old dresser as though nothing\nhad happened. She had dodged it. Perry made a second copy, a little better than the first, gave her\none, and she scurried off with it as soon as it was in her hand. The other he stuck to the edge of his\ngrandfather's map with a bit of tape, so that it hung off the side where there was a good deal of empty\nspace. It took about two minutes to unfold.</p>\n<h2>Apples and tanneries</h2>\n<p>Makheneb had two errands. He wanted a treat for Strawberry, and he wanted someone to refit his armour.\nIt was leather armour, and it had been made for someone else.</p>\n<p>On the edge of town there were fruit trees everywhere, on people's land and leaning out over the paths.\nBranches over the path were plainly anybody's. As for the rest, Makheneb could not see how anyone could\nown a tree. He picked apples carefully, so as not to damage anything. He hopped a fence to get at the\nbest of them, tried one, found it delicious, and took three more, since he ought to think of the other\nhorses too, and turned to go. The farmer was standing there watching him. Makheneb waved. The farmer\nwas too stunned to speak or to stop him. It was not that the Elf had been sneaky about it. He had simply\nsolved a small problem, openly. They would talk about the sheer audacity of it for generations.</p>\n<p>The armour was harder. There were no shops in the Shire, in the way there were in Bree, apart from a\nfew places like the inn. You were expected to know where things were, or whom to ask. Farms were easy\nto find; services happened in people's houses. Makheneb tried following his nose for a tannery, which\nhe felt sure he could smell from a long way off. He went out of town and back in again several times and\nfound nothing.</p>\n<p>Perry had given up his breastplate to the long-folk, and he was going about Michel Delving in a shirt,\na pair of short trousers with a great many pockets, and a pair of clogs. None of that had felt dangerous\nin the Shire. He did not know how it would go on the road, where a pointed enough bramble might take\nhim out. He too went looking for a tanner, and found none. He and Makheneb passed each other in the\nstreet three times.</p>\n<h2>Melody Took</h2>\n<p>Marigold had gone looking for books, and in particular for storybooks, and she had found a great many.\nThe Mathom-house's book-room was all narrow gaps between shelves, with books piled on the floor three\nfeet high. She had made one pile she had been through and another she was still working through.</p>\n<p>The first thing she found was a little book of a hundred pages or so, a revised and condensed collection\nof stories by a hobbit named Melody Took. They were tales of strange creatures and strange sightings: a\ntwo-headed cow, magic done by an Elf, and one in which Gandalf appeared, apparently to help someone cheat\nin a cart race. And in one of them there was a single, sideways mention of the long-folk of the North\nMoors.</p>\n<p>She kept digging, and at last she found what the little book had been made from: seven old journals in\nMelody Took's own tight hand. Melody had gone about asking not only a great many hobbits but a great many\npeople passing through for their stories, and she had written down what they told her. Some of it was\nplainly someone pulling her leg. All of it had been set down faithfully, exactly as it was told, so that\nthe quality of the tales varied wildly, but the breadth of them was astonishing. It looked like forty or\nfifty years of work. The first volume never left the Eastfarthing; Melody had been finding her feet with\nlocal lore. Then she had found a knack for it and a taste for it, and the later volumes went further\nafield, with travellers passing through and people she went to meet. She had died in her late eighties,\nand the seventh volume stopped a third of the way through.</p>\n<p>Her handwriting was hard to read. Marigold had got to the fourth volume without finding the long-folk\nstory she was sure must be in there somewhere when Malva came by.</p>\n<p>\"I'm sorry, dear. You'll need to put the books away. You can come back tomorrow. We open between first and\nsecond breakfast.\"</p>\n<p>\"Can I borrow it?\"</p>\n<p>Marigold was a hobbit, which helped. It did not help enough. \"That will be quite enough,\" said Malva. \"It\nwill be here in the morning. Please put the book down. Firework and I will walk you out.\"</p>\n<p>Marigold went out looking sadly back at the books.</p>\n<p>A little hobbit boy was waiting to one side of the door with a conspiratorial gleam in his eye. \"She kick\nyou out?\"</p>\n<p>\"She kicked me out.\"</p>\n<p>\"We've got to wait till the dog has its dinner,\" he said. \"That's how we got in last night. I'm Ammie.\"</p>\n<p>\"I'm Marigold.\"</p>\n<p>They sat on a bench by the square to wait. Marigold watched Perry and Makheneb pass each other twice more,\nlooking sadder each time, and at last she called them over.</p>\n<p>\"How are you doing, bud?\"</p>\n<p>They asked her whether she knew anywhere good to eat, and whether she knew of a tanner. Any tanner, she\ntold them, would be downstream. The river did not run through Michel Delving. The tanning was done down in\nthe Eastfarthing.</p>\n<p>\"Where's the stream? I haven't found a single stream here.\"</p>\n<p>They went to get something to eat. After dinner, Marigold said, she had plans.</p>\n<h2>The inn</h2>\n<p>When they got back to the inn, Már and Lily had a small crowd around them in one corner. Már had had more\nthan a few, and was telling stories with more enthusiasm than the others had ever seen in her. They were\nnot tales of the Company's adventures, but tales from before, things Már had seen or heard. Some of them\nwere very interesting, and some were probably complete nonsense. Now and then Lily chimed in with a story\nof her own, and Perry, eating his dinner, was surprised by them. At the gate of Bree he had watched people\ncome and go, but he had never really got to know them or hear their stories. Lily had. She had a great\nwealth of them, mostly from the east and the south, from the Men who traded up and down the roads.</p>\n<p>Makheneb sat and made arrows. He still had the Númenórean arrowheads from the ruins, and he fitted them to\nnew shafts and bound them. The innkeeper kept bringing him a fresh drink and lingering, and the third time\nhe asked whether he could try the great bow. Makheneb took him out the back. The bow was almost as tall as\nthe innkeeper, and it was like handing a bow to a small child. He could not draw it. He said he had had\na better reach when he was younger.</p>\n<p>Perry had a few drinks, since Már was having a few, and went round asking people on their own whether they\nhad any experience with a needle. He had an idea: trousers whose legs could be tied on below his short ones\nand taken off again. Nobody at the inn could help, but somebody knew who could. Salvina was a marvel at all\nkinds of needlework; she could surely manage it. She would be in bed by now, but here were the directions,\nand he should go in the morning after a pleasant breakfast and just ask politely. Perry thanked him and\nbought him a drink, since he insisted.</p>\n<p>Then Perry taught some of them a card game, and they taught him theirs, all of them trick-taking games and\nlittle variations of one another, and the evening turned into a tournament. Perry swung wildly between\nlosing badly and suddenly sweeping everything off the table. Beginner's luck, they called it, but he also\nmade choices so idiotic that they became genius. By the end he had three or four new friends. He did not\nwant anything from them. He did give them an open invitation to Bree, and he wrote a letter to his niece\nand nephew about his travels and left it with one of them, a hobbit named Posco. Posco was going to the\nEastfarthing and could get it that far, and then see that it found its way to Bree.</p>\n<p>\"I haven't seen my family in a long time,\" said Perry.</p>\n<p>\"I haven't seen mine in almost two weeks,\" said Posco.</p>\n<p>Perry asked whether he could bring Posco anything back from the west. Berries, said Posco, and mushrooms,\nif Perry had extras. They had tried growing them in the garden, but those things needed an unruly sort of\nplace and gardens were too orderly. And the children always got to the wild ones round Frogmorton before\nthe grown-ups knew they were ripe. The children there were resourceful. They were also devious. Posco had\nbeen one of them himself.</p>\n<p>Perry mentioned the child he had met at the Mathom-house, Primula, and that she had been fetching a map for\nBilbo.</p>\n<p>\"The Brandybuck girl? What's Bilbo up to now?\" Posco shook his head. Bilbo had had his adventure and come\nback wealthier than anyone, and now he put ideas in children's heads. Dragons, and Dwarves (no offence to\nthe one in the corner), and trolls and Elves and wizards. It was nothing but trouble for the young. At least\nat Posco's age you could hear those stories without feeling you had to go off into the wild.</p>\n<h2>The heist</h2>\n<p>After dark Marigold went back to the Mathom-house, and Ammie was waiting.</p>\n<p>Round the back there was a pile of boxes, precariously stacked: a big wooden crate at the bottom and a\ntool chest on top. Ammie scrambled up and jumped, and caught the edge of a small window that was mostly\nthere to let the air in. Its frame had been bent so far that it was barely attached. He pulled himself\nthrough. \"It's in here!\"</p>\n<p>Marigold followed. She could not get a grip at first. Then she found the edge of a shelf inside and began\nto drag herself through, slowly, and realised as she went that the shelf was not fixed to anything, not to\nthe wall and not to the shelves on either side. She could push on through the window and fall on her face,\nor go carefully and risk bringing the whole shelf down, or back out. She pushed on. She overshot. It was\nabout six feet down to a rough carpet, and she landed on her nose. She kept herself from crying out. Her\nnose bled, and it might be broken, and it hurt a great deal, but nobody came.</p>\n<p>Ammie came and patted her face in the dark. \"Your nose is still there.\" It was a bit bent, and he gave it\na sharp tug, and it snapped back into place. Field medicine in the hands of a hobbit child.</p>\n<p>\"Do you know what you're getting?\" he asked.</p>\n<p>\"Yes. Do you?\"</p>\n<p>He led her through the house, down narrow passages between furniture stacked almost to the ceiling, up\ncreaking stairs past the bookshelves, and up to the next floor, where there was a bust made of marble.</p>\n<p>\"That's my grandma,\" said Ammie.</p>\n<p>It was too dark to read the name on it. The two of them might just carry it between them. It would fit out\nof the window, but getting it out safely would be another matter. It could go out of the front door, if the\ndoor were open.</p>\n<p>\"We could come back tomorrow,\" said Marigold, \"and take it out the front.\"</p>\n<p>She tried lifting it. She could carry it alone, but only the way you carry something very heavy across a\nroom and then must put it down at once. It would break her toes if she dropped it. They carried it down\ntogether towards the front door, Marigold taking the weight while Ammie told her where to go. But he could\nnot make up his mind where to put it, and she had to put it down somewhere, now. So she set it a few paces\ninside the door and put an open book in front of it, so that it looked as though the bust were sitting there\nreading.</p>\n<p>By then Marigold was so nervous about the bust that she had forgotten all about the journals. They made their\nplan. Tomorrow, when the Mathom-house opened, Ammie would go in and start crying and screaming to draw\neveryone to him, and Marigold would take the bust out the front door. Then they left by the door.</p>\n<h2>Breakfast, and Salvina</h2>\n<p>In the morning the Company ate breakfast together. The cooking in the Shire is modest but plentiful and well\nseasoned: potatoes with sage and rosemary and a great deal of butter. They meant to leave at midday.</p>\n<p>Marigold felt the others out, carefully. How did they feel about rules? How did they feel about children?\nAbout security? About heavy things? They did not know what to make of it. If a crime was bad enough, Makheneb\nsaid, he usually just killed it. Perry told her what Posco had said about Bilbo sending children off on\nerrands. Marigold, who had grown up on stories of Bilbo and who wanted adventures of her own, was a little\nnervous at that. \"No,\" she said, \"Bilbo's really cool. The kids are good. The kids are good.\"</p>\n<p>Then Perry went to see Salvina. Her hobbit-hole sat in the crook of a little dip in the land, one of two or\nthree homes that faced one another round it. Her door was wide open on a fine day, and she had set herself up\njust inside it, out of the direct sun but in the breeze. Someone was hanging out washing.</p>\n<p>\"Can I help you?\"</p>\n<p>\"Salvina? My name is Perry. I'm only passing through, but someone at the inn sent me to you.\" He showed her\nthe shorts. They were the only ones he had, which was why he wanted them to be as versatile as possible. \"I\nspend a good deal of time on the road, and falling down in the woods, and I'm worried about what bites.\nCould these be extended? And untied again, to make them shorts?\"</p>\n<p>She had nothing that would fit him as it was. She hurried in and came out with several kinds of fabric,\nmost of them bags of some sort, from something like a potato sack at one end to something like a pillowcase\nat the other, and held them up against him and looked him over. \"Do you have another pair of trousers?\"</p>\n<p>He had not thought to bring any. She and the woman hanging washing conferred, and came back with a very long\ndress. He could wrap it around himself, sideways, for decency, while she worked on his shorts. They gave him\na room and a chair. He had to hold the dress in place the whole time.</p>\n<p>He offered to pay. She thanked him and said that if he would sit and talk with her while she worked, that\nwould be payment enough. What she wanted was gossip.</p>\n<p>Perry is not a man who tells stories about himself. His work was to watch and to listen. But he gave her\nsomething: everyone in the Company was jealous of Strawberry. The rest of them rode old ponies and old\nhorses, and there was Makheneb on Strawberry, like something out of a song, and the very leaves seemed to part\nfor her. The others had been proposing schemes: persuading Makheneb to trade, or stealing her in the night and\npretending she was lost, so that whoever found her first would get to ride her.</p>\n<h2>The second heist</h2>\n<p>Makheneb had nothing in particular to do that morning. He did not trust whatever Marigold was up to, and so he\nfollowed her, quietly.</p>\n<p>Ammie found Marigold near the Mathom-house. \"Are you ready?\"</p>\n<p>It was busier than usual, and Malva was greeting people at the door. Ammie went in first. Marigold went in a\nlittle after him, so that they would not seem to be together. Makheneb slipped in round her while she stood\nlooking awkward and suspicious by the entrance, and tucked himself into a nook further inside.</p>\n<p>From there he saw it. Up on the half-landing, among the bookshelves, there was a ladder that slid along a\nrail, and Ammie was at the top of it. Ammie fell. It looked like a strange sort of fall, and then Makheneb saw\nthat the boy's leg had caught in the rungs, and he heard it snap, and the scream that followed was real.</p>\n<p>Makheneb did not use the stairs. He caught the banister and was up at the boy's side at once. The leg was\nbroken badly, with the bone out through the skin. He drew his knife and slit the trouser leg open. To\neveryone looking up from below, there was suddenly an Elf with a drawn knife standing over a screaming child.</p>\n<p>Firework came yapping. Malva came up and saw what he was doing, and as he looked about for something to use as\na splint, she clucked and handed him a broomstick instead, and helped him. She apologised over and over. \"I'll\nmake a sign,\" she said, \"so that children don't climb the ladder.\" Makheneb stopped the bleeding, set the leg\nagainst the broomstick, and bound it tight with strips of cloth. The boy would have to be carried. Was there a\ndoctor? There were a couple of people who were good with healing, Malva said, mostly with animals. She led the\nway.</p>\n<p>Marigold, meanwhile, had picked up the bust. At the front there is a little step, only a few inches high, but a\nfew inches is enough when you are not expecting it. She stumbled. The bust slipped out of her hands and came down\non her left foot.</p>\n<p>The pain was blinding. She tipped the bust off her foot. At least two of her toes were broken. The bust had landed\nflat side down, and she did not think it had broken, though a chip might have come off. The bust was her first\nconcern, and she kept quiet. She could still walk on her heel. She hobbled across the road with it, to a gap in a\nfence where a little trail went off into a field, and set it down in the long grass, where nobody would see it\nunless they were right on top of it. Then she sat down and had a good cry about her toes.</p>\n<p>When she had finished, she saw Malva leading Makheneb away down the street with the boy in his arms. The\nMathom-house was empty. Even the dog was gone. She limped back in, went straight to the books, took the sixth\nvolume of Melody Took's journals, and hid it under her shirt.</p>\n<h2>Odo Boffin</h2>\n<p>The healer was a few doors down: Odo Boffin, a barber-surgeon and animal doctor. There are two sorts of healer.\nOne makes poultices and good broths that help you feel better. The other says <em>no, that's on backwards, let's set\nit right,</em> or cuts it off. Odo was the second sort. There was a hacksaw hanging on his back wall, and a great\nsling hanging from the roof for holding up a pony or a cow. He was grateful this had not happened in calving\nseason.</p>\n<p>By the end of the morning Makheneb was a minor celebrity in Michel Delving: the Elf who had saved the child with\nsuch quick action, and where had he learned those techniques?</p>\n<p>Marigold came limping in as he was leaving. Her toes were already badly swollen and purple.</p>\n<p>\"You'll never believe what happened,\" said Makheneb. \"A child fell. They may never walk again. Right through the\nleg. I thought that was supposed to be one of the strongest bones in the body.\"</p>\n<p>Ammie lay on the table while Odo looked at him. His eyes were red and his cheeks flushed from crying, but he was\nspent now, and calmer. He looked over at Marigold.</p>\n<p>\"Did you get it?\"</p>\n<p>Marigold nodded. Makheneb saw that too.</p>\n<p>Odo looked at her foot. \"I was skipping rocks,\" said Marigold, \"and I dropped a really big one.\"</p>\n<p>He had her sit and put the foot up. He enlisted Makheneb to help, and cleaned it and cooled it until the swelling\nbegan to go down. As far as he could tell, he was able to work the bones into a position where they should heal\ncorrectly. Then he bandaged it until she had a great club of a foot, and told her firmly to stay off it for a\ncouple of weeks, and to have the dressing changed every three or four days.</p>\n<p>She whispered to Ammie where she had left the bust. If he wanted it moved again, that was his problem.</p>\n<p>Leaning on Makheneb on the way back to the inn, she told him she was fine. She could ride. She had been worse.\n\"That is one brave child,\" she said. Makheneb said that Odo had been aghast to hear what ideas somebody had put in\nthe boy's head.</p>\n<h2>The road west</h2>\n<p>The Company set out west at midday, all five of them. Perry was the guide. He still had Tom's honeycomb for\nCírdan, and he now had trousers whose legs he could tie on and off, and he was no longer holding up a dress.</p>\n<p>They left the main roads. Here and there they rode on the broken remains of old roads, not kept up but not gone,\nwandering off in strange directions; the way they followed west was a footpath, well trodden, that had never been\nmade into a road. They met a few travellers, fewer than on the roads round Bree, and some of them on foot, whom\nthey passed.</p>\n<p>At one place the old broken cobbles turned north while their path went on west, and at the fork there were two\nmen standing by the road talking. Something about them struck Marigold as wrong. She could not have said what;\nthere was nothing plain to see, and they were not showing any weapons. She only knew she did not want to ride\nright past them. \"Keep an eye out, guys,\" she said, and she led them wide. They slowed down and turned off early,\nwent across country, overshot, and came back to the path well beyond the men. It wearied them. That night they\nmade a cold camp, with no fire.</p>\n<p>Marigold's foot throbbed. She could not lie flat with it raised; she was on a horse with it hanging down, and she\ncould feel her pulse in her toes. It was not bad, but it was obnoxious. She had brought a book of home remedies\nwith her, and there were two or three plants in it that looked promising, to keep the wound clean and bring the\nswelling down, if they could be made into a paste. She and Makheneb went off from camp to look for them. It was\nthe root that mattered, and the book only showed the plant in flower, so it took them some time to recognise it.\nIt was later and darker than they liked by the time they found it. While the others made camp and cooked and saw\nto the bedrolls, the two of them brought the roots back, washed them, boiled them, and pounded them into a paste,\npale green like horseradish. Marigold undid her bandages. There was her furry foot, and her big squashed purple\ntoes, and she packed the green paste in between them and bound it all up again and hoped for the best.</p>\n<h2>A bird</h2>\n<p>Out in the foothills, Makheneb asked the others to let him make a detour.</p>\n<p>There was a kind of bird in these parts, a great raptor of the size of a turkey vulture, with a wingspan of seven\nor eight feet. They were not hard to kill so much as rarely seen; around Mirkwood they almost never strayed so\nfar that an Elf got the chance. They were a delicacy. Makheneb had known the great Eagles at the Battle of Five\nArmies, and knew them for a whole people, and these birds were no kin of theirs, whatever the Eagles might say\nabout lesser birds. They did not swoop down on their prey. They seized it, flew up, and dropped it to its death,\nand then fed on it. Mostly they took sheep. Marigold had heard of them in the stories that hobbits tell children to\nkeep them from running off into the woods: a bird that might grab a young hobbit and fly away with it.</p>\n<p>Makheneb's first argument was that it was the best food there was. His second was that he was very hungry. The\nsecond was more convincing.</p>\n<p>He and Perry hunted it together. Marigold did not realise until afterwards how carefully the two of them had kept\nher busy, and kept an eye on her. She had been the bait. The bird had come for her. Makheneb's arrow brought it\ndown, and a clean stroke through the neck finished it. That evening at camp nobody undersold it or oversold it. It\nwas delicious, and there was plenty left over for the road.</p>\n<h2>Elf-land</h2>\n<p>They crossed a hill and had a brief glimpse of the horizon, and there was the Gulf of Lhûn. Then they came down\ninto a valley. They could not yet see the Grey Havens, but they were near; it was a few days away at most.</p>\n<p>Makheneb felt the change as they came into the valley. He had crossed into Lothlórien once, and he had come by\nRivendell on his way west. In the lands of the Elves everything feels old and still. Here it felt like a held\nbreath, and he did not know what it was being held for, or when it would be let go.</p>\n<p>\"We are entering Elf-land,\" he told the others. \"We're getting close. Keep your wits about you. If we meet any of\nmy kin, let me make the introductions. You can talk to them; only be respectful.\"</p>\n<p>The valley bottomed out in a great many streams, all running down to the gulf beside the big river to the north,\nand there, where their path went, was a bridge. It was not Wood-elf work, but it was plainly Elvish, and it crossed\na creek still full from the spring floods. In Lórien, and near the Woodland Realm, the Elves always knew when\nsomeone came. Here there was no sense of anyone at all. It was very strange.</p>\n<p>Lily had gone ahead a couple of hours before to scout the way. Now they heard her bird-whistle, the one Makheneb had\ntaught them. It was a passable bird, but not the right one; a real Elf would have done it properly, so Makheneb\nknew at once that it was Lily. He found her across the creek on the far side of the bridge, tucked down and trying\nto stay hidden, wide-eyed, shaking her head and pointing at the bridge.</p>\n<p>Makheneb made himself small and nocked an arrow, and crept round to see. Under the bridge, deep in its shadow, was a\ntroll.</p>\n<p>It had Lily's horse. It had lashed Buddy to the roots under the bridge with vines, or something like vines, and it\nwas stacking stones to make a pit big enough to roast a horse over. Lily was hurt, and could not see any way to\nget in and get Buddy out again.</p>\n<p>Makheneb knew about trolls. This was a cave-troll, not a great chieftain of its kind, but very strong, with a hide\nso tough that even arrows struggled to pierce it. They hate Dwarves above all things, and they are fiercest against\nthem. Nobody fights one alone unless they are very lucky or have something close to magic on their side. But they\nare as stupid as rocks. You can sometimes simply trick them and talk them round. If they find out they have been\ntricked, they get very angry, and conversations with trolls tend to end with the troll picking up the nearest stick\nor stone and beating your head in. And trolls turn to stone in the sun.</p>\n<p>Már went back into the trees with the horses. It was midday. The troll was in the shadow under the bridge, in a cut\nabout twenty feet deep, and to reach them in the sunlight it would have to come out of its shade.</p>\n<p>They argued it out. Marigold wanted to talk to it; she was the least use in a fight anyway, with her foot. If it went\nwrong, the others would shoot. Makheneb and Perry would lie hidden on either side of the bridge with arrows ready,\nfar enough away that the troll could not get at them. Perry needed all the help he could get to hit anything with a\nbow, but he would have the first shot prepared. And they had something to bargain with: the Ranger rations they had\nfound in the ruins.</p>\n<h2>Marigold and the troll</h2>\n<p>Marigold came limping up the path towards the bridge, as old and lame as she could make herself look, which was not\nvery hard. She stopped where the troll could see her but would have to work to throw anything at her. Makheneb had\ngiven her his rope and grapnel for lowering things.</p>\n<p>The troll sniffed the air, and caught sight of her.</p>\n<p>\"You're a bit small,\" it said. \"And you stink of Elf.\"</p>\n<p>\"I know,\" said Marigold. \"I want to get that smell off me. Thanks for telling me. It looks like you have a horse\nthere. I'm really struggling with my foot.\"</p>\n<p>\"You should come down and try the horse.\"</p>\n<p>\"I know a little bit about you,\" said Marigold. \"I'm a little scared to come down and try the horse.\"</p>\n<p>\"The horse can't hurt you. The horse is secured.\"</p>\n<p>\"Well, I can't really walk down. So what if we trade? I have some food here. It was given to me by an old friend,\nsomeone very clever and very powerful. And I gave some of it to an old friend of mine who was a troll and lived under\na bridge, and after he ate it he was able to walk out in the sunlight just like anyone else. It was incredible. I've\ngot some left over. You're so lucky. I'd trade it for the horse. But I need help. I'm so old I can hardly walk. Look\nat my broken foot.\"</p>\n<p>The troll looked at her with clear suspicion. \"I'll try it,\" it said at last. \"You don't look big enough to eat a\nhorse.\"</p>\n<p>\"I just need to get out of here. I don't want to stink of Elf like everything else round here.\"</p>\n<p>\"The horse stinks of Elf too.\"</p>\n<p>\"Then I'll have to wash it in a river. Is there one nearby?\"</p>\n<p>\"You can come down to this creek.\" It stamped across the water and stood looking up at her, deep in the shadow of the\nbridge.</p>\n<p>\"I'll lower this down once you untie the horse,\" said Marigold. \"And once you've eaten it, you can bring the horse up\nhere for me. I've got more rations to pay you with.\"</p>\n<p>\"Eat the rations and then bring the horse up?\"</p>\n<p>\"Take pity on this old woman. I can bring you more rations to help you walk into the light.\"</p>\n<p>The troll tried to think that through. Then it waved a hand, as though she were making it all too complicated,\nstamped over, pulled the lashings half off the horse, and stamped back. \"All right. The horse is loose.\"</p>\n<p>Marigold lowered the rations on the rope. They were in a satchel of waxed leather, a few small tightly wrapped bricks,\nthe kind you crumble into boiling water. The troll grabbed one and bit through it and ate half.</p>\n<p>\"As soon as you've finished it,\" said Marigold, \"you can walk out in the light for three hours. So use it quickly.\"</p>\n<p>They could see it trying to do sums. \"Do I need to eat all of them to go in the sun?\"</p>\n<p>\"One more bite and you're good.\"</p>\n<p>It ate half of the second brick, leaving a brick and a half. Then it went over to Buddy and lifted him bodily onto its\nshoulder, and took four steps out into the sunlight, and stopped.</p>\n<p>It had turned to stone. Buddy was still up on its shoulder, six feet off the ground, on top of a troll made of stone.</p>\n<p>\"We're alive!\"</p>\n<p>Lily called out from where she was hiding. She could move, she said, but she thought she had broken a couple of ribs.\nWhen the troll came out at her, Buddy had thrown her, and she had struck the side of the bridge. The troll had not\nseen her, and she had crept away into the undergrowth. She came hobbling over, holding her side.</p>\n<h2>Getting Buddy down</h2>\n<p>The troll's rope was probably rubbish tied in knots, and nobody trusted it. They made their own. Perry remembered the\nslings the cow-doctors use to lift a cow off the ground, and they built something like that: a harness under the horse\nand a system of ropes from the bridge above. Marigold, with her foot as it was, had to direct rather than haul, and\nbecause she had to explain everything, the work took more care and more time, and came out better for it. The point\nwas not to lift Buddy so much as to guide his fall, so that he would not break a leg; a horse with a broken leg would\nhave to be put down. Már and Lily were at the bottom, the others above, and on the count of three the ones below lifted\nand the ones above pulled. For a moment it seemed the ropes would not hold, and Buddy would drop. It was a controlled\ndrop, and he came down without breaking anything. Lily was hurt again in the doing of it, worse than before. It was\nbetter, she thought, that it was her and not the horse.</p>\n<p>Makheneb had left them to it. Trolls have hoards, and he wanted to find this one's. He followed the stream up a little\nway, and found the troll's den by the stink, which was no stranger to him. He pushed in. There were rotted carcasses of\nanimals, and a couple of skeletons that had been people, and scraps of cloth and scraps of metal and shiny things stacked\nin a corner. From one skeleton's finger he took a ring with a ruby in it. There was a skull with the lower part of its\nface smashed in and some of its hair still on it, and round the skull was a circlet of gold. And there was a pile of coins\nand bright things, enough for several trips.</p>\n<p>He came back again and again, stinking, and every time he brought more treasure and left for more, and after a while he\nwas wearing the golden circlet, a little crooked, like a crown. The others were doing well enough with the pulleys that\nthey let him get on with it. There was treasure enough for each of them to have a share.</p>\n<p>They camped that night on the bridge. It was easily defended, and they knew exactly where the threat was: it was stone,\nbeneath them.</p>\n<h2>The Grey Havens</h2>\n<p>The next day they came to the Grey Havens.</p>\n<p>It was late in the afternoon, and the sun was going down beyond the city and throwing the ships into silhouette. The\nHavens are famous for their ships, and none of them had ever seen anything like them. As they came nearer, the\nsilhouettes became ships at quays, three or four great galleys, and one in a dry dock half built. Roads came in from the\nnorth and the south to meet their own, and down from the north came great wagons carrying whole tree trunks. They passed\nthrough fields and farmland, and came to a ring of settled country around the city, and inside that a city within the\ncity. The people they passed looked at them with curiosity, but not unkindly.</p>\n<p>Makheneb searched his memory. The Grey Havens were unlike any other Elvish place. He had heard somewhere that not only\nwould he be welcome here, but that Men and Dwarves had come here too, now and then; perhaps because of the docks, the way\nout to whatever lies beyond the sea. As people looked at the Company and did not stop them, the old worry about whether\nthey would be allowed to stay settled in him. There would be a part of this city where all of them were welcome, and they\nwould need no special leave.</p>\n<p>For Perry and Lily and Marigold it was the biggest and grandest city they had ever seen. They knew what a boat was; they\nhad even heard of people going down a river in a boat. These were not boats. Some of them had boats on them. They were\nhouses on the water.</p>\n<p>Perry wanted to be indoors at once. It was overwhelming to be out in the street. It was overwhelming to look at the sea.\nHe had found out about the sea two weeks ago and had been told in the same breath that it was forbidden to him. He was\nhalf convinced that if he went near the water they would arrest him.</p>\n<p>\"Where are all the trees?\" he asked Makheneb. \"This isn't like your home.\"</p>\n<p>\"No,\" said Makheneb. \"More trees at home, and a lot less water.\"</p>\n<p>At the gate into the city proper, where people went in and out with their wares, there was an Elf waving people through\nand now and then asking a question or looking at a slip of paper. They came up to him in a clear little caravan, walking\nbeside their horses, with Makheneb in front. The Elf was finely dressed, with beautiful hair, and to Lily's surprise he\nsmelled very nice.</p>\n<p>\"Friend from Mirkwood,\" he said. \"Who are all these with you? I am Edrain, the steward.\"</p>\n<p>\"It's good to make your acquaintance. We have been long on the road,\" said Makheneb.</p>\n<p>\"Do you have a host?\"</p>\n<p>\"Not yet.\" They had met some of the Fair Folk on the road coming this way, Celenneth and Linnea and Eryndil, and had said\nthey might meet them here. Edrain did not know them.</p>\n<p>\"Well,\" he said, \"in the absence of a host, and that can change, of course, you are welcome at the Dwarven House. We have\nroom for any guest.\" It was just outside Nevlond's main gate, a little inward and to the left. One of the Elves at the gate\nwould take them. There were Fair Folk available if they had business in the city and wished to arrange it. \"We only ask that,\nif you have no host, you go with an escort. With an escort, the city is yours to explore and to do your business in. We are\nglad to have you.\"</p>\n<p>\"Heading west?\" he asked Makheneb. \"Over the sea?\"</p>\n<p>\"No. Not yet. It has not come yet, for me. Perhaps someday, if I am invited.\"</p>\n<p>Edrain seemed to assume that of course Makheneb would go; why would anyone not? But he respected the decision to wait.</p>\n<h2>The Dwarven House</h2>\n<p>The Dwarven House filled a whole city block, one single long building, with beautiful balconies that ran all the way around\nthe upper floor. Each of them was given a room. It was not an inn, where you pay and are nobody in particular. Most visitors\nhere had a host, who paid their costs; this house was the catch-all for those who were not Elves and came uninvited, and it\nwas kept as a kindness. They would not pay for their rooms. Food would be brought. They might have to fit in with others'\ntimes, and wait for an escort to be free if they wanted to go somewhere.</p>\n<p>To Perry, the escort seemed at first a question: <em>do you not trust us?</em> Then he thought about it as a gatekeeper would. There\nwere people who ought to be escorted in Bree and were not. He understood it completely. He would rather somebody told him\nwhen he was doing something wrong, since he was bound to, and he did not want to be arrested at the water's edge.</p>\n<p>The rooms were clean, and smelled of the sea. Before dinner they stood on the balcony and watched the sun go down over the\nwater. The city went on across the firth, down to the water on one side and on the other rising sheer on bluffs hundreds of\nfeet high, and a mile and a half on there was simply a beach. Perry could not take his eyes off the sea. He was afraid of it,\na little, the way someone who fears heights is afraid of a great height, and it was the most beautiful and dizzying thing he\nhad ever seen. The world was very large.</p>\n<p>He put on an Elvish robe, and as he did he felt the honeycomb in the pocket of his trousers, and remembered it was meant for\nCírdan. They would meet him at some point. He pushed the trousers, honeycomb and all, under the bed, so that no one would come\nin and throw them away.</p>\n<p>Makheneb set about cleaning himself up. Elves do not shave, but he had been on the road a long time, and Edrain's standard of\ndress was a good deal finer than most. Makheneb's own had been a poor place to start. He meant to make himself passable\nbefore he went among his kin. He had money now, and could buy things without an escort. He meant to find a weapon-master, an\nElf if he could, and perhaps someone who knew Númenórean work, and a tailor, and perhaps new leather armour. If they were going\nto stay a while, and they had healing to do, it would be a couple of weeks at least.</p>\n<p>Marigold took out her journal and began to draw what she had seen.</p>", "played": "2025-07-16", "slug": "06-the-mathom-house-and-the-troll", "title": "The Mathom-House and the Troll"}, {"date": "Mid-to-late Summer, 2965", "html": "<h2>The Dwarven House</h2>\n<p>The Grey Havens lie on either side of the Firth of Lhûn, where it opens into the Gulf and the Gulf\nopens into the sea. On the western shore is Haelond; on the eastern shore is Nevlond, where the\nCompany had come in. The Dwarven House stood in Nevlond, and despite its name it was not only for\nDwarves. It was where the Elves put anyone who was not an Elf and had no host.</p>\n<p>Nevlond was greener than they had expected. From the balconies they could see gardens and trees\neverywhere. Across the water Haelond looked grander, all fine stonework and craft, and in each city\nthere was a tower. The one in Nevlond served as a lighthouse, with a beacon at the top.</p>\n<p>Those were days of rest. Lily's broken ribs began to knit, and by the end of their stay they had\nhealed. Makheneb and the others learned what they could of the things they had brought out of the\ntroll's den. Már's badger necklace was Dwarf-work from Erebor, with eyes and claws of green stones,\nand there was power in it that made its wearer more formidable. The ruby ring was an Elvish ring\nthat brought with it a grace in courtesy. The golden circlet, with a pearl set in it, was also of\nElvish make, and it carried a blessing that could put heart into others. Makheneb, who thought\nhimself courteous enough already, gave the circlet to Perry, and Perry was delighted with it.</p>\n<p>Perry had a new leather corslet, too, fitted close and made cunningly light, to replace the one he\nhad given up.</p>\n<p>On their second day they began to notice the other guests. There were four Dwarves who kept a clockwork\nroutine. They had taken a corner of the common room for their own, where a couple of them played a\nsimple game with chips of stone, and they talked in low voices and smoked. Every so often one of them\nwould get up and speak to Edrain the steward, get a shake of the head, and sit down again. Már went\nover to them. Their leader was a Dwarf named Thramli, and he was cordial. He had come to petition\nCírdan and had not yet been granted an audience. He would say no more than that, and did not ask her\nto sit down.</p>\n<p>Lily noticed three of the Dúnedain together. She had never seen three at once. They had communities\nsomewhere, she knew, but in her experience they came through Bree singly or in pairs. There were two\nwomen and a man, and they kept to themselves. One of them always seemed to hold a certain corner of the\nbar while the other two went out with escorts, and they took it in turns, as though they were keeping\nsomeone here to wait for something. Lily introduced herself and asked what brought them there. She got\na gentle rebuff, very polite, and a mention that they had been using the Arantarion, the library over\nin Haelond.</p>\n<h2>An audience</h2>\n<p>Perry asked his escort how one went about seeing Círdan. Did they just walk in? Would they be put on some\nsort of list and made to wait all day?</p>\n<p>His escort was hundreds of years old, and Perry had not been told the customs, and it showed in the\nreply, which was the kind of answer one gives to a small child. \"I'm afraid that audiences are somewhat\nlimited just now. If there were something of great importance, I could see it relayed. But I cannot say\nhow long the wait might be.\"</p>\n<p>\"Could you tell them,\" said Perry, \"that Tom sent us?\"</p>\n<p>\"I will make it known.\" The name meant nothing to her. It might mean something to someone else.</p>\n<p>The Elves had given each of them an escort: Calalindir, a warden of the harbour; Ithrilas, a young scribe;\nand Nenuviel, a poet of the Havens. Marigold asked hers where she might hear the stories the Elves told one\nanother. As it happened, there was to be a performance that very evening by Mellind, a singer and\nstoryteller of Haelond, and they all went.</p>\n<h2>The Stone Garden</h2>\n<p>They crossed the firth to the sister city. The Stone Garden was a great plaza looking out over the gulf,\nand its paving made patterns and pictures across the whole of it, like a canvas laid on the ground. Elves\nstood and sat about it, eating and drinking and laughing. The performance ran from late afternoon until\ndusk, through all the colours of the sunset, and the space carried sound beautifully. Red-leaved trees\nflared in the last of the light.</p>\n<p>Mellind came out and played a few notes on a flute. She had other instruments about her. Then she began to\ntell a story. Makheneb could follow the words, though none of the names meant much to him; it seemed to\nbe a story from before the Elves came east to these shores, about lands and trees and countries across the\nsea. The others understood none of it. They did not need to. The language was so musical that its feeling\ncame through the melody and the verse alone: beauty, and loss, and discovery, and a great strain and\nstruggle that ended in some great deed.</p>\n<p>Perry cried through most of it. He had not understood a word.</p>\n<p>Haelond had trees of its own, too: a tall, straight, grey kind, ninety feet high, strange to all of them,\nwhose resin scented the whole of that side of the city. It was used in the shipyards, and in the liquor.</p>\n<h2>The pillars</h2>\n<p>On the way back up from the Stone Garden they passed two tall pillars, richly carved, each telling a story\nin pictures. The left-hand one told of the Last Alliance of Elves and Men. The right-hand one commemorated\nthe siege of Barad-dûr, and the sacrifice of the Elves, and of the Dúnedain and their kings. None of it\nmeant much to the Company. It was like walking into a museum that had a very thorough history of some far\ncountry for the last two and a half thousand years.</p>\n<p>Lily asked Calalindir what he made of it. It was not a grey story. It did not pretend the enemy might have\nbeen redeemed; but nor was it mere propaganda. It said plainly: here was the threat, here was what was\nthreatened, here was what had to be raised against it, here were the sacrifices, here was how it had been\nwon, and who had lived and who had died.</p>\n<p>\"I was present,\" said Calalindir, \"at the defeat of the Witch-king, many years ago.\" That was about a\nthousand years ago.</p>\n<p>\"Do you remember much of it? Does it feel like a thousand years?\"</p>\n<p>\"Memory is a strange thing. There are years that stand out, and years that flow together. That was a tragic\nand memorable year. I remember too much of it still.\" These pillars were a memorial to those who had not\ncome back: those for whom immortality had been denied. It was a strange way to put it, to the three who\nwould die of age. For the Elves, dying was not the way of things, except for those who died in times like\nthat, and it was a different kind of grief.</p>\n<p>As they walked, they saw that many of the buildings were empty. They were not derelict, only tidy and\nempty. They did not see any children.</p>\n<p>They passed the bridge-gate, which was shut. Then they came to the tower on the Haelond side. It was called\nthe Tower of Lofty Light, and it had another name: the Arantarion, the name the Dúnedain had spoken of. The\nlibrary was there, and the forges were beneath it. Beside it stood a stone palace with roofs of red tile.\nIt had been the hall of Gil-galad, who founded the city, one of those who came long ago over the sea.\nGaladriel, whose name only Makheneb knew, had once lived on the Nevlond side with others of her kin, and\nhad given it over to Círdan and gone away east; she dwelt now in Lothlórien. The Noldor were those Elves\nwho had come across the sea into Middle-earth, and the Sindar those who had never crossed it.</p>\n<h2>The Arantarion</h2>\n<p>Lily and Marigold and Már went into the library.</p>\n<p>It went down several levels underground, and everything in it was kept with a quiet rigour. It was room\nupon room in a great ring, with paths between that felt almost like a maze; they came in at the centre and\nfound at least two rings of rooms and shelves around them, and at least three levels. None of them had ever\nimagined so much writing in one place. There were scholars, and there were people making copies. Across the\nway, one of the Dúnedain from the Dwarven House went by with a couple of scrolls, carrying them to a study\nroom.</p>\n<p>Lily wanted to know how it was all arranged; surely it was not just put anywhere. An attendant explained.\nA great deal of the library's wealth came from Elves on their way west over the sea. They took everything\nof value with them when they left Middle-earth for ever, but a great many of their writings they left here.\nMuch of the archive was what remained of Elvish settlements all over the land, down the long years. Lily\nthought of the empty buildings. The city must once have held three or four times as many as it did now.</p>\n<p>Marigold asked for stories of monsters, and of anything like the banes. That, she was told, needed\nauthorisation. Instead, as a kindness, they offered her the histories written for children.</p>\n<p>Lily slipped away and went, quietly, to where the Dúnedain were working. One of the women looked up. \"Can I\nhelp you?\"</p>\n<p>\"Hi, yes. I just found this library. It's so huge; I'm just trying to get the lay of the land. What are you\nall researching?\"</p>\n<p>\"Who are you?\" It was not a question about her name. It was a question about what right she had to ask.</p>\n<p>Lily told her she was a fellow traveller with a project of her own, that they had met some dark things on\ntheir travels, and that they were trying to learn where such things came from. She told her about the Orc\nin the Moors.</p>\n<p>The woman sat up at that. Lily was not, then, entirely a stranger to the wild. Her name was Laeroneth. \"We,\ntoo, have met things that are not in their proper places,\" she said. \"We hope to learn more here. It is a\nwonderful resource. You should make use of it.\"</p>\n<p>Lily asked whether she could borrow things. Unfortunately, Laeroneth said, they did not lend. You could study\nhere for years unless you knew how to find what you wanted. But the attendants were very helpful, if you could\ntell them what you were looking for and they believed you had good reason. \"If you find yourself getting\nnowhere, come to me, and I'll see if I can help.\"</p>\n<p>Lily asked one of the librarians whether a Ranger named Findemir had ever brought anything here, or read\nanything. The head of the library was Galdor, though it was not Galdor she spoke to. They were quite sure that\nFindemir had never visited.</p>\n<h2>The forges</h2>\n<p>Calalindir took Makheneb and Perry to the forges.</p>\n<p>There were forges on several scales. There were smithies for weapons and armour, and those, it seemed, were for\nbeginners. Then there were the great forges where they made things for ships and buildings, working in tons.\nThe whole tower had been built around them; the heat of the forges was drawn off and led through it, and the\nair brought in from outside was warmed and sent all over the tower. They were sweating a little.</p>\n<p>Calalindir brought them to Gaethel of the Anvil, who had charge of all of it. \"One of our guests, Makheneb of\nMirkwood, has a question for you.\"</p>\n<p>Makheneb laid the great bow on the bench, and the broken Númenórean sword from the ruins. \"I wonder whether you\nknow anything of the history of these. There seems to be some kind of curse on the bow, at least. I wonder\nwhether you know anything of the nature of that sort of thing.\"</p>\n<p>\"A curse?\" said Perry. Nobody had told him there was a curse on the bow. The only curses he had heard of were\nthe terrible kind, where everyone around was infected. \"How do we fix this?\"</p>\n<p>Gaethel picked up the bow and turned it over and looked at it, and handed it back. \"There is indeed a darkness in\nthis. If you will allow us, we could keep it here, and try to understand what we can of the nature of the curse.\nI cannot promise that we can remove it.\" He would send word to the Dwarven House.</p>\n<p>Then he turned to the sword. \"This is of Númenórean make. It is a good sword. We could reforge it.\"</p>\n<p>Makheneb had no skill with swords. It should be Perry's, he said. Perry asked whether it could be made into\nsomething more like an axe. Gaethel said that was very tractable indeed.</p>\n<p>\"We will get back to you about the bow,\" he said. \"We will make sure you get it back. I do not like keeping cursed\nthings about indefinitely.\"</p>\n<p>\"Neither do I,\" said Makheneb.</p>\n<h2>Círdan will see you</h2>\n<p>When they came back into the common room of the Dwarven House, Edrain came to them. \"Círdan will see you in the\nmorning.\"</p>\n<p>Thramli was at Már's side at once. \"How did you manage an audience?\"</p>\n<p>\"You know Tom?\" said Már. It had all come down, she explained, to some honey in Perry's pocket. Apparently that\nmattered to Círdan. \"That's pretty much it. I wish I understood more, to be honest.\"</p>\n<p>\"Would you bring our petition before Círdan on our behalf?\"</p>\n<p>Már had meant to offer to help them anyway. \"What is the petition? If I can help, I'd like to.\"</p>\n<p>\"I'll buy some drinks,\" said Thramli. \"Come and sit down. There are a few pieces to understand.\" And Már sat at\nthe Dwarves' table.</p>\n<p>High in the Blue Mountains, Thramli said, there was a thing called the Cavity Stone. It was very old, and there\nwas a group of Dwarves whose work was to keep the place where it lay. It was a place that was left as it was and\ntended, the way a Dwarf-hall keeps a part of itself as it has always been and goes and builds new things elsewhere;\nit mattered for its stonework, and it mattered more deeply than that. The stone itself had not been made by\nDwarves. It had been found. And it was doing strange things to the minds of those who kept it. They were starting\nto crack. It had taken the Dwarves thirty years to work out that the stone was the thing all the troubles had in\ncommon. When they cut off someone's exposure to it at the first signs, the effects stopped growing worse, but they\ndid not go away, and nobody had found a way to undo them.</p>\n<p>Mostly it showed itself as disturbances of the mind. One of the keepers had died in an accident. It might have been\ncarelessness, the kind of carelessness that comes over someone who knows every safety rule inside out and one day\nsimply does not follow them. Another had threatened violence, and had to be restrained and kept apart, watched,\nand allowed no weapons. And some of them had begun to speak in strange tongues. The Dwarves had tried to write down\nwhat they said, as it sounded, and did not know what language it was.</p>\n<p>Did it affect animals? There were no animals kept near it, so they did not know.</p>\n<p>Thramli gave Már the scraps of paper. They were copies of the ones meant for Círdan.</p>\n<p>Had they looked in the library? They had not. They had been told they were not permitted.</p>\n<p>\"Why?\" Már asked. They did not know. They had only been told.</p>\n<p>That struck Már as suspicious. Lily had been let into the library without anyone asking her anything. And the steward\nwho kept the Dwarves from Círdan was the same one to whom all the escorts answered.</p>\n<p>Meanwhile one of the Dúnedain, the man, came over to Lily. \"Círdan hasn't been seeing anyone,\" he said. \"How did you\nmanage it? I thought the returning ship would take all his attention for some time.\"</p>\n<p>\"The returning ship?\"</p>\n<p>\"You didn't hear? There was a ship of Elves heading west. It was turned back by strange storms, and it has had to\ncome home. It has taken up most of their attention. The ones at the top are trying to work out whether it is only for\nnow, and what it means. I've never heard of a voyage being turned back like that.\"</p>\n<p>Lily told him that Laeroneth had kindly offered to help them in the library, and asked whether he would like her to\nmention their business to Círdan. He thanked her. \"I don't feel our questions are well put yet. In time, with enough\nresearch, I hope we can bring Círdan a question worthy of him.\"</p>\n<h2>Canthwë</h2>\n<p>Marigold is a hobbit, and she loves her pipe as much as any hobbit. As soon as she came into the Dwarven House that\nevening she could smell pipe-weed, and she could have told you exactly which leaf it was, and within a few years which\nharvest. In the corner sat an old man, a Man by the look of him and not an Elf, with a long beard, smoking. She had not\nseen him before. He caught her staring, and held up his pipe. \"Smoke with me.\"</p>\n<p>His name, he said, was Canthwë. He liked walking about the city best of all. He loved the green things, and did a\nlittle gardening, trimming this and that. He was not an Elf, but he seemed to go wherever he liked, and to see Círdan\nwhenever he pleased; he was some kind of friend of the household, or servant, or a member of it in some loose way that\nwas never made clear. He lived in the city and came to the Dwarven House to sit sometimes.</p>\n<p>His talk came and went. He would ask Marigold about herself, and then he would go off on strange tangents, long and\nwinding, about little things: how certain beasts of the field were not what you thought, and the animal you took for a\nhorse was nothing of the kind and no relation to any horse. Then he would come back round to some fellow he had met down\nby the docks, and what they had done. Marigold grew very comfortable.</p>\n<p>It was not a strong leaf; pipe-weed only relaxes you, and is certainly not meant to show you visions. At first she felt\nloose and easy all over. Then everything began to turn monstrous, and the monstrousness was sucked quickly away, and\nsomething glorious took its place; and it went on happening, over and over, as though terrors kept coming out of a\ncupboard and somebody kept slamming the door on them and unrolling a beautiful tapestry in front of it. She lost track of\nwhat he was saying. The visions were so vivid that she found herself looking round at them, and almost speaking to them,\nas if they were really there.</p>\n<p>When it settled at last, Canthwë was gone. Most of the house had gone to bed. Marigold sat there with her body loose and\nher heart racing, and with the aftertaste of having seen something glorious, and something else that disturbed her and\nthat she could not quite remember.</p>\n<h2>Before Círdan</h2>\n<p>In the morning they made themselves ready. They had no time to go to the market and buy better clothes; they would have to\ndo with what they had, made as clean as possible. Perry had washed his hair and slicked it back, and was wearing what he\nthought of as his best. It was not presentable. He had the Elvish circlet on, and his trouser-legs were tied on, all three\ntimes over. It was certainly a look.</p>\n<p>Már did her best to resolve not to be hot-headed. Other people were counting on her for their health. She meant to watch\nCírdan closely and find the right time to raise the Dwarves' petition.</p>\n<p>They talked over what they wanted. Lily wanted to get into the library properly. They all wanted help with what they had\nseen, with what they were going to see, and with what to do about it. Why was the white beast <em>the weapon against</em> anything?\nWhy was the fanged woman so frightening, and bald? Did he know anything of the long-folk? Was there some original charge, some\nfirst set of instructions, that the Númenóreans had been given and that had come down to the Rangers like a message passed\nalong a line, getting fainter at every step? Círdan was so old that he might simply know. Perry had given Círdan's honey\nexactly the one nibble. He had also dipped into it a few times since, because his pocket knife was in the same pocket.</p>\n<p>Edrain himself took them to the tower of Círdan. It rose from an eight-sided base, and the tiles were worked with patterns\nof the sea, and there were shells set into the stone. Inside it was clean and bright, and the windows were set so that the\nsun came in without any glare, and the whole place was warm, as though they were outside though they were indoors. They went\nup to a mezzanine, and up again, to a room where guests were received, with tables and small refreshments laid out. Then the\ndoors opened.</p>\n<p>They had seen a great many Elves by now, and almost none of them had beards. Círdan had a beard, and grey hair. For Makheneb\nhe was the oldest Elf he had ever seen, by a long way. He guessed at five thousand years, and more.</p>\n<p>Círdan came forward. \"A friend of Iarwain Ben-adar!\" he said, and embraced them. \"Welcome, welcome, welcome, friends!\"</p>\n<p>\"Círdan, right?\" said Perry. \"I'm guessing you know Tom?\"</p>\n<p>\"Iarwain Ben-adar, as we call him. Yes. He is older even than I.\"</p>\n<p>\"And you don't look a day over sixty.\"</p>\n<p>\"It has been an age, and an age, and an age,\" said Círdan.</p>\n<p>\"Well,\" said Perry, \"we're on a sort of quest for him. We're helping the Rangers of the North with a spot of bother they're\nhaving, with these giant creatures that sometimes are and sometimes aren't asleep, all over the place. Oh, and also: honey.\nHere you go.\"</p>\n<p>For a moment Círdan simply held it. Then he fetched a bowl and put the honey in it, and smelled it, and dipped his fingers in\nand tasted. \"It is still good,\" he said. \"This is a perfect gift.\"</p>\n<p>Perry apologised for having had a bit. Círdan was not in the least concerned.</p>\n<h2>The council</h2>\n<p>Már began by singing <em>Ghostcleavers</em>, to show him what they had faced. Círdan listened politely, and just as she was about to\ngo back into the chorus he cut her off. \"I apologise for the rudeness. I am rather pressed for time today, because of other\nduties. But if you have particular questions or concerns, I can address them.\"</p>\n<p>Lily gave him the short version, with no song. They were working with Tom and the Rangers, looking into these banes, and they\nhad a great many questions, and they had come to him because of his wisdom, and his knowledge, and the library. She would like\nto be allowed into the library properly, please.</p>\n<p>Halfway through, Marigold caught a smell she knew from the night before. Canthwë had wandered in and was eating the food off\nthe side tables.</p>\n<p>When Lily had finished, Círdan nodded. \"Ah, yes. I am familiar with some of these. You call them banes. That is a useful name.\"\nHe pointed at Canthwë. \"One such thing, more a place than a thing, corrupted one of the Istari. I have taken care of it.\"</p>\n<p>Már and Makheneb knew the word. The Istari were the Wizards; they knew of at least a couple of them. Canthwë, it seemed, was\none, and one of these banes had corrupted him, and Círdan had that in hand. To Perry and Lily and Marigold it sounded rather\nas though Círdan's gardener had got corrupted, and Círdan had seen to it. Perry heard <em>Istari</em>, and supposed Canthwë had once\nbeen a star. It had not been here, Círdan said, but far to the east, beyond the Misty Mountains, further east even than\nMirkwood. \"I am making sure that he is no danger to himself or others. He is capable of great power, and he could be a great\ndanger.\"</p>\n<p>Then Már gave him the scraps of paper from the Dwarves. It took him a moment to make sense of them, because they were written\nas they sounded. Then his face hardened.</p>\n<p>\"Who said this?\"</p>\n<p>\"The Dwarves who kept the stone,\" said Már, \"before they were taken from their work. They were the ones.\"</p>\n<p>\"This is the Black Speech,\" said Círdan. \"This is the language of the Enemy.\" He paused. \"I will hear the rest of you out. We\nwill come back to this.\"</p>\n<p>Perry asked his question. \"Why haven't we been corrupted? And what should people do so that they aren't?\"</p>\n<p>Círdan looked at him with compassion. \"Much of what I have heard of your folk seems to be greatly out of date,\" he said. \"You\nhave a tremendous courage about you. You say this as though you mean to seek out the rest of them.\"</p>\n<p>\"In fact, that's our quest,\" said Perry. He took out his grandfather's map and spread it on the floor. \"We've been to two. This\nwas the wolf, the giant white beast. This was the fanged woman. Both asleep. They both seemed all right. And we're heading here,\nand here.\" He pointed to the Blue Mountains, and south of Tharbad. \"But we're pretty lost. We only know we're supposed to be\nchecking on them. When we get to the Blue Mountains we won't know what we're looking for, or how to get in. Maybe it's with the\nDwarves; maybe it's this stone. What we want to know is where it is, and what we're looking for, and how to deal with it.\"</p>\n<p>\"You have been working with Tom,\" said Círdan. \"Tom is many things. One thing he is not is organised.\" He looked round at them.\n\"Do you urgently need to be anywhere?\"</p>\n<p>\"Is there an urgency about these banes?\" asked Marigold. \"Should we go and see to them as soon as we can? And what does seeing to\nthem look like?\"</p>\n<p>\"There are urgencies of different kinds,\" said Círdan. \"I am dealing with a crisis now that must be resolved by Mereth Bantiel, a\nfestival at the end of summer, a couple of months from now. After that I can sit down with you and we can make a plan together:\nwhat you would need, and whom I could send with you, to the Blue Mountains or wherever we decide. I would welcome your company\nuntil then. You would have the run of the cities. We will find you quarters of your own; you need not stay at the Dwarven House.\nYou will be my guests. This is a matter of great weight and delicacy, and I do not wish you to run headlong into anything that\nwould put you in danger. I do not believe that a matter of months will make a great difference to this.\"</p>\n<p>Lily asked whether they could help with the ship.</p>\n<p>Every year at the end of summer, at Mereth Bantiel, Círdan said, they sent a ship into the West, with any Elves who were leaving\nthese lands. The ship now returning was last year's. It had not yet arrived, but it had sent a message ahead of it. In the next few\ndays they would send ships out to meet it, because it sounded as though those aboard had suffered greatly, and the ship was hardly\nseaworthy; it had not sunk, but it might not make it home. And there was this year's ship, with its passengers already listed.\nShould they send both out again? Wait a year? What should become of the passengers? Those were the questions before him. Within a\nfew weeks some of his people would go out to understand things better, and then there would be decisions.</p>\n<p>Then he turned back to Már. What was her concern?</p>\n<p>\"How are the Dwarves supposed to protect themselves?\" she asked. \"They have people affected whom they can't treat. How are they\nsupposed to fight something they can't strike?\"</p>\n<p>\"What is your own part in it? How well do you know this stone, and the people who keep it?\"</p>\n<p>Only what they had told her, said Már. It had been brought to her attention by other Dwarves; she had never seen it herself. But it\nsounded like the things the Company had been seeing near the banes: the dead, and the man frozen, perhaps cursed.</p>\n<p>\"Some of the Enemy's devices have the power to warp the minds of the weak,\" said Círdan. \"I do not mean that unkindly. In the face of\ngreat power, many of us are weak. The speech you have shown me is strengthening the effect. As a first measure, those who speak such\nthings must be kept apart from the thing itself. I do not think the danger is a matter of months. But it is like water set over a\nfire: you may heat it for a long time before it boils, and once it boils it boils. The water has been heated, and it is not yet\nboiling, and these words are more heat. If that speech goes on in the presence of that thing, it will come to the boil the sooner.\"</p>\n<p>\"So it's only the speaking near the stone? If they speak like that miles away, it isn't as much of a problem?\"</p>\n<p>\"It should matter very little, by comparison.\"</p>\n<p>He would send someone back with the Dwarves, he said, to see that the right measures were kept to in the meantime. It would not be\nan answer, but it would keep things from growing worse than they need while a better answer was found.</p>\n<p>\"Thank you,\" said Már. She felt easier knowing they would have someone who knew.</p>\n<p>Then they were dismissed, and it was late morning.</p>\n<h2>What they made of it</h2>\n<p>On the way back they talked it over. Már was surprised that there was anything more important than the banes. They had brought him\nthis, and he had said, in effect, <em>we will get to it when we get to it.</em> Lily thought it made more sense than it seemed. Nothing\nlike the ship had ever happened before; the Dúnedain said so, and the Elves, she thought, did not get rattled very often. Perhaps\nit was a perfect storm. Perhaps even the storm was connected to another bane. There were fifty things on the list now, and she\ndid not like any of them.</p>\n<p>At least he was sending someone to the Dwarves. And Círdan, they agreed, had plainly been charmed by Perry. They had better keep that\nfriendship strong.</p>\n<p>Lily had looked over, while Círdan was talking about the minds of the weak, and seen Canthwë eating the vegetables off a tray with his\nfingers, taking a bite and dipping back in.</p>\n<h2>The villa</h2>\n<p>When they got back to the Dwarven House, Edrain was waiting, with their escorts and a few others. Their new quarters were ready. They\ncould gather their things and go, and their horses and ponies would be brought over.</p>\n<p>It was a villa in a very fine part of Nevlond. There were abandoned buildings here too, though fewer than on the Haelond side. It\nstood three storeys high, and it had been partly refurnished for them, some things taken out and new things brought in and the whole\nplace freshened up. Each of them had several rooms to themselves, with common spaces to share. And each was given a little clasp to\nwear, which showed that they had Círdan's favour. They could go freely through both cities, and use the library, and their requests\nwould be taken rather more seriously from now on.</p>\n<p>Lily was not used to living in a house where everyone had their own rooms, and she went round them all in the mornings, waking\neverybody up. The only one who was ever up before her was Marigold.</p>\n<h2>The weeks of summer</h2>\n<p>Summer went on in Mithlond, and the Company settled into its days.</p>\n<p>Már went to the markets. The weavers made a cloth that was very light and breathed beautifully, and the Elves wore it in overlapping\nlayers, so that its colours showed through one another and blended. The outer layers often carried designs a little out of line with\neach other, and as the wearer moved the designs shifted against one another. The glassmakers laid glass on glass in different colours,\nin intricate patterns, and made beautiful jars. And there was a wine found nowhere else, made with the resin of the tall grey trees of\nHaelond, and with seaweed in it too, strong-tasting and heady.</p>\n<p>On the Nevlond side the gardens were a riot of every kind of plant, and Marigold drew them in her book.</p>\n<p>Már went back to see the Dwarves. Thramli thanked her over and over. They were going home, and with them someone whom Círdan trusted\nas a right hand, who would hold things together until Círdan could send more, probably some time in the autumn. The Dwarves owed her a\ngreat favour. If she ever came up their way, into the Blue Mountains towards the Halls of the Dwarves, they would gladly host her. And\nif she happened to come across anything in the library about a certain very old disagreement over who had been first to a certain\nthing, anything at all that supported his side of it, Thramli would be much obliged. It was the kind of petty feud that is so very\nDwarvish. Már began to suspect it might be why they had been kept out of the library.</p>\n<p>Lily spent half of every day in the library, with or without anything particular to look for, and sat with the Dúnedain when they would\nlet her. She told them a little more of what her company was about, and of Findemir, and Moriel. And they told her theirs. They had found\nsome old Númenórean things they could not identify, and the place where they had found them, and they were trying to learn more about\nboth. Lily's history with Findemir, and the fact that Marigold had taken up Findemir's errand when he fell, made an impression on them.</p>\n<p>When she was not at her books she was in the stables. She took care of Buddy, and she followed the Elves who worked with the animals and\nasked them a thousand questions and tried to learn what she could. The Elves had not perfected the breeding of horses, but they had come\na long way; theirs were some of the most magnificent beasts she had ever seen. Their chief experience of mortality, living here, was their\nanimals, which came and went in the blink of an eye. They shared their knowledge freely. Buddy had charms of his own, and no one was rude\nto him.</p>\n<p>Perry went to the gatehouses. It was where he felt most at home. At first they rebuffed him: <em>we are trying to do a job here; go and see\nthe city.</em> But he must have mentioned it to Edrain in passing, because a place was made for him, a small and carefully limited one, where\nhe could help and could watch. There were not the crowds there had been at the gates of Bree. What was remarkable was who came. There were\nElves from Mirkwood, and one from Lothlórien. Some brought whole wagons, and some came alone on a single horse. All of them came with the\nsame soberness. They had lived all their lives in the east, and they were never going back, and they were coming here to wait for a ship\nthat might or might not sail at the end of summer, into a future none of them knew. Perry never had the chance to sit down with any of them,\nbut their manner was muted, and the sadness of it weighed on him for a long while.</p>\n<p>Perry was also afraid of the sea, and he set out to cure himself of it. At first he only watched the water. Low tide in Haelond left it only\nthree feet deep, and that he could manage; he started by putting his toes in. Out on the flats at low tide there was an older Elf, older\nthan Makheneb though nothing like as old as Círdan, who caught crabs and other things with his hands. His name was Otto. He had been a\nsailor once, and he was not quite ready to go west, and he much enjoyed waiting, and cooking. He became Perry's friend. Perry learned about\nthe sea from him, and listened to his stories, and went to his crab boils, and one day walked all the way in, chest deep, and looked round\nvery proudly, as if he did this all the time.</p>\n<p>Perry had also never had so many taverns to go to, and the cities were far more crowded than Bree, however empty the buildings. He made\nfriends in them, and drank the local wine, and lived, within reason, on Círdan's account.</p>\n<p>Makheneb saw about new armour, and practised, and every now and then disappeared for days at a time into the forests of Lindon to hunt.\nComing back from one of these, he found a message from Gaethel waiting for him: he should come and collect his bow. The smiths had cleansed\nit. The Shadow that had been upon it no longer weighed on the one who carried it. Something of it still lingered, Gaethel warned. It would\nmake it harder for him to move unseen among his enemies; it would draw their attention when he was near. Orcs, at the very least.</p>\n<p>He went and found Perry to tell him. He had it back, and it was even better than before.</p>\n<h2>The ship</h2>\n<p>Meanwhile Círdan's ships were readying to go out to meet the returning one. Már and Lily were interested in going. Marigold would take any\nchance to see something new. Perry would not go on a boat. Makheneb thought it might be better if he stayed; he would be too tempted to keep\ngoing west.</p>\n<p>In the end, they stayed in the Havens, and waited to see what the ship would bring.</p>", "played": "2025-07-29", "slug": "07-mithlond", "title": "Mithlond"}, {"date": "End of Summer, 2965", "html": "<h2>Before the festival</h2>\n<p>Summer was drawing to its end, and the Havens were making ready for Mereth Bantiel. A small ship was\ngoing out to meet the one that was coming home, and the Company could have gone with it. Nobody had\nasked them to; they would have had to volunteer. The festival was the reward, somebody said, and the\nvoyage would be the punishment, and why would anyone choose the punishment? None of\nthem went. They stayed in the villa and waited. Their own departure would come after the festival;\nthere would still be time to reach the Blue Mountains before winter.</p>\n<p>As the preparations quickened, something else crept into them. Everyone knew about the returning\nship by now, and it had not yet come. So the Havens made ready for a feast with one eye always on the\nsea, as though bad news might come over the horizon at any moment. It was like getting ready for the\nmidwinter holiday while a grandmother lies ill, and nobody yet knows how ill.</p>\n<p>Lily felt it most. Her answer, when things grew tense, was to keep her hands busy. She had gone to the\nlibrary to study and found herself putting books away, and after that she was there every morning,\nshelving, whether anyone had asked her to or not. At the stables she did the same, cleaning and\ncounting and checking whatever needed doing.</p>\n<p>There she noticed Buddy's friend. The Elves' horses were not great warhorses, but they were taller\nthan Buddy, and lean and rangy, strong without bulk. One of them was a white horse called Levlay.\nWhen they let the horses out to run in the great field, Buddy could never keep up, and Levlay would\ncircle back and run beside him. The two of them were friends. The stablemaster said to Lily that\nBuddy had settled in very well, and was flourishing among the Elves' horses. Then he told her about\nthe horse-run. At one point in the festival the horses would run through the streets, through one city\nand over the bridge into the other, a procession that was more of a stampede. The roads had to be\nmade ready for it. A couple of lead horses went first and the rest learned to follow. Buddy had simply\nbeen left out of it, of course. But Levlay was so keen on him that the stablemaster wondered whether\nLily would be willing for Buddy to run too.</p>\n<p>Lily said it would be an honour. She said it as coolly as she could. As soon as the stablemaster's back\nwas turned, she did a little dance.</p>\n<h2>Mablin</h2>\n<p>Perry spent his spare hours on the walls, looking out to sea. He had learned about the Straight Road, the\nway across the sea to the West, and that the westbound ship had been turned back somewhere along it and\nhad sent word by bird. His clasp from Círdan let him go most places, with an escort, and it was not a time\nof emergency, so they let him up into the towers that looked out over the water.</p>\n<p>He was not the world's greatest thinker, but he was in tune with what people were feeling. He knew by now,\nfrom Otto, that this Third Age was an age of loss, and that the Elves were passing on to something better,\nand he felt the weight of that. He wanted to talk with people about better days, and to hear about times\nthat had been good. He found a guard named Mablin, who had the air of a grandfather and a grandfather's\nknack for stories: not the kind where you sit down and are told a story, but the kind where something\ncomes up and he says, <em>oh, that reminds me</em>, and gives you five minutes of a whole world you never knew\nexisted. He would not be made to teach anything in order. Perry tried, and he resisted. The only way to\nlearn from him was in scraps. But Perry soaked those up with such eagerness that Mablin warmed to him.</p>\n<p>Perry came to understand two things about him. Mablin wanted very much to go west. And he did not want to\nleave. He did not want to give all this up. He wanted to go, and he wanted to stay, and now there was the\nnews, and the fear that the way might be shut and the choice taken out of his hands. That whole season\nwas bittersweet to him, and he kept it at bay by telling Perry stories of the ages before.</p>\n<h2>You weren't there</h2>\n<p>Makheneb had very little patience for any of it. The Elves were in a state because one ship had not come\nhome. Makheneb had come from the east, and in the east things were already bad. The Elves of the Havens,\nit seemed to him, were sitting in a comfortable hidey-hole without the faintest idea.</p>\n<p>One evening there was a gathering outdoors, low-key, with food and drink, people coming and going.\nMakheneb had been keeping to himself. Nearby a couple of Elves were talking, and they mentioned the Battle\nof Five Armies in passing, as a little blip out in the east, and dismissed it.</p>\n<p>Makheneb had known Elves who died in that battle. He began to recite their names. He knew them all, and he\nsaid them one after another, and he grew louder and angrier as he went. \"You weren't there,\" he told them.</p>\n<p>It killed the evening. People drifted away awkwardly, and went home. But to their credit, word got round.\nNow and then after that some Elf would come up to him out of nowhere and say, <em>we are so sorry for your\nlosses.</em> And the rest gave him room.</p>\n<h2>Nutmeg bread</h2>\n<p>Marigold did not understand why it mattered so much that the ship should come back, or why people went\nanywhere at all, and every time somebody explained it to her she lost the thread. She went looking for\nwhoever still had some joy in them, and found her way into the kitchens.</p>\n<p>They were mostly in Nevlond: great kitchens for feeding crowds, in the middle of the long preparations\nbefore a feast, when every sauce and every spice-mix is made ahead so that on the day it can all be put\ntogether. The Elves of Lindon used almost all the same things that grew in the Shire, apart from what came\nfrom the sea, but they put them together in their own proportions, and the result was quite different.\nMarigold rolled up her sleeves. She traded recipes. And she took one of their breads and changed it a\nlittle, the way changing the proportions a little in baking gives you something new, and made it hers: a\nbraided loaf, flaky, with nutmeg in it, that you could tear chunks off in the street. She made it so many\ntimes, at such a scale, that she had it perfect.</p>\n<p>The kitchens were full of talk about the festival: which songs, which singers, something with the boats in\nthe harbour, perhaps even fireworks. Marigold collected all of it and made herself the Company's guide.\nThis was happening on this street, and then a couple of hours later they would have to be over here for\nthis. There was a storyteller she was especially excited about. Nobody else was as excited as she was.</p>\n<p>At the villa the Company mostly passed one another in the halls, coming and going. Lily called a family\nmeal every day, and insisted on it. Perry slept when he could, as he always had, since his old master at the\ngate had given him such wild shifts; and every night he went to bed an hour later than the night before,\nuntil he had lapped everyone else and was falling asleep in his porridge. Once, at one of the family\ndinners, he woke up long enough to say <em>take me to that</em> about somebody's day, and fell asleep again. The\nvilla was a place to leave your things and to sleep, and to see each other now and then. Otherwise there\nwas far too much to see.</p>\n<h2>Mereth Bantiel</h2>\n<p>The festival began with a line of ships going out through the harbour. Then the horses were brought down to\nthe water's edge, and they came charging up through the streets of Nevlond, over the bridge into Haelond, all\nthe way up to the gates and round again, back towards the stables, and Buddy ran with them beside Levlay.\nThey had seen the ships at the quays and on the water, but they had never seen them under full sail,\nsweeping across the horizon.</p>\n<p>After that the festival did not stop for three or four days. There were Elves who were not great ones for\nparties, but everyone was, for this, as people are on the last night of the year. Because the city was not as\nfull as it might have been, all the life of it poured into a few streets and a few quarters, which blazed\nwhile the rest of the city lay dark and quiet. It went on into the small hours and eased into the next day\nwithout stopping: some went home to sleep, and others were up at dawn gathering crabs on the beach for the\nmorning. Yssiongorn, the wine of the Havens, flowed very freely.</p>\n<p>Perry had been hibernating for this. He was awake for all of it. He took naps in the corners of taverns and\nled crawls from one to the next, and grew rowdier every night. Makheneb, who had always preferred the night\nand the dark, was not a reveller. He was the sort who, pulled into a crowd, slips quietly out of it. Perry\nkept bringing him drinks, and Makheneb kept pouring them out.</p>\n<p>Marigold's plan lasted until the middle of the second day, when it became clear they could not coordinate\nanything. She had bitten off more than she could chew. She took naps behind rocks and then went on to the next\nthing. The Elves' food came in very small portions and an enormous variety, far too many dishes ever to try\nthem all, and she tried to try them all, like someone at a fair who has resolved to visit every stall. Lily\ndarted about trying to see everything.</p>\n<h2>The ship</h2>\n<p>Before dawn on the third day, Lily was still up. She had stayed up all night, determined to see everything,\nand now she was helping clean up and still drinking a little, since if you help clear up you get free wine.\nThe light was just beginning in the east.</p>\n<p>Then she heard it. It was faint at first, a wailing. It went on, and it grew, and it began to come from more\nthan one direction. People came out of their doors, curious, and began to walk towards the shore, and as they\nwalked, they too broke out wailing. Everyone was heading for the water.</p>\n<p>The Company found one another near the shore. The horizon was golden and pink, and the light lay scattered on\nthe water. A ship was coming in. Its mast was broken, and a makeshift rig had been raised on half a mast. There\nwas only one ship. There should have been two: the returning ship, and the one that had gone out to meet it.\nFive or eight small boats were rowing out to it, and a larger ship was waiting further off, holding back because\nthe broken ship was coming in so slowly without its mast.</p>\n<p>Along the shore Elves were on their knees weeping, or holding one another, or talking in confused and frightened\nvoices.</p>\n<p>Lily looked at the ship, and then looked again, and understood. It was not the ship that had gone out to meet\nthe returning one. It was the westbound ship itself, the <em>Lómelindë</em>. Of the other ship, the <em>Gwaeloth</em>, there was\nno sign at all.</p>\n<h2>After</h2>\n<p>The <em>Lómelindë</em> came into harbour, and those aboard came ashore gaunt and broken. Only a third of them had come\nback.</p>\n<p>The festival was over. Lily and Marigold made the braided nutmeg bread, a great deal of it, and carried it to the\nhouses where the Elves off the ship had been taken in. There they learned the stark truth. The <em>Lómelindë</em> had\nnever met the <em>Gwaeloth</em> at all. And of those who had sailed in her, two in every three were gone.</p>\n<p>Perry went to find Mablin, and found him at the watchtower, staring out with hollow eyes, smoking. The death of an\nElf, Mablin told him, was like the death of a human child. So much future was stolen, even from one who had lived\nfor centuries. After that he said nothing, and Perry sat beside him and smoked with him in silence.</p>\n<p>Makheneb had already packed his things.</p>\n<p>That evening Edrain came to them. Círdan would see them in the morning.</p>\n<h2>Canthwë</h2>\n<p>Outside the Arantarion in the morning they met Canthwë again. He was puffing contentedly at his pipe and blowing\nlittle circles and triangles and squares of smoke.</p>\n<p>Marigold said good morning and asked how he was.</p>\n<p>He was doing wonderfully, he said.</p>\n<p>\"Aren't you sad?\"</p>\n<p>\"Sad? About what?\"</p>\n<p>\"About the ships,\" said Marigold. \"The Elves that didn't come back.\"</p>\n<p>He seemed to remember. \"Oh, yes. That's awful. That's terrible.\" They pressed him, and all at once, with startling\nclearness, he began to name them: every Elf who had sailed, on both ships, and whether each had come back or not.\nFor the <em>Lómelindë</em>, one in every three had come back. For the <em>Gwaeloth</em>, none.</p>\n<p>A moment later the clearness had gone, as suddenly as it came. He was smoking happily again, as though he had never\nsaid a word of it.</p>\n<p>The Company understood then what had been done to him. Whatever Círdan had done to Canthwë's mind worked like a lid\nthat shut itself again the moment anything rose beneath it.</p>\n<h2>Círdan's charge</h2>\n<p>They climbed the tower and came before Círdan.</p>\n<p>He began with Makheneb. He apologised for the careless jesting about battles long past, and for the weight of memory\nand loss it had laid on him. He spoke a little of the death of Elves, who by nature do not die. Then he turned to the\nothers and asked each of them how long they thought they had left to live.</p>\n<p>Perry guessed forty years. Lily said fifty. Marigold said she did not know.</p>\n<p>Against lives so short, he laid his charge on them.</p>\n<p>They had seen the banes and touched them, and they understood something of them. The banes ought, in the end, to be\ndestroyed. The Company did not have the power to destroy them. But it could make sure that they were safe: safe for\nthis generation and for every generation after it. That was their task. They must go to each place and make sure\nthere was a way of doing things that would last across the generations. It did not matter what it was, a law, or a\nrite, or a religion, or a story, so long as every generation knew not to touch the hot stove. What mattered was that\nit was never broken.</p>\n<p>Nenuviel, his aide, was going north to the Cavity Stone in the Blue Mountains, with the Dwarves. The Company would go\nwith her. And he would go on sending ships into the West, twice a year, until the Straight Road opened again, or until\nLindon had spent itself.</p>\n<p>They asked more. Tom's list, it turned out, was too small. There were not only four of these things. Four were on Tom's\nmap; there was the place in the east that had taken Canthwë; and there was at least one other that nobody knew anything\nabout at all.</p>\n<p>Canthwë, Círdan told them, had soaked up the curse of that place in the east the way a rag soaks up wine. That frightened\nthem. They were being sent round to clean up places like that. Were they at risk of becoming like him? Would he have to\nput them down?</p>\n<p>Círdan was taken aback. Then he realised he had been speaking too simply, and needed to be simpler still. \"Have you met\nGandalf?\" he asked.</p>\n<p>\"The fireworks man?\"</p>\n<p>So he told them. He told them of the Valar, and of the Maiar, great and powerful beings, and how a few of them had come\ninto Middle-earth to help in different ways, and some had taken on the likeness of Men. Gandalf was one of them, powerful\nbeyond anything they knew and hiding it very modestly. Such beings could command the weather, and call fire, and move\nstone, and open what was shut, each in their own way. They were called the Istari, the Wizards. Canthwë had once gone by\nanother name, which Círdan had sealed. He had been very powerful, and he had been working a great power in that place,\nand the curse had drawn on that power. That was the tragedy of it: the greatness of his power was what made him take the\ncurse into himself. \"But this is not a working you are at any risk of performing,\" said Círdan, \"and I do not believe the\nother banes you meet pose that same danger. You do not stand where he stood, and you cannot do what brought it on him.\"</p>\n<p>Perry did not believe a word of it. There were things he knew about the world, and fireworks-makers turning out to be\nwizards was not one of them, and Canthwë was plainly just a man who smoked too much. Lily thought some version of it might\nbe true. Marigold listened gravely.</p>\n<p>Then Círdan spoke of his own fear. He feared that the Beast of Belegaer had woken in the sea. He gave them a scrap of script\nwith its name written in the Black Speech. Long ago, he said, a Silmaril had been lost in the deep, and he feared it might\nhave roused the creature. He did not know what kind of tradition could ever settle a thing that lay in the sea. But if the\njewel could be taken from it, perhaps the beast could be settled again.</p>\n<p>Lily asked him not to send another ship yet. \"Wait until spring,\" she said. They would go north into the mountains now, and\ncome back down in the spring, and go with it then. Círdan agreed.</p>\n<p>Before they left, Lily found the chance to ask Laeroneth, and another of the Dúnedain, Meneldil, about Makheneb's great bow,\nthe Great Bow of Angnir. They suggested that she look for the tellers of old tales in certain towns, who carried such stories\ndown.</p>\n<h2>Keeping the fire</h2>\n<p>They talked it over among themselves afterwards.</p>\n<p>The long-folk, at least, had a tradition already; they kept the fanged woman asleep, and they were not well known, so nobody\nwas seeking them out. The first bane had a tradition too, and a well-kept one, though the Company would tell the Rangers it\nneeded doing a good deal more often than every forty years. The bigger question there was who had been trying to break into it.\nOne of those men had come through Bree, and they had come from the south.</p>\n<p>Círdan had told them what he could. He did not himself know what these things were, apart from what he had told them of Canthwë\nand of the Beast of Belegaer; what he knew of the Blue Mountains was what they had just told him. What he advised was this: that\nthe peoples bound to each place, and to its tradition, should find allies and stay in touch with one another, so that they\ncould gather together if any one of them faltered, or if something broke loose. He suspected that the answer at each place\nwould be different. Anything the Company could work out, and set down as a plan, even one they could not carry out themselves,\nwould help. What they wanted in the end was something that would still be there generations from now, so that if an emergency\ncame there would be a known way to call for help.</p>\n<p>Perry had an idea. They could cut great stacks of wood and set them up between all the places, and if a place needed help, it\nwould light its fire. There would have to be strict punishments for lighting one for no reason, and riders with buckets of\nwater for when it was an accident. The thought of learning each place's ways well enough to build a tradition that made sense\nthere, and that would work within it, excited Perry more than anything else had.</p>\n<p>Somebody asked whether the Cavity Stone was the bane of the Blue Mountains. Círdan had been careful not to say, but he had\nseemed to think so. They would not have to go to the Dwarves, solve one problem, and then go fifty miles on and solve another.\nProbably.</p>\n<h2>The ride north</h2>\n<p>Círdan knew he was asking a great deal of them, and he equipped them well. Each of them could have their pick of a horse fit for\nthe mountains. Leaper was retired, and left in good hands. Perry took Levlay, Buddy's friend. Lily kept Buddy, who was sleek and\nwell fed now and no longer anything like half-starved. Strawberry went home: one day Tom simply turned up at the stables,\npetting her, and said he was taking Strawberry back to the Old Forest. They were given better gear, too, Elvish work that did the\nsame job at a third of the weight. And Makheneb had his bow back, with its Shadow lifted from it.</p>\n<p>Perry laid out their route, straight and direct, since there was no point adding the weight of the mountains to their journey\nbefore they had to. It was the lovely end of summer, and the weather was glorious. They rode north and made excellent time.</p>\n<p>Ahead lay the valley of the Dwarves. But as the valley opened before them, a shadow of despair began to gather.</p>", "played": "2025-08-26", "slug": "08-the-returned-ship-and-the-ride-north", "title": "The Returned Ship, and the Ride North"}, {"date": "End of Summer, 2965", "html": "<h2>Home</h2>\n<p>As they rode, Már told them what to expect. Dwarf-homes were tunnels, she said, and little rooms and\ncoves cut into the rock, and her family would take them in. Makheneb might get some looks; an Elf was\nnot a common guest under the mountains. And she reminded them, once more, never to speak Fenja's\nother name.</p>\n<p>Of her own people she spoke gladly. Her mother and father were there, and her sister, who was\nmarried. Her sister and her husband grew things in the deep places of the mountain, food raised\nwithout the sun, and her sister kept a bakery that made the most of it. They had twin boys, Már's\nnephews. One of them wanted to follow his parents into the growing. The other, Nolly, had begun to\nlearn Már's own trade, the laying of ghosts, and his mother was not at all sure she liked it. His\ngrandparents were proud of him. There was a friend, too, Adis, who had hunted ghosts beside Már:\ntimid, but clever, the sort who would rather think a thing through than run at it.</p>\n<p>Nenuviel knew nothing of mountains. She was going to judge the matter for Círdan, and she would lean\non the Company for the rest. Thramli's party, the Dwarves who had come to the Havens, had been five;\nthey had ridden home ahead of the Company. The broken Númenórean sword from the ruins had come\nback from the smiths at the Havens reforged for Perry into a long-hafted axe, with a keen edge on it.</p>\n<h2>The valley</h2>\n<p>They came down into the valley in the middle of a sunny afternoon, half a day from the nearest\nsettlement, and there they found Thramli's party.</p>\n<p>All five were dead. Their bodies were scattered along the way, and they had not been killed by\nbeasts. It was the work of weapons. They had been dead less than two days; the scavengers were only\nbeginning.</p>\n<p>Each of them took it differently. Makheneb went cold and still, and looked on as though from far\naway. Perry went into the bushes and was sick. He had never seen anything like it. \"This could have\nbeen the road to Bree,\" he said, and could not stop saying things like it. Már's fear went straight\nto her home. She thought it might be a punishment, for having promised help.</p>\n<p>Dwarves care greatly for their dead, and Már knew what to look for. Rings, necklaces, sword hilts\nand shields were heirlooms, and would tell which families these had been. One shield was very fine\nand no heirloom at all; she marked it, for later. They loaded three of the bodies onto the horses and\nwent on foot beside them.</p>\n<h2>The charge</h2>\n<p>Makheneb saw them first. Dwarves were coming at them, running, with hammers and axes raised. It was\nno Dwarvish way of fighting. Dwarves were known for their shield walls, for close order and patient\ndefence; these came on wild, like beasts, and their eyes were rolled back white in their heads.</p>\n<p>Among them Már knew her nephew. He was yelling, and she did not think he saw her.</p>\n<p>\"Már,\" said Makheneb, \"should we shoot them?\"</p>\n<p>Már had no answer for that. She went forward instead and shouted to Nolly in Dwarvish, calling him by\nhis name, with the badger at her throat. He came to a sudden stop and stared at her, slack-jawed,\nwithout understanding. Then slowly, as though she were coming into focus, he began to see her.</p>\n<p>The others did not stop. Makheneb shot to stop them, not to kill. His first arrow struck one, and\nslowed him, but the Dwarf did not act as a man acts who has been shot. The second went through\nanother's leg and pinned it to the ground, and the Dwarf pitched forward onto his own hammer. Perry,\nwho knew he was no archer, stood in front of Makheneb, and when a hammer came down he caught it on\nthe flat of his long-hafted axe. Marigold slipped away over the hill to hide. Lily held\nthe line with her sword.</p>\n<p>Nenuviel fought from horseback with her spear, and she killed one of them, and did it gracefully.\nThen a hammer crushed her leg and she fell from the saddle. The Dwarf she had speared through came\nwalking up the shaft, and wrenched the spear out of her hands.</p>\n<p>A hammer took Már in the chest and broke her collarbone. She turned to a woman of her own people, one\nwith the white eyes, and tried to speak to her; no word of it went in. Afterwards she wished she had\nnot tried to reason with them at all.</p>\n<p>Perry went in. He saw, on one of the Dwarves, a great axe-wound in the chest, fresh and open and not\nhealed at all, and a moment later he took an axe-blow of his own in the same place. He shrieked. When\nit was over he had fallen down, spent, more hurt than he had ever been in his life.</p>\n<p>Nolly let fall his weapon. \"Már,\" he said. He knew her. He threw his arms around her and said he was\nsorry, and sorry, and sorry again.</p>\n<p>Marigold had come back, and an axe caught her, and she was bleeding. Már shook herself free of Nolly\nand charged. Perry swung at the last of them still fighting, and missed; then all of\nthem together, Nolly too, wrestled him to the ground and bound him. His name was Veig. He was awake,\nbut he was not in his right mind.</p>\n<p>There had been seven who attacked them. Three were dead. Two lay senseless, with Makheneb's arrows\nin them, and Veig was bound. Nolly was himself again.</p>\n<p>Nolly remembered nothing but being at home in the mountains. He did not understand why he was\noutside, or why he had been attacking them, and he knew every one of the Dwarves who had just been\nhurt and killed. He was distraught, and did whatever he was told.</p>\n<h2>The last miles</h2>\n<p>They put Nenuviel on her own horse, with the bound Dwarf across it. The other five horses carried two\nDwarves each, the dead and the senseless, and everyone else walked. They would not come in until after nightfall.</p>\n<p>Már urged them all to be ready to explain themselves, and she was counting on Nolly to bear witness.\nBefore they reached the settlement, Makheneb went among the bodies and drew out every arrow that was\nplainly Elvish. Már walked beside the dead, setting them straight and making them presentable, over\nand over. The last thing she wanted was for her people to take offence at some small thing. It all\nlooked well enough. She was nervous all the same.</p>\n<p>Marigold and Perry could barely walk. They trailed behind with Nolly, trying to keep up with the\nhorses, and they went slowly, being friends to one another.</p>\n<h2>The weigh station</h2>\n<p>It was a couple of hours after dark when they came to the first weigh station, and they were hailed.</p>\n<p>Már gave her name, and her father's. \"I have Nolly here with me,\" she said, \"and we are trying to\ntravel home.\" They asked who all the rest were, and what their business was. \"My companions,\" she\nsaid, and named them: Marigold, Makheneb, Perry, Lily, and Nenuviel. They had been at the Grey\nHavens, and spoken with Círdan, and they were making their way to the mountains. And then, because\nthere was no hiding it: they carried more than companions on their horses. They had been witness to a\ntragedy, and been drawn into the fighting, and they had dead with them, and wounded, and they meant\nto bring these Dwarves home to their families.</p>\n<p>As the guards understood whom the Company was bringing, one of them broke into a wail. He stood tall\nat his post while he did it, but the sound was piercing, bottomless, soul-crushing. The other took it\nup too, and began to ring a bell in a set pattern.</p>\n<p>Others came running down, and when they saw, they wailed as well. They took the reins of the horses.\nThey came alongside Perry and Marigold and Már and gave them an arm to lean on. Seven or eight of\nthem escorted the Company up into the mountain proper, and all the way they heard bells echoing\nfurther up, and more and more people gathered along the way. Some were already looking at the dead,\nand knowing them. It became a procession of grief, winding up the mountain and into the Hall.</p>\n<h2>Elin</h2>\n<p>There one of the lords of the Dwarves met them. There was a council, and Elin sat on it. She was a\ndistant relation of Már's, the kind somebody had once mapped out for her, near enough that she knew\nwho Elin was and that they were kin. There was always one of the council awake, whatever the hour,\nand tonight it was she. She came and welcomed them, and embraced Már.</p>\n<p>\"It is so good to see you,\" she said. \"But what a moment of tragedy. Come. Come in.\" And then: \"Are\nthere others?\"</p>\n<p>There were not. They had brought everyone.</p>\n<p>Elin gathered people about her and began to give orders. The wounded went one way, for tending. The\ndead went another, to be made ready. Then she asked what was to be done with the three who lived but\nwere bound.</p>\n<p>Nobody had yet asked how they came to be dead, and Már did not tell it now. She spoke only of the\nthree. \"We are concerned for their well-being,\" she said. When they had been awake their eyes had\nbeen rolled back in their heads, and even Nolly had not known anyone for some minutes. There was no\ntelling how the others would be when they woke. \"They may not know you,\" she said. She had been\nlucky that Nolly did. Elin promised that they would never be left alone, and that they would be\nrelieved of their arms. Their weapons, she meant.</p>\n<p>The best of the Dwarves' healers went to Nenuviel, who looked the worst, and they did much for her.\nMarigold and Már were tended as well. Cots were put up and guest rooms made ready, with the promise\nthat there would be talk. \"We will mourn the dead once they are prepared,\" they were told. \"You\nshould rest. And then let us talk.\" Nothing seemed to be happening out in the valley that needed\nanyone to go back that night. The talk would wait for morning.</p>\n<h2>The first night</h2>\n<p>Már woke in a cold sweat from a dream she could not remember. It was still dark. The room was\nsomething like a quiet house of healing, with a few others awake somewhere near, and she did not\nwant to disturb them. There were halls enough, and empty at that hour, and she paced them.</p>\n<p>The Dwarves had been hospitable. But did they know? What was going through their minds? She turned\nall of it over. There was something so deeply wrong in every part of it. It took her near an hour to\ncome to a place where she could bear it: that what had happened was not right, but she did not think\nthey had had a choice. There would be a way forward in the morning, she thought. With Elin, and with\nNenuviel, and with others, they might find an answer; perhaps Nolly would remember something. What had\nhappened had happened, and it was a tragedy. They had the means, at least, to find out what it was,\nand to protect Nolly and the other Dwarves from it after. Let it never happen again. With that\nresolve she went back to sleep.</p>\n<p>Around Makheneb there were yards of stone, hundreds of them, maybe thousands, in every direction. He\nwas used to sleeping in caves, though not so deep as this. The rooms were centuries old, and nobody\nworked the rock anywhere near the healing-rooms in the middle of the night. He found the cool\ndampness of it comforting, and liked the quiet. Here he would hear anyone coming.</p>\n<p>Marigold woke very early, and kept herself occupied until others were awake by drawing. She drew the\nmoment the Dwarf with the axe came at her, over and over, the same scene again and again.</p>\n<h2>Morning</h2>\n<p>At about the sixth hour Már was woken by her sister, and found herself wrapped in a hug. When she had\npushed back enough not to smother, her sister stood away from her and held her face in her hands, and\nkept holding it.</p>\n<p>\"Oh, you're all right,\" she said. \"You're all right.\" She was crying, relieved and overjoyed. \"You\nbrought my Nolly back. I'm so thankful. He's been missing for weeks.\"</p>\n<p>That shook Már. She was grateful, and glad, and said so. She was sorry, she said; she had not known\nhe had been gone so long. When had he disappeared? How had it happened? Had he been here, and then\nsimply gone, and the others with him?</p>\n<p>Her sister would come round to those questions in time. For now she would not leave the reunion. She\nwanted only to be with Már, and to rejoice that she was here, and that neither of them was dead,\nthough others were. When Már asked about Nolly, she turned the question aside, and came back each time\nto the same thing: he was all right, and he was home, and Már had brought him.</p>\n<p>Marigold was already awake, and heard it from a little way down the hall. She let them have their\nspace.</p>\n<p>Soon after, food was brought to all of them. Nenuviel came round to see how everyone was. She was\nlimping, on a crutch with a makeshift extension, but looked well enough all things considered, and\nshe took particular care over Marigold, asking how the tending had gone for her, whether she felt any\nbetter, whether she needed anything. Marigold's cheeks were blotched from crying through the night.\nShe gave no real answer. Nenuviel stepped closer and held out an arm, not presuming, asking. Marigold\nlet herself be hugged, and Nenuviel drew her in and kissed the top of her head and held her close.\nMarigold felt her body loosen, a little.</p>\n<p>Dwarves came to the rooms. They were welcome to rest, they said; or, if they chose, they might attend\nthe remembrance of the dead, which was about to begin. They explained what to expect and how it was\ndone, and told them they were invited but in no way bound.</p>\n<p>Before they reached Marigold, a small Dwarf child burst in. It had plainly come in at the wrong door,\nlooking for somebody else. It stopped and stared at her very hard.</p>\n<p>\"Are you a child?\" it asked. \"Where's your hair?\"</p>\n<p>Marigold did not understand. Many Dwarf-women wore thick sideburns, and she had none.</p>\n<p>\"It runs in the family,\" she said.</p>\n<p>The child nodded as if it understood entirely, and then went red, as though it had just remembered\nthat it had asked something rude, and fled to find the right room.</p>\n<h2>The remembrance</h2>\n<p>Már and her sister were told that the remembrance would soon begin, and asked whether Már had eaten.\nShe had not, and they fed her before the two of them went. Marigold went, and Nenuviel.</p>\n<p>Perry did not. In the ordinary way Perry was the most hospitable of them, always the one to go where\nhe was wanted. This time he shut himself in his room, wrapped in several Dwarf robes, ate as much as\nhe could, and was a long way from home.</p>\n<p>It was not like any funeral the Company had known. There were two parts to it. The first was a\nreciting of lineages: this was who they had been, and these were all those who had come before them.\nThe dead had been washed and laid out. Before each one were set precious things, things they had made,\nor owned, or that bore on who they had been, and many people had brought small keepsakes as well. Some\nof the dead wore beautiful clothes or jewels; some had their weapons or armour with them. That was the\nsecond part: the things that would remain after they had decayed, by which the dead would always be\nremembered. For some, people spoke of the halls and passages they had carved.</p>\n<p>Nolly was there with his father and mother, standing alongside Már. He was attentive and aware, and he\ndid not shed a tear. He looked to be in shock. They walked past all the dead in turn.</p>\n<p>Then that part ended, and they were led into another room, where there was food, and people talked\nquietly. The dead were being taken to their resting places. Their kin would receive some of the\nthings that had been set before them, and some would be buried with them.</p>\n<h2>The council</h2>\n<p>There Elin came to Már. There would be a formal sitting of the council, she said, to understand what\nhad happened, and to hear about Már and her guests. It would be after the midday meal and the rest\nthat followed it. Until then the hours were set apart, a sacred time. They might talk, or wander, or\nrest; and then they were all bidden to the council.</p>\n<p>Már knew something of the council, though she had never stood before it. There were seven, the\ngoverning body of the Dwarves of the Blue Mountains. It was not quite like being brought before a\nqueen, and not quite like a town meeting either, but somewhat closer to the town. There was protocol\nand procedure, and it was no small thing to be heard. With so little warning, she thought fewer than\nseven would sit. And the council liked to be of one mind. It seldom was, and so those who felt\nstrongly, but not strongly enough to overthrow the whole matter, would stand aside and not vote. Four\nin favour and three standing aside would carry it. Four in favour, two aside and one against would\nnot. It was a council much given to doing nothing.</p>\n<p>The fine shield was hers now. Nobody had claimed it; it belonged to no family.</p>\n<h2>The sacred hours</h2>\n<p>Perry came out of his room to find more food. In his robes, which were too small for him, he carried\nthe platter they had brought his breakfast on, and was piling it with Dwarf pastries and trying to\nedge away when he was caught in a conversation about how important the council was going to be. He\ndid not listen. He ate. When the platter was empty he went looking for a bakery, meaning to take\nwhatever he could off whatever windowsill without talking to anybody.</p>\n<p>It was Már's sister's bakery he robbed. Among her many specialities the greatest were her braided\nbeards: sweet plaited loaves, with jam and other fillings running through them in colours, like\nribbons woven through hair. There was one kind made with a nut that grew about those parts, roasted\nand ground to a paste with pieces left whole, so that it was creamy and crunchy at once. Perry took a\nselection. Within hours his room was a wreck, and he had built what nest he could out of the blankets\nand pillows. He was not in the least prepared for the council.</p>\n<p>As he ate, the weight of all he had seen grew less present. It did not go away. It became the kind of\nhurt you do not feel until something presses on it. It showed itself as a clarity of purpose, and a\nloss of innocence. Before, Perry had been the one who wanted everybody to have a good time, and to\nfeel that they belonged together. That was what he had been doing at the Havens. There had been bad\nthings in the world, of course: shakedowns by bandits on the road, and a shady man he knew who lied to\npeople and said things that were a little off. That had been as near to evil as he could imagine. Now\nhe had seen kin turn on kin and kill one another, and he knew it could have been him and his own\nfamily; that it had only happened to be somewhere else. He came out of it among the crumbs of those\nbeautiful pastries, ready, and hardened, and having lost that part of himself.</p>\n<p>Makheneb went to find a library. There were two near at hand, and he chose the larger. It was\nmeticulous records, for the most part. He had not known stone could have so many qualities. Here were\nsamples taken on such a day from such a place, what stone was in them, how much had been quarried, and\nin what direction, which precious metals had been drawn out, which gems. There were so many words he\nhad never heard that he understood about one in four. He knew what a diamond was; the rest was beyond\nhim. What was not about stone was about the running of a people inside a mountain. He had found the\nquartermasters' records, the stewards' accounts, the reckonings of several hundred years of Dwarves.</p>\n<p>Marigold wandered and looked at everything, as one does in a strange town where everything is new and\nnothing quite makes sense. It was a very different people from the one she had grown up among, but\nthere were things they shared, and she found them in the food, and in the way the Dwarves moved about\ntogether. She had eaten at several tables now. At the Havens she had got into the kitchens before the\nfestival and traded recipes. Here too she was able to ask a few simple questions about how their\npastry was made, and she came away with a fair notion of how she might learn to make it herself,\nwhen she was not grieving, and not getting ready for a council.</p>\n<h2>What to say</h2>\n<p>Then the Company sat down together to decide what they would put before the council, and who would\nspeak for them.</p>\n<p>Their errand had not changed. They had come to bring Nenuviel to look at the Cavity Stone and find out\nwhat ailed it, on Thramli's behalf. That was still the first thing. Now they had much else to account\nfor as well, and there would be questions; and for all they knew, it was all one matter.</p>\n<p>Should they speak of making a new tradition, when they did not yet know what was wrong? Someone\nthought it might comfort the council to hear that the Company meant to give them something they could\nact on, something to keep the thing in check, so long as they were honest that they did not know yet\nwhat it would be. Nenuviel would be going back to the Havens once the task was done; she was there to\nhelp with the lore. Perhaps the answer would be simply to seal the place, and to write into their\nhistories that it was sealed for good reason and must never, ever be unsealed.</p>\n<p>What they needed was knowledge of how the thing worked on people. Who had been touched by it, and how\nnear had they been? Was there some threshold? Had they been in the room with it, or touched it, or\nnever come near it at all? How big was it? Could it be taken away, and dropped in the sea? Nobody knew.\nNolly, if it was the same thing that had taken him, had not been near it; the last he remembered was\nsimply being in the mountains. Could people be brought back? Nolly had been, and it had been kin that\ndid it. Perhaps it took those who loved them most, calling them home. They would need to watch Nolly,\nsomeone said, for they had not yet seen him wholly recovered.</p>\n<p>First, though, they had to convince the council that the thing was a threat, unless the council\nalready believed it. They could not afford to divide anyone. \"It is dangerous; please, let us help\nyou help yourselves\" was hard to say no to. The danger was that the Dwarves would not want help from\noutside, and that was why it was good Már should lead: she was not from outside. So Már would speak\nfor them.</p>\n<p>And there was the dead party's own errand. Thramli's company had gone to the Havens seeking this very\nhelp. If the Company were only finishing what they had set out to do, then to refuse the Company was\nto say those Dwarves had died for nothing. That was how they might honour the dead.</p>\n<p>They might bring witnesses, like Nolly. Somebody suggested bringing one of those still afflicted\nbefore the council, well bound, so that they could see for themselves. Már agreed, so long as he was\nproperly restrained.</p>\n<p>But they did not yet know for certain that the Cavity Stone was the cause. There were a few hours\nbefore the council. Each of them might do one thing in that time: go to see the ones who had been\nbound, and how they were now; or find out from Nolly whether he was in any state to bear witness; or\nlearn who had sent Thramli in the first place, and what the story of that had been.</p>\n<p>Then they would go before the council of the Dwarves of the Blue Mountains.</p>", "played": "2025-09-09", "slug": "09-the-blue-mountains", "title": "The Blue Mountains"}], "company": [], "home": {"html": "<h1>Banes of Beleriand</h1>"}, "people": [], "timeline": {"html": "<h1>Timeline</h1>"}};
